/*
 * Pearson Edexcel A-Level Geography (9GE0) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Every item is written as one part of a real 9GE0 question: a
 * Figure described in full or a named, located case study comes first, then the
 * demand, because real papers are resource- and case-study-led. Skills,
 * calculations and fieldwork-style items appear throughout.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Only the tariffs Pearson's assessment guide lists for Papers 1 and 2:
 * 3 Suggest, 4 skills/Explain, 6 and 8 Explain, 12 Assess, 20 Evaluate (the
 * invented 16-mark items are gone).
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. Point-marked short items; levels items give indicative content
 * and Edexcel's level ladders with the AO split (12-mark AO1 3 + AO2 9; 20-mark
 * AO1 5 + AO2 15).
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const GEOGRAPHY_EDEXCEL_PRACTICE = {
  'T1': {
    name: 'Tectonic Processes & Hazards',
    questions: [
      { q: `Study Figure 1. This is part of an investigation into the deaths caused by selected earthquakes, 1995–2015 (approximate published figures, rounded; data adapted for this practice question).\n\nFigure 1: deaths caused by selected earthquakes\nKobe, Japan (1995) 6,430\nKashmir, Pakistan (2005) 86,000\nL'Aquila, Italy (2009) 309\nChile (2010) 525\nChristchurch, New Zealand (2011) 185\nNepal (2015) 8,960\n\nCalculate the range in the number of deaths caused by these earthquakes. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: 86,000 − 185 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 85,815 deaths (1)\nAward 2 marks for the correct answer with no working shown.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 2 (data written for this practice question). Figure 2 shows the number of geophysical disasters (earthquakes, volcanic eruptions and tsunamis) recorded in a global disaster database in each decade.\n\nFigure 2: recorded geophysical disasters per decade\n1960s 98\n1970s 121\n1980s 187\n1990s 264\n2000s 301\n2010s 312\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in recorded geophysical disasters between the 1960s and the 2010s. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (312 − 98) ÷ 98 × 100, or 214 ÷ 98 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer to one decimal place: 218.4% (1)` },
      { q: `The 2011 Tōhoku earthquake in Japan had a moment magnitude (Mw) of 9.0. The 2010 Haiti earthquake had a moment magnitude of 7.0. On the moment magnitude scale, each increase of one whole unit releases approximately 32 times more energy.\n\nCalculate approximately how many times more energy was released by the Tōhoku earthquake than by the Haiti earthquake. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: difference of 2 units, so 32 × 32 (or 32²) (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 1,024 times (accept approximately 1,000 times) (1)\nDo not credit 64 (32 × 2).` },
      { q: `A seismometer is located 300 km from the focus of an earthquake. Assume that P waves travel through the crust at 6.0 km/s and S waves at 3.5 km/s (values written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the time difference, in seconds, between the arrival of the first P waves and the first S waves at the seismometer. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (300 ÷ 3.5) − (300 ÷ 6.0) = 85.7 − 50 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 35.7 seconds (1)` },
      { q: `Study Figure 3. Figure 3 is a photograph taken in Port-au-Prince, Haiti, after the January 2010 earthquake (Mw 7.0). It shows a street of collapsed two- and three-storey concrete buildings. Several upper floors have fallen flat onto the floors below ('pancaked'), and thin steel reinforcing rods can be seen sticking out of the broken concrete columns. People are searching the rubble by hand.\n\nSuggest one reason why buildings such as those in Figure 3 caused a high number of deaths.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 2 further marks for expansion.\nPossible reasons:\n• Poor-quality construction (1): thin reinforcing bars and weak concrete mixes meant columns failed under shaking (1), so heavy floors pancaked with little void space for survivors (1).\n• Lack of enforced building codes (1): weak governance meant regulations were not applied (1), so many self-built or informal buildings were not designed to withstand ground shaking (1).\n• Heavy concrete roofs/floors (1): once supports failed the weight crushed occupants (1), and rescue by hand was slow, so trapped people died before being reached (1).\nCredit other valid reasons. Maximum 3 marks.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 4. Figure 4 is a photograph of the lower slopes of Mount Merapi, Java, Indonesia, a highly active composite volcano. It shows terraced fields of rice and vegetables on dark volcanic soils, a small village of houses about 8 km from the summit, and a large quarry where sand and gravel from old lahar deposits is being dug out and loaded onto trucks.\n\nSuggest one reason why people continue to live close to volcanoes such as Mount Merapi.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 2 further marks for expansion.\nPossible reasons:\n• Fertile soils (1): weathered volcanic ash and lava are rich in minerals (1), supporting high crop yields and several harvests a year, which is a vital livelihood in a densely populated rural area (1).\n• Income from extracting materials (1): sand and gravel from lahar deposits are sold for construction (1), giving employment where other jobs are scarce (1).\n• Low perception of risk / attachment to place (1): eruptions may be infrequent within one lifetime or people have strong cultural and spiritual ties to the land (1), so the perceived benefits outweigh the perceived risk (1).\n• Lack of alternatives (1): poverty means families cannot afford land elsewhere (1).\nMaximum 3 marks.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 5 (data written for this practice question). Figure 5 is a line graph showing the number of deaths from tectonic hazards each year, 1990–2020. The line is mostly low (fewer than 10,000 deaths a year), but it has very large, isolated peaks in 2004 (about 230,000), 2008 (about 88,000) and 2010 (about 230,000).\n\nSuggest one reason why the trend in deaths from tectonic hazards is difficult to identify from Figure 5.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 2 further marks for expansion.\nPossible reasons:\n• The data is dominated by a few megadisasters / low-frequency high-magnitude events (1), such as the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami (1), so single years distort any trend line or mean (1).\n• Death tolls are uncertain or disputed (1), for example estimates for Haiti 2010 vary widely (1), so peaks may be over- or under-stated (1).\n• Tectonic events are irregular and not linked to a cycle (1), so a 30-year period is too short to show a clear long-term pattern (1).\nMaximum 3 marks.` },
      { q: `On 26 December 2004 a megathrust earthquake of about Mw 9.1 occurred off the west coast of northern Sumatra, Indonesia. The tsunami it generated killed around 230,000 people in 14 countries, more than 160,000 of them in the province of Aceh, Indonesia.\n\nExplain one reason why the number of deaths in Aceh was so high.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation.\nPossible reasons:\n• Proximity to the epicentre (1): Aceh was only a few hundred kilometres from the rupture, so the tsunami arrived within about 15–30 minutes (1); there was too little time to evacuate even if a warning had been given (1); waves of up to 30 m surged several kilometres inland over the low coastal plain (1).\n• No tsunami warning system in the Indian Ocean in 2004 (1): there were no ocean buoys or national warning networks (1), and no siren or mobile alerts to communities (1), so people were on the beach or in low-lying homes when the waves arrived (1).\n• Low awareness/education (1): tsunamis were rare in living memory (1), so people did not recognise natural warning signs such as the sea retreating (1) and some went onto the exposed seabed (1).\n• Dense coastal settlement (1): Banda Aceh and fishing villages lay on low, flat land with few high buildings (1), and the removal of mangroves reduced natural protection (1).\nMaximum 4 marks.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 6 (data written for this practice question). A student investigated whether there is a significant relationship between the quality of governance and deaths from earthquakes for ten countries. Rank 1 = highest value.\n\nFigure 6\nCountry | Governance index (0–100) | Rank | Deaths per 100,000 people exposed | Rank\nA | 82 | 1 | 2.1 | 9\nB | 75 | 2 | 0.9 | 10\nC | 68 | 3 | 4.8 | 7\nD | 61 | 4 | 3.0 | 8\nE | 55 | 5 | 7.5 | 6\nF | 47 | 6 | 12.4 | 4\nG | 40 | 7 | 9.6 | 5\nH | 33 | 8 | 21.3 | 3\nI | 26 | 9 | 35.8 | 1\nJ | 18 | 10 | 28.1 | 2\n\nThe formula for Spearman's rank correlation coefficient is rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n).\n\nCalculate the value of rs. Give your answer to three decimal places. You must show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct values of d (difference in ranks): −8, −8, −4, −4, −1, 2, 2, 5, 8, 8 (1)\n1 mark for correct d² values and Σd² = 64 + 64 + 16 + 16 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 25 + 64 + 64 = 322 (1)\n1 mark for correct substitution: rs = 1 − (6 × 322) ÷ (1000 − 10) = 1 − 1932 ÷ 990 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: rs = −0.952 (1)\nAllow follow-through from an incorrect Σd². The negative sign is required for the final mark.` },
      { q: `The Hawaiian Islands lie in the middle of the Pacific Plate, far from any plate boundary. Approximate ages of the oldest volcanic rocks are: Kauai 5.1 million years, Oahu 3.0 million years, Maui 1.3 million years and Hawaii (Big Island) less than 0.7 million years. Kīlauea, on the south-east of Hawaii, is currently active. The Pacific Plate moves north-west at about 7–10 cm per year.\n\nDraw an annotated cross-section to show how a chain of volcanic islands of different ages forms over a hotspot.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Award up to 4 marks for an accurate, annotated cross-section:\n• Mantle plume shown rising from deep in the mantle beneath a stationary hotspot, with magma rising through the lithosphere (1)\n• Plate shown moving over the stationary hotspot, with the direction of plate movement marked (north-west for the Pacific Plate) (1)\n• Active volcano shown directly above the plume, with older, extinct volcanoes carried away from the hotspot (1)\n• Annotation showing increasing age and erosion/subsidence of islands with distance from the hotspot (for example Hawaii → Maui → Oahu → Kauai; older islands lower and eroded, eventually seamounts) (1)\nMaximum 1 mark for an unannotated diagram.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 7. Figure 7 is an extract from a global disaster database. It states that an event is only recorded as a disaster if it meets at least one of these criteria: 10 or more people reported killed; 100 or more people reported affected; a declaration of a state of emergency; or a call for international assistance. Figure 7 also shows that the number of recorded tectonic disasters rose sharply between 1960 and 2000.\n\nExplain why the data in Figure 7 may not be a reliable guide to trends in tectonic disasters.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for each valid point, with up to 1 further mark for expansion of each point, up to a maximum of 4 marks.\nPossible points:\n• Improved reporting and communications since the 1960s (1): satellites, mobile phones and global media mean more remote events are now recorded, so the rise may partly reflect better recording rather than more events (1).\n• Thresholds are low and fixed (1): an event killing 10 people counts the same as one killing 200,000, so counts of disasters say little about their scale (1).\n• Population growth and urbanisation (1): more people now live in exposed areas, so the same physical events are more likely to pass the 'affected' threshold (1).\n• Death and 'affected' figures are often estimates (1): they may be inflated or understated for political reasons, or lost where governance is weak (1).\n• Thresholds depend on decisions by governments (1): declaring an emergency or requesting aid varies with politics, not only with hazard impact (1).` },
      { q: `Park's model (the disaster response curve) shows how quality of life changes over time before, during and after a hazard event. It shows a pre-disaster normal, a sharp fall during the event, then phases of relief, rehabilitation and reconstruction.\n\nExplain one way in which the shape of Park's model may differ for an earthquake in a developing country compared with one in a developed country.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a difference and up to 3 further marks for explanation.\nPossible answers:\n• The fall in quality of life is deeper in a developing country (1): weaker buildings and lower preparedness cause more deaths and destruction (1), for example Haiti 2010 compared with Christchurch 2011 (1), so the curve drops much further below the pre-disaster normal (1).\n• Recovery is slower and longer in a developing country (1): relief depends on international aid which takes time to arrive (1), and lack of insurance and government funds slows reconstruction (1), so the rehabilitation and reconstruction phases are stretched over many years and quality of life may not return to the pre-disaster level (1).\n• In a developed country the curve may recover to above the previous level (1): reconstruction includes improved building codes and better infrastructure (1), so mitigation and preparedness improve future resilience (1).\nMaximum 4 marks.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 8. Figure 8 is a world map showing the epicentres of earthquakes of magnitude 5 and above over a ten-year period. Epicentres form narrow, continuous bands along the mid-Atlantic and East Pacific ridges; wider bands around the edges of the Pacific Ocean (for example Japan, Indonesia, Chile and the west coast of North America); a broad zone from the Mediterranean through Iran to the Himalayas; and a few scattered points in the middle of plates, such as Hawaii.\n\nExplain the global distribution of earthquakes shown in Figure 8.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Most earthquakes occur at plate boundaries, where stress builds as plates move and is released suddenly as friction is overcome.\n• Narrow bands of shallow-focus earthquakes at divergent boundaries (ridges) where plates move apart and the crust fractures; transform faults offset ridges.\n• Wider bands at convergent (destructive) boundaries around the Pacific Ring of Fire: subduction of dense oceanic lithosphere creates earthquakes at shallow, intermediate and deep focus along the Benioff zone, so the band is wider.\n• Broad zones at collision boundaries (Himalayas, Iran) where thick continental crust buckles and faults over a wide area.\n• Conservative boundaries, such as the San Andreas Fault, produce shallow earthquakes as plates slide past each other.\n• Intraplate earthquakes (for example near hotspots such as Hawaii, or reactivated ancient faults) explain the scattered points.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated or generic knowledge of tectonic processes; limited use of the figure.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge applied to the pattern shown; links between boundary type and distribution partly made.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; the pattern is fully explained with clear links between boundary types, focus depth and the width of earthquake zones shown in Figure 8.` },
      { q: `Mount St Helens, USA, sits above a destructive plate boundary where the Juan de Fuca Plate subducts beneath the North American Plate; its 1980 eruption was highly explosive (VEI 5). Eruptions in Iceland at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge are usually effusive, with fissures producing long flows of basaltic lava.\n\nExplain why volcanic eruptions at destructive plate boundaries are usually more explosive than those at constructive plate boundaries.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 6):\n• At constructive boundaries, decompression melting of the upper mantle produces basaltic magma, which is low in silica, hot and runny (low viscosity).\n• Gases escape easily from low-viscosity magma, so eruptions are effusive with lava flows and fire fountains.\n• At destructive boundaries, the subducting oceanic plate releases water into the overlying mantle wedge, lowering its melting point and producing magma that becomes andesitic or rhyolitic as it rises through and melts continental crust.\n• This magma is high in silica, cooler and highly viscous, so it traps gases (water vapour, CO₂).\n• Pressure builds until the vent is blocked or the dome fails, causing explosive eruptions with pyroclastic flows and ash columns, as at Mount St Helens (lateral blast).\n• Explosivity is measured by the logarithmic VEI; destructive boundary volcanoes commonly reach VEI 4–6.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; some relevant points about magma types.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Accurate knowledge of magma differences; partly explains the link between silica, viscosity, gas and explosivity.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate and detailed explanation of the processes at both boundaries, with clear causal links from magma formation to eruption style.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 9. Figure 9 is a cross-section of the Japan Trench before and after the 2011 Tōhoku earthquake. Before: the Pacific Plate is subducting beneath the overriding plate and the leading edge of the overriding plate is locked and bent downwards. After: the locked section has ruptured, the edge of the overriding plate has sprung upwards by several metres, and a large dome of water is shown above it, spreading out as waves towards the Japanese coast.\n\nExplain how earthquakes such as the one shown in Figure 9 can generate tsunamis.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• At subduction zones friction locks the plates; strain builds as the overriding plate is dragged down and deformed.\n• When stress exceeds friction, the megathrust fault ruptures and the overriding plate rebounds upwards (elastic rebound) — Figure 9 shows uplift of several metres.\n• This vertical displacement of the seabed lifts the entire water column above it, creating a dome of water.\n• The water flows outwards under gravity as a series of waves with very long wavelength and low amplitude in deep water, travelling at high speeds (several hundred km/h).\n• As waves enter shallow coastal water they slow, wavelength shortens and height increases greatly; the sea may first retreat (drawdown) if a trough arrives first.\n• Large shallow-focus events (Mw 9.0 at Tōhoku) produce the greatest displacement; strike-slip earthquakes rarely generate tsunamis because there is little vertical movement.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; little reference to Figure 9.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge linked to Figure 9; explanation of the sequence partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, accurate explanation of the full sequence from fault rupture to coastal wave, clearly interpreting Figure 9.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 10. Figure 10 shows Degg's model. It is a diagram of two overlapping circles: one labelled 'Natural hazard (e.g. earthquake, volcanic eruption)' and the other labelled 'Vulnerable population'. Only the area where the circles overlap is labelled 'Disaster'.\n\nExplain why a tectonic hazard does not always result in a disaster.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Figure 10 shows a disaster only happens where a hazard coincides with a vulnerable population; a hazard in an unpopulated area (for example a remote mid-ocean ridge eruption) is not a disaster.\n• A disaster requires significant impacts (deaths, injury, economic loss) that exceed a community's ability to cope using its own resources.\n• Vulnerability depends on social, economic and political factors: poverty, poor housing, low education and weak governance increase vulnerability.\n• Resilience and capacity to cope — preparation, building codes, warning systems, insurance — reduce impacts so a hazard of similar magnitude may not cause a disaster (for example Chile 2010 Mw 8.8 with around 500 deaths).\n• Risk can be summarised as Risk = (Hazard × Vulnerability) ÷ Capacity to cope.\n• Hazard magnitude and type matter, but a low-magnitude event in a highly vulnerable place may still become a disaster.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Degg's model.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial application of the model to explain the link between hazard and vulnerability.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed knowledge, clearly applied; explains how vulnerability, resilience and capacity determine whether a hazard becomes a disaster, using the model and examples.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 11. Figure 11 is a Pressure and Release (PAR) model for the 2010 Haiti earthquake. Root causes: a long history of political instability and corruption; national debt; extreme poverty. Dynamic pressures: rapid urbanisation in Port-au-Prince; lack of building regulation and training; few emergency services. Unsafe conditions: densely packed informal housing on steep slopes; poorly built concrete buildings; most people on very low incomes. These are shown as an arrow of increasing pressure meeting the hazard: a shallow Mw 7.0 earthquake about 25 km from the capital.\n\nExplain how the factors shown in Figure 11 increased vulnerability to the 2010 earthquake.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The PAR model shows disaster as the result of pressure from vulnerability meeting the physical hazard.\n• Root causes (political instability, corruption, debt, poverty) limited government capacity to invest in building codes, infrastructure and emergency planning.\n• Dynamic pressures translate root causes into unsafe conditions: rapid urbanisation led to unregulated informal construction; lack of training meant poor-quality concrete and reinforcement.\n• Unsafe conditions — dense, poorly built housing on steep slopes — meant many buildings collapsed and landslides occurred, causing very high deaths (estimates range from about 160,000 to over 300,000).\n• Few emergency services and damage to the port, airport and hospitals slowed rescue and relief, increasing deaths and the spread of disease (cholera later in 2010).\n• Because of low incomes and little insurance, households could not recover, increasing long-term impacts.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; factors listed from Figure 11 without explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate explanation of how some factors increased vulnerability; partial links between levels of the model.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed explanation that clearly links root causes through dynamic pressures to unsafe conditions and to the impacts of the earthquake.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 12 (approximate published figures). Figure 12 compares two earthquakes in 2010.\nHaiti, 12 January 2010: Mw 7.0; focus about 13 km deep; epicentre about 25 km from Port-au-Prince; estimated deaths 160,000–316,000.\nChile, 27 February 2010: Mw 8.8; focus about 35 km deep; epicentre about 100 km from Concepción; deaths about 525.\n\nExplain how differences in governance may help explain the contrasting impacts shown in Figure 12.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The Chile earthquake released far more energy (about 1.8 units larger, so over 500 times more energy), yet deaths were several hundred times lower, so human factors must explain much of the difference.\n• Chile has strong building codes, developed after earlier earthquakes (for example 1960), which are enforced; many buildings resisted shaking.\n• Chile had trained emergency services, national disaster planning and public education; Haiti had weak, under-resourced government institutions.\n• Corruption and political instability in Haiti meant regulations were not enforced and informal construction was common.\n• Governance affects response: Chile coordinated its own relief quickly; in Haiti government buildings and ministries were destroyed, so relief relied on international agencies and was slow to coordinate.\n• Other factors also matter (Haiti's very shallow focus and proximity to the capital), so governance is not the only explanation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic statements about governance with limited use of Figure 12.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge of governance, partly applied to the two earthquakes.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, accurate knowledge clearly applied, explaining how specific aspects of governance led to contrasting impacts, using evidence from Figure 12.` },
      { q: `On 22 February 2011 a shallow Mw 6.2 earthquake struck Christchurch, New Zealand, killing 185 people. Large parts of the eastern suburbs, built on loose, water-saturated sands and silts near the Avon River, were affected by liquefaction; thousands of homes were later demolished and the area was cleared as a 'red zone'.\n\nExplain why secondary hazards such as liquefaction can increase the impacts of an earthquake.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Liquefaction occurs when ground shaking increases pore-water pressure in saturated, unconsolidated sediments so they lose strength and behave like a liquid.\n• Buildings tilt, sink or crack because foundations lose support, even if the structure itself resists shaking — explaining widespread house damage in eastern Christchurch.\n• Sand boils and silt ejection damage roads; underground pipes, sewers and power cables fracture, cutting services for weeks.\n• Damage to infrastructure slows rescue and recovery and raises economic losses (insurance claims; the 'red zone' meant whole suburbs were abandoned).\n• Other secondary hazards — landslides, tsunamis, fires from broken gas mains — similarly extend damage beyond the area of primary shaking.\n• Secondary hazards may affect areas far from the epicentre or occur after the main shaking, so their impact is harder to plan for.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge of secondary hazards; little reference to Christchurch.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate explanation of liquefaction and its effects, partly applied to the context.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, accurate explanation of the process and how it increases social and economic impacts, clearly applied to Christchurch.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 13. Figure 13 is the hazard management cycle. It is a circular diagram with four stages linked by arrows: Mitigation → Preparedness → Response → Recovery → back to Mitigation. A hazard event is shown occurring between Preparedness and Response.\n\nExplain how actions in the preparedness stage of Figure 13 can reduce the impacts of a tectonic hazard.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Preparedness takes place before the event and aims to reduce vulnerability, so that the response stage is quicker and more effective.\n• Education and drills (for example Japan's annual Disaster Prevention Day on 1 September, ShakeOut drills in California) teach 'drop, cover and hold on' and evacuation routes, reducing deaths.\n• Early warning systems (Japan's earthquake early warning, Pacific Tsunami Warning Center, Indian Ocean Tsunami Warning System since 2006) give seconds to minutes to stop trains, shut gas supplies and move to high ground.\n• Hazard mapping and evacuation plans show where to go and help officials plan shelters, as with the evacuation around Mount Pinatubo in 1991, which saved many thousands of lives.\n• Stockpiling supplies and training emergency services shortens the time before relief arrives.\n• Preparedness links back to recovery and mitigation: lessons learned feed into the next cycle.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic statements about being prepared.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge of preparedness, partly linked to reducing impacts.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed knowledge with clear explanation of how different preparedness actions reduce impacts, linked to the cycle in Figure 13 and examples.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 14 (approximate published figures). Figure 14 is a map of Europe showing the spread of the ash cloud from Eyjafjallajökull, Iceland, between 14 and 20 April 2010, covering most of northern and western Europe. Text boxes state: 'About 100,000 flights cancelled over six days'; 'About 10 million passengers affected'; 'Airlines lost an estimated US$1.7 billion'; 'Kenyan flower exporters unable to send produce to Europe'.\n\nExplain why a volcanic eruption of moderate size (VEI 4) such as Eyjafjallajökull had such widespread impacts.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The eruption occurred beneath an ice cap; meltwater chilled the magma rapidly, fragmenting it into very fine, glassy ash that stayed suspended for longer.\n• The ash column reached the jet stream and prevailing north-westerly winds carried it over European airspace, which is among the busiest in the world.\n• Volcanic ash is a hazard to jet engines (it melts and can cause engine failure), so authorities closed airspace as a precaution.\n• Globalisation and interdependence: just-in-time supply chains and air-freighted perishable goods (Kenyan flowers and vegetables) meant impacts spread to economies far from Iceland.\n• Many travellers were stranded worldwide, with knock-on impacts on businesses, tourism and insurance.\n• Local impacts in Iceland were small (jökulhlaups, ash on farmland), showing that the scale of a disaster depends on connections, not only magnitude.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited reference to Figure 14.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate explanation of the impacts, partly linked to the nature of the eruption or to global connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, accurate explanation linking the physical characteristics of the eruption with global connectivity, using evidence from Figure 14.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 15. Figure 15 is a photograph of the town of Armero, Colombia, after the 1985 eruption of Nevado del Ruiz. It shows a wide, flat sheet of grey mud with the roofs of a few buildings sticking out. A caption states that a relatively small eruption melted part of the summit ice cap and that a mudflow travelling down a river valley buried the town about 70 km from the volcano, killing over 20,000 people.\n\nExplain why lahars such as the one shown in Figure 15 are a major volcanic hazard.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Lahars are mudflows of volcanic ash and debris mixed with water from melting ice and snow, crater lakes or heavy rainfall.\n• They follow river valleys and can travel tens of kilometres at high speed, so they affect people far from the volcano who may feel safe (Armero was about 70 km away).\n• They have the consistency of wet concrete; they bury settlements and set hard, making rescue extremely difficult.\n• They can be triggered by small eruptions, or by rainfall long after an eruption, so they are hard to predict.\n• Armero shows the importance of governance: hazard maps showing the town at risk existed but warnings were not acted upon and no evacuation was ordered.\n• Lahars destroy farmland, bridges and roads, causing long-term economic impacts.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge of lahars; little use of the figure.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge, partly applied to explain the hazard shown.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Detailed, accurate explanation of the nature of lahars and why they cause high losses, clearly applied to Figure 15.` },
      { q: `Figure 16 is a cross-section of the Earth from the Mid-Atlantic Ridge to the Andes. It shows convection currents in the mantle, a rising mantle plume beneath the ridge, new oceanic crust being formed at the ridge, and the Nazca Plate sinking steeply into the mantle at the Peru–Chile Trench.\n\nExplain the processes that cause the movement of tectonic plates.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Heat from radioactive decay in the core and mantle drives mantle convection; hot, less dense material rises and cooler, denser material sinks.\n• Mantle convection was the traditional explanation for plates being dragged along, but it is now thought to be less important than forces at plate edges.\n• Slab pull: cold, dense oceanic lithosphere sinks at subduction zones, pulling the rest of the plate behind it; this is now considered the main driving force.\n• Ridge push (gravitational sliding): new lithosphere at ridges is hot and elevated, so plates slide away down the slope from the ridge as they cool and become denser.\n• Sea-floor spreading at ridges provides evidence: palaeomagnetic stripes of normal and reversed polarity are symmetrical either side of ridges, and the age of oceanic crust increases away from ridges.\n• Mantle plumes and hotspots show deep-mantle upwelling which may contribute to plate movement and rifting.\n• Subduction and the Benioff zone show plates being recycled, balancing new crust formation.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; limited understanding of the processes.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge of some processes with some explanation of how they cause movement.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge of a range of processes; clear explanation of how slab pull, ridge push and convection interact, supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `The 2015 Nepal earthquake (Mw 7.8) killed around 9,000 people. The 2016 Kaikōura earthquake in New Zealand (Mw 7.8) killed 2 people.\n\nExplain why earthquakes of similar magnitude can have very different impacts.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\nPhysical factors:\n• Focus depth: shallow-focus earthquakes produce stronger surface shaking than deep-focus ones.\n• Distance from the epicentre to population centres (Nepal's epicentre was near Kathmandu and densely populated valleys; Kaikōura was in a sparsely populated area).\n• Geology: soft sediments amplify ground shaking and may liquefy (Kathmandu valley lake sediments); bedrock reduces shaking.\n• Secondary hazards: steep Himalayan slopes caused landslides and avalanches; tsunamis at subduction zones.\n• Duration of shaking and timing (time of day affects whether people are indoors).\nHuman factors:\n• Building quality and enforcement of building codes (unreinforced brick and masonry in Nepal; codes enforced in New Zealand).\n• Population density and urbanisation.\n• Level of development, governance, preparedness and emergency services; access to remote mountain villages.\n• Wealth and insurance affect recovery.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge of physical and/or human factors with some explanation of how they affect impacts.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge of a range of physical and human factors, with clear explanation of how they interact to produce different impacts.` },
      { q: `Composite volcanoes such as Soufrière Hills on Montserrat (which erupted repeatedly from 1995, killing 19 people in 1997 and burying the capital Plymouth) can produce a wide range of hazards.\n\nExplain the threats to people from primary and secondary volcanic hazards.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\nPrimary hazards:\n• Pyroclastic flows (pyroclastic density currents): fast-moving clouds of hot gas and rock that travel at over 100 km/h and are often over 500 °C; nearly impossible to escape — killed 19 people on Montserrat in 1997.\n• Tephra and ash fall: heavy ash causes roofs to collapse, damages crops and causes respiratory problems; volcanic bombs near the vent.\n• Lava flows: usually slow-moving so they rarely kill but destroy property and farmland.\n• Volcanic gases: CO₂ and SO₂ can suffocate or poison people and livestock (for example Lake Nyos 1986 CO₂ release).\nSecondary hazards:\n• Lahars: mudflows following valleys far from the vent (Armero 1985).\n• Jökulhlaups: glacial outburst floods when eruptions occur beneath ice (Iceland).\n• Landslides/flank collapse and tsunamis.\n• Longer-term: contamination of water, famine, disease, climatic cooling (Pinatubo 1991).\nThreat depends on hazard type, eruption style, distance and warning time.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; limited explanation of threats.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge of several hazards with some explanation of their threats.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge of a range of primary and secondary hazards with clear explanation of why each threatens people.` },
      { q: `On 11 March 2011 a Mw 9.0 earthquake occurred off the north-east coast of Honshu, Japan. The tsunami reached heights of up to about 40 m, killed or left missing around 18,500 people, caused a nuclear accident at the Fukushima Daiichi power plant and caused economic losses estimated at about US$235 billion.\n\nExplain why the 2011 Tōhoku event is considered a megadisaster.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• A megadisaster is a high-magnitude, large-scale disaster with significant human and/or economic impacts affecting a large area, possibly with global consequences, often needing a complex response.\n• Physical scale: one of the largest earthquakes ever recorded; the rupture was hundreds of kilometres long and the tsunami travelled across the Pacific.\n• Multiple, cascading hazards: earthquake, tsunami and then a nuclear meltdown at Fukushima, leading to the evacuation of a 20 km zone and long-term contamination.\n• Economic scale: the costliest disaster on record at the time; damage to ports, factories and supply chains disrupted global car and electronics production.\n• Global consequences: debates about nuclear power (for example Germany's decision to phase out nuclear power).\n• Japan is a developed, highly prepared country with sea walls and warning systems, yet these were overwhelmed (for example sea walls at Taro were overtopped), showing that even high capacity to cope can be exceeded.\n• Complex recovery lasting over a decade, involving governments, insurers and international actors.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; limited understanding of what makes a megadisaster.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge with some explanation linking impacts to the idea of a megadisaster.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge with clear explanation of the scale, complexity and global reach of the event.` },
      { q: `A hazard profile is a diagram, often in the form of a radar chart, that compares hazards by characteristics such as magnitude, speed of onset, duration, areal extent, frequency and spatial predictability.\n\nExplain how hazard profiles can be used to compare tectonic hazards.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Hazard profiles plot several physical characteristics on a common scale, so contrasting hazards (earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, tsunamis) can be compared visually.\n• Magnitude: measured on scales such as moment magnitude (Mw) and VEI; the Mercalli scale measures intensity of shaking and damage.\n• Speed of onset: earthquakes are sudden with almost no warning; volcanic eruptions often give precursors; tsunamis may give minutes to hours.\n• Duration: earthquakes last seconds to minutes; eruptions may continue for months or years (Montserrat).\n• Areal extent: earthquakes and eruptions often affect a local or regional area; tsunamis and large eruptions may affect ocean basins or the globe.\n• Frequency and spatial predictability: most hazards occur at plate boundaries, so locations are predictable, but timing is not.\n• Profiles help managers prioritise resources and choose strategies (for example warnings for hazards with slower onset).\n• Limitations: profiles are subjective, ignore vulnerability and human factors, and the same hazard type can vary greatly between events.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; limited understanding of hazard profiles.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge of characteristics with some explanation of how they allow comparison.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; clear explanation of how profiles are used to compare hazards, with some recognition of limitations.` },
      { q: `Before the 1991 eruption of Mount Pinatubo, Philippines, scientists monitored increasing earthquakes, ground swelling and SO₂ emissions and ordered the evacuation of around 60,000 people. In contrast, a panel of scientists in L'Aquila, Italy, was criticised after a Mw 6.3 earthquake in 2009 killed 309 people, following weeks of small tremors.\n\nExplain why volcanic eruptions can often be forecast more successfully than earthquakes.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Volcanic eruptions usually have measurable precursors as magma rises: increasing small earthquakes (harmonic tremor), ground deformation measured by tiltmeters, GPS and satellite radar, and changes in gas emissions (SO₂, CO₂) and temperature.\n• Volcanoes are fixed points that can be intensively monitored by observatories; the Pinatubo evacuation saved many thousands of lives.\n• Earthquakes occur on faults that may be very long and deep; the build-up of stress cannot be measured directly.\n• There are no reliable precursors for earthquakes: foreshocks, radon emissions and animal behaviour have been tested but are inconsistent — L'Aquila tremors did not reliably signal the main shock.\n• Seismic gap theory and statistical frequency allow forecasting of probabilities over decades, not prediction of time and place.\n• Earthquake early warning detects P waves and gives only seconds of warning before S and surface waves arrive.\n• Even volcanic forecasting is uncertain: some eruptions occur with little warning and false alarms reduce trust.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated basic knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge of monitoring with some explanation of the difference between earthquakes and volcanoes.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge with clear explanation of why precursors make volcanic forecasting more successful, and the limits of both.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 17. Figure 17 shows strategies used to reduce earthquake damage in Tokyo, Japan: a high-rise building on rubber and steel base isolators; cross-bracing in the walls of an office block; a computer-controlled counterweight (mass damper) at the top of a skyscraper; and an area of older wooden houses being replaced by fire-resistant buildings with wider roads.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies that modify the event to reduce the impacts of earthquakes.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Modifying the event cannot change the earthquake itself, so strategies focus on land-use planning and engineering to resist shaking (aseismic design, retrofitting, base isolation, cross-bracing, mass dampers, fire breaks); for tsunamis, sea walls and coastal defences.\nAO2:\n• Aseismic engineering has been effective in developed countries: Japanese building codes meant few high-rise buildings collapsed in 2011, with most deaths caused by the tsunami.\n• Land-use zoning avoids building on unstable ground or fault lines and replaces high-risk areas with open space or firebreaks.\n• Effectiveness is limited by cost: retrofitting is expensive, so it is uncommon in developing countries; low-cost options (bamboo frames, rubber tyre foundations) may help.\n• Effectiveness depends on governance and enforcement of codes; older buildings may remain vulnerable.\n• Engineering can create a false sense of security: Japanese sea walls were overtopped by the 2011 tsunami.\n• Strategies are most effective in combination with modifying vulnerability (education, warnings) and loss (insurance, aid).\n• Judgement: highly effective for reducing deaths from shaking where wealth and governance allow, but less effective for extreme events and for poorer communities.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic, unbalanced assessment; limited use of Figure 17.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment with some reference to Figure 17; a conclusion may be offered but is weakly supported.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured assessment of several strategies; a reasoned conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, well-supported assessment drawing on Figure 17 and examples, leading to a fully justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 18 (data written for this practice question). Figure 18 shows trends in tectonic disasters, 1980–2020, as three lines: the number of recorded disasters per year rises from about 20 to about 35 and then levels off after 2000; deaths per year fluctuate with no clear trend but show very large peaks in 2004 and 2010; economic losses rise from under US$10 billion per year to over US$40 billion, with a very large peak in 2011.\n\nAssess the extent to which tectonic disasters are becoming a greater threat.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• The frequency of tectonic events is broadly constant over time; changes in disaster trends reflect exposure, vulnerability and reporting.\n• Disaster databases (for example EM-DAT) have threshold criteria and improved reporting over time.\nAO2:\n• Rising economic losses reflect more valuable infrastructure and assets in exposed areas, especially in developed countries (Tōhoku 2011, about US$235 billion).\n• Population growth and rapid urbanisation in hazard zones (for example Kathmandu, Istanbul, Manila) increase the number of people exposed.\n• Deaths show no clear upward trend and are dominated by a few megadisasters; improved warning, building codes and preparedness have reduced deaths per event in many places.\n• The levelling off of disaster numbers since 2000 may suggest improved risk reduction.\n• Data reliability: more events recorded because of better communications, so the early part of the record may be understated.\n• The threat is uneven: growing for poorer, rapidly urbanising countries, while resilience increases elsewhere.\n• Judgement: the economic threat is increasing, but the human threat is not clearly growing; the trend depends on the measure used.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic, unbalanced assessment; limited use of Figure 18.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment with some interpretation of the trends; weakly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; structured assessment of several trends; reasoned conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; balanced, well-supported assessment interpreting all three trends in Figure 18, leading to a fully justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `With reference to tectonic events you have studied, such as the 2010 Haiti and 2011 Christchurch earthquakes:\n\nAssess the importance of the level of development in determining the impacts of tectonic hazards.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Development (GDP per capita, HDI) affects vulnerability and resilience; the inverse relationship between development and deaths; developed countries often have higher absolute economic losses but lower losses relative to GDP.\nAO2:\n• Haiti 2010 (Mw 7.0, deaths 160,000 or more) compared with Christchurch 2011 (Mw 6.2, 185 deaths): wealth supported building codes, emergency services and insurance in New Zealand.\n• Developed countries can afford monitoring, warning systems and aseismic buildings; recovery is faster (Park's model).\n• However, physical factors are also important: magnitude, depth, distance from population centres, and secondary hazards (the tsunami in Tōhoku caused most deaths despite Japan's wealth).\n• Governance can be more important than wealth: corruption and weak enforcement can make middle-income countries vulnerable (for example building collapses in Turkey 2023), while some poorer places with good community preparedness (Bangladesh-style community warning, Indonesia after 2004) reduce deaths.\n• Economic impacts are higher in absolute terms in developed countries.\n• Judgement: development is a major factor shaping vulnerability, but it operates through governance and preparedness and interacts with hazard magnitude.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic, unbalanced assessment.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment using some examples; weakly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; structured assessment of development against other factors; reasoned conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; balanced, well-supported assessment with detailed examples, leading to a fully justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 19. Figure 19 shows two versions of Park's model plotted on the same axes (quality of life against time). Curve A, for a developed country, drops a little at the time of the event, recovers quickly through relief and rehabilitation, and ends above its pre-disaster level. Curve B, for a developing country, drops much further, stays low during a long relief and rehabilitation period, and ends below its pre-disaster level.\n\nAssess the usefulness of Park's model in understanding responses to tectonic disasters.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Park's model shows quality of life against time through pre-disaster normality, the event, relief (hours–days), rehabilitation (weeks–months) and reconstruction (months–years), and the possibility of recovering to a better or worse state.\nAO2:\n• Useful for comparing events: Figure 19 shows that wealth, preparedness and governance affect the depth of the fall and speed of recovery (for example Christchurch compared with Haiti).\n• Useful for planning: identifies when different types of help (search and rescue, temporary shelter, reconstruction) are needed and highlights mitigation and preparedness to raise the pre-disaster level.\n• Encourages building back better, so the curve ends higher.\n• Limitations: it is a generalised model with no real scale; quality of life is hard to measure; phases overlap in reality.\n• It does not show different experiences within a population (the poor may recover more slowly than the wealthy) or cascading events and aftershocks (Christchurch 2010–11 sequence; Fukushima).\n• It is descriptive rather than explanatory, so it should be used alongside the PAR model and the hazard management cycle.\n• Judgement: useful as a framework to compare and plan responses, but limited in explaining why responses differ.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic, unbalanced assessment; limited use of Figure 19.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment of usefulness; weakly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; structured assessment of strengths and limitations; reasoned conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; balanced, well-supported assessment drawing on Figure 19 and examples, leading to a fully justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `After the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, an Indian Ocean Tsunami Warning System was set up, using seismometers, deep-ocean pressure sensors and national warning centres. Japan, before the 2011 Tōhoku tsunami, already had a warning system, sea walls up to 10 m high and regular evacuation drills, yet around 18,500 people died or were missing.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of strategies used to manage the impacts of tsunami hazards.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Strategies modify the event (sea walls, breakwaters, mangrove replanting), modify vulnerability (warning systems, hazard mapping, evacuation routes and vertical evacuation buildings, education) and modify loss (aid, insurance).\nAO2:\n• Warning systems are effective for distant tsunamis, giving hours of warning (Pacific Tsunami Warning Center), but less so for local tsunamis that arrive in minutes.\n• Warnings only work if communicated to communities (the 'last mile'); in 2004 there was no system, which contributed to about 230,000 deaths.\n• Japan 2011: warnings were issued within minutes and many evacuated, saving many lives, but initial estimates of wave height were too low and some sea walls were overtopped, giving a false sense of security.\n• Sea walls are expensive and can damage the coastal environment and tourism; mangroves are cheaper and sustainable but give less protection against very large waves.\n• Education is cheap and effective (for example indigenous knowledge on Simeulue, Indonesia, in 2004 saved many lives).\n• Aid and reconstruction after 2004 were large but uneven, and some coastal communities were displaced.\n• Judgement: a combination of strategies is most effective; hard engineering alone is least reliable for megadisasters.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; generic, unbalanced assessment.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment of some strategies; weakly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; structured assessment of a range of strategies; reasoned conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; balanced, well-supported assessment drawing on both events, leading to a fully justified conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 12 (approximate published figures), which compares two earthquakes in 2010.\nHaiti, 12 January 2010: Mw 7.0; focus about 13 km deep; estimated deaths 160,000–316,000.\nChile, 27 February 2010: Mw 8.8; focus about 35 km deep; deaths about 525.\n\nUsing Figure 12 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that governance is the most important factor in determining a country's vulnerability and resilience to tectonic hazards.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Governance: how a country is run — the quality of institutions, laws, regulation, corruption, planning and emergency services, at national and local levels.\n• Vulnerability and resilience depend on physical, economic, social and political factors; Risk = (Hazard × Vulnerability) ÷ Capacity to cope.\nAO2 — for the view:\n• Figure 12: Chile's earthquake was far larger but caused far fewer deaths; Chile enforced building codes and had trained emergency services, while Haiti had weak and corrupt institutions.\n• Governance influences land-use planning, building regulation, warning systems, education and disaster response (for example Japan's national preparedness).\n• Poor governance prevents effective use of aid (Haiti reconstruction problems).\n• Weak enforcement of building codes contributed to deaths in Turkey and Syria in 2023.\nAO2 — against the view:\n• Economic development determines what a government can afford, so wealth and governance are linked.\n• Physical factors: magnitude, depth, proximity to population and secondary hazards (Haiti's shallow focus near the capital; Tōhoku tsunami despite good governance).\n• Social factors: population density, urbanisation, education, age and gender affect vulnerability.\n• Community resilience and NGOs can partly compensate for weak governance (for example community-based preparedness in Indonesia after 2004).\n• Geographical isolation (Nepal 2015) affects response regardless of governance.\nConclusion: governance is often the key factor that turns wealth into resilience, but its importance varies with hazard magnitude and other factors.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 12; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation with partial use of Figure 12; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation considering several factors; a conclusion that is partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation drawing on Figure 12 and a range of examples, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` },
      { q: `With reference to the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, the 2011 Tōhoku earthquake and tsunami, and the 2010 Eyjafjallajökull eruption:\n\nEvaluate the view that megadisasters need a different management approach from smaller tectonic disasters.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Megadisasters: high-magnitude, large-scale events affecting large areas or multiple countries, with complex or global impacts; may involve cascading hazards.\n• Management through modifying the event, vulnerability and loss; the hazard management cycle; Park's model.\nAO2 — for the view:\n• 2004: impacts in 14 countries required an international response (UN coordination, large-scale aid) and a regional warning system; national approaches were insufficient.\n• Tōhoku: cascading hazards (earthquake, tsunami, nuclear accident) overwhelmed national plans designed for smaller events; new design standards for extreme events were required.\n• Eyjafjallajökull: a moderate eruption with global economic impacts needed international coordination of airspace management and new ash concentration thresholds — managing systems rather than local people.\n• Scale of recovery needs global finance, insurers and long-term reconstruction.\nAO2 — against the view:\n• The same principles apply: preparedness, education and warning reduce deaths for both small and large events.\n• Local community action is still vital in megadisasters (evacuation knowledge in Japan, Simeulue 2004).\n• Many smaller disasters in poorer countries also need international help (Haiti 2010).\n• Good management of everyday hazards builds the resilience needed for megadisasters.\nConclusion: megadisasters require greater scale, international coordination and planning for cascading and global impacts, but are built on the same core strategies.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited reference to the events; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation with partial reference to the events; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation using the events; a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation drawing effectively on all three events, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 20. Figure 20 shows three groups of strategies for managing tectonic hazards. Modifying the event: land-use zoning, aseismic buildings, sea walls, lava diversion (as on Heimaey, Iceland, in 1973, where sea water was pumped onto lava flows). Modifying vulnerability: monitoring and warning systems, hazard mapping, education and evacuation drills. Modifying loss: emergency aid, insurance, reconstruction funding.\n\nUsing Figure 20 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that modifying vulnerability is the most effective way to manage tectonic hazards.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Explanation of the three approaches in Figure 20 and their place in the hazard management cycle.\nAO2 — for the view:\n• Monitoring and warning save lives: Pinatubo 1991 evacuation; Japan's earthquake early warning; the Indian Ocean Tsunami Warning System since 2006.\n• Education and drills are low-cost and suitable for poorer countries; community preparedness builds long-term resilience.\n• Hazard mapping enables evacuation planning (but Armero 1985 shows maps need to be acted upon).\n• Modifying the event is often impossible for earthquakes and eruptions; only the effects can be modified.\nAO2 — against the view:\n• Modifying the event through aseismic buildings is crucial because buildings kill people; Chile and Japan show this is highly effective.\n• Lava diversion on Heimaey and Etna shows some volcanic hazards can be modified.\n• Earthquakes cannot be predicted, so warnings give seconds only; vulnerability strategies depend on people responding correctly.\n• Modifying loss through aid and insurance is essential for recovery and to stop disasters increasing poverty (Haiti); insurance is less available in developing countries.\n• Effectiveness depends on hazard type, magnitude, wealth and governance.\nConclusion: modifying vulnerability is often the most cost-effective in saving lives, especially in developing countries, but an integrated approach using all three is most effective.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 20; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation with partial use of examples; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation of different approaches; a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation drawing on Figure 20 and a range of examples, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` },
      { q: `Each year earthquakes cause far more deaths worldwide than volcanic eruptions, but around 800 million people live within 100 km of an active volcano.\n\nEvaluate the view that earthquakes present a greater risk to people than volcanic eruptions.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Risk as the probability of harm from a hazard; Risk = (Hazard × Vulnerability) ÷ Capacity to cope.\n• Hazard profiles: speed of onset, duration, areal extent, frequency and predictability of earthquakes and eruptions.\nAO2 — for the view:\n• Earthquakes are sudden with little or no warning, cannot be predicted, and affect large urban areas (Haiti 2010, Kashmir 2005, Turkey–Syria 2023 each killed tens of thousands).\n• Secondary hazards — tsunamis, landslides, liquefaction, fire — extend impacts (2004, Tōhoku 2011).\n• Many large cities lie on plate boundaries (Tokyo, Istanbul, Tehran, Los Angeles).\nAO2 — against the view:\n• Volcanoes have precursors that allow forecasting and evacuation, but very explosive eruptions can affect wide areas and the global climate (Pinatubo 1991 cooled global temperatures by about 0.5 °C).\n• Some volcanic hazards are very deadly: pyroclastic flows and lahars (Armero 1985).\n• Volcanic eruptions can last for months or years, causing long-term displacement (Montserrat).\n• Eyjafjallajökull 2010 shows volcanic risk can be economic and global.\n• A rare super-eruption would pose a greater risk than any earthquake.\n• Risk depends more on vulnerability and capacity than on the hazard type.\nConclusion: in terms of deaths and frequency earthquakes pose a greater risk, but risk depends on location, magnitude and vulnerability.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation with limited examples; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation of both hazards; a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation with a range of examples, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 11. Figure 11 is a Pressure and Release (PAR) model for the 2010 Haiti earthquake. Root causes: political instability, corruption, debt and extreme poverty. Dynamic pressures: rapid urbanisation, lack of building regulation, few emergency services. Unsafe conditions: dense, poorly built housing on steep slopes; very low incomes. These meet the hazard: a shallow Mw 7.0 earthquake near Port-au-Prince.\n\nUsing Figure 11 and your own knowledge, evaluate the usefulness of the PAR model compared with other models in understanding tectonic disasters.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• The PAR model, Degg's model, Park's model and the hazard management cycle, and what each shows.\nAO2 — strengths of the PAR model:\n• Figure 11 shows that the PAR model explains why disasters occur, by tracing vulnerability to root causes — not only the physical hazard.\n• It highlights the role of governance, poverty and urbanisation, so it identifies where long-term action is needed (for example reducing corruption and enforcing building codes).\n• It can be applied to compare places (Haiti and Chile) and shows how pressure could be released.\nAO2 — limitations and comparison:\n• It focuses on the causes of vulnerability and gives little detail on hazard magnitude or on the response and recovery over time (Park's model).\n• Degg's model is simpler and shows clearly that a disaster requires both hazard and vulnerable population, but lacks depth.\n• Park's model and the hazard management cycle are more useful for planning response and recovery.\n• The PAR model is qualitative and hard to measure; root causes may be contested and may operate at a global scale (debt, colonial history).\n• It is less useful for megadisasters in developed countries where vulnerability is low but magnitude is extreme (Tōhoku).\nConclusion: the PAR model is the most useful for explaining why disasters happen where they do, but it needs to be used alongside other models to understand the full disaster sequence.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 11; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation comparing models; a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation drawing on Figure 11 and other models with examples, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 18 (data written for this practice question). Figure 18 shows trends in tectonic disasters, 1980–2020: the number of recorded disasters per year rises from about 20 to about 35 and then levels off after 2000; deaths per year fluctuate with no clear trend but show very large peaks in 2004 and 2010; economic losses rise from under US$10 billion per year to over US$40 billion, with a very large peak in 2011.\n\nUsing Figure 18 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that the impacts of tectonic hazards have increased over time.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Measures of impact: deaths, numbers affected, economic losses; the role of disaster databases and their threshold criteria; reporting changes over time.\nAO2 — for the view:\n• Figure 18 shows rising economic losses, reflecting more people and more valuable assets in exposed areas, especially cities in developed and emerging economies (Tōhoku 2011).\n• Recorded disasters increased to 2000, linked to population growth and urbanisation in hazard zones.\n• Megadisasters in 2004, 2010 and 2011 show extreme impacts; globalisation spreads impacts further (Eyjafjallajökull 2010, supply chains after Tōhoku).\nAO2 — against the view:\n• Deaths show no clear trend and are dominated by a few events; deaths per event have fallen in many places due to building codes, warnings and preparedness.\n• The increase in disasters may be due to better reporting rather than more events; tectonic event frequency is roughly constant.\n• Economic losses are often not adjusted for inflation or GDP; as a proportion of GDP they may not have increased.\n• Disaster numbers levelled off after 2000, suggesting risk reduction is working.\n• Trends vary between places: impacts may be falling in developed countries but rising in rapidly urbanising developing ones.\nConclusion: economic and global impacts have increased, but there is little evidence that human impacts have increased, and data reliability limits firm conclusions.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 18; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation with partial use of Figure 18; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation of trends; a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation interpreting Figure 18 critically with examples, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 21 (approximate published figures). Figure 21 compares four tectonic events.\nKobe, Japan, 1995: Mw 6.9; shallow focus; about 6,400 deaths.\nSichuan, China, 2008: Mw 7.9; shallow focus; about 87,000 dead or missing; many landslides.\nChristchurch, New Zealand, 2011: Mw 6.2; very shallow focus; 185 deaths; widespread liquefaction.\nNepal, 2015: Mw 7.8; about 9,000 deaths; landslides and avalanches.\n\nUsing Figure 21 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that physical factors are more important than human factors in determining the scale of a tectonic disaster.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Physical factors: magnitude, focus depth, distance from population, geology, secondary hazards, hazard type, time of day, duration.\n• Human factors: development, governance, population density, building quality, preparedness, education, emergency services.\nAO2 — for the view:\n• Figure 21: the higher-magnitude events (Sichuan, Nepal) caused more deaths, and secondary hazards such as landslides in mountainous terrain increased impacts.\n• Geology matters: liquefaction at Christchurch; soft sediments in the Kathmandu valley amplify shaking.\n• Megadisasters (Tōhoku 2011) show that extreme magnitude can overwhelm even well-prepared countries.\nAO2 — against the view:\n• Kobe (Japan, developed) had fewer deaths than Sichuan despite being shallow and near a city; building codes were strengthened after 1995.\n• Sichuan: poorly built schools collapsed (the 'tofu-dreg' schools controversy), showing governance failure.\n• Haiti 2010 (Mw 7.0) and Chile 2010 (Mw 8.8) show human factors outweighing magnitude.\n• Nepal: unreinforced masonry, poverty and remoteness slowed rescue.\n• Physical and human factors interact (the PAR model): a hazard only becomes a disaster where vulnerability exists (Degg's model).\nConclusion: physical factors set the potential scale of a disaster, but human factors usually determine the actual scale, especially for deaths.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 21; judgement, if offered, is unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; an unbalanced evaluation with partial use of Figure 21; generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; a structured evaluation of physical and human factors; a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, detailed knowledge; a balanced, coherent evaluation interpreting Figure 21 critically alongside other examples, leading to a fully substantiated conclusion.` }
    ]
  },
  'T2': {
    name: 'Landscape Systems, Processes & Change',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 1 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1 shows the winter accumulation and summer ablation, in metres water equivalent (m w.e.), for a small Alpine valley glacier, 2018–2022.\n\nYear | Winter accumulation (m w.e.) | Summer ablation (m w.e.)\n2018 | 1.42 | 2.05\n2019 | 1.18 | 2.31\n2020 | 1.65 | 1.89\n2021 | 1.21 | 2.62\n2022 | 1.30 | 2.14\n\nCalculate the mean annual net mass balance of the glacier for 2018–2022. You must show your working. Give your answer in m w.e. to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Net balance for each year (accumulation − ablation): −0.63, −1.13, −0.24, −1.41, −0.84; total = −4.25 (1)\n• Mean = −4.25 ÷ 5 = −0.85 m w.e. (1)\nAnswer must be negative for the second mark. Award 2 marks for a correct answer with no working only if −0.85 is stated; accept −0.85 m w.e. per year.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 1 (data written for this practice question).\nA student measured the distance from five fixed marker posts to the cliff edge on the Holderness coast, near Aldbrough, in 2014 and again in 2024.\n\nMarker post | Distance to cliff edge 2014 (m) | Distance to cliff edge 2024 (m)\nA | 42.0 | 23.5\nB | 37.5 | 21.0\nC | 50.2 | 29.4\nD | 44.8 | 27.1\nE | 39.0 | 22.0\n\nCalculate the mean annual rate of cliff retreat for the five posts between 2014 and 2024. You must show your working. Give your answer in metres per year to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Retreat at each post: 18.5, 16.5, 20.8, 17.7, 17.0 m; total = 90.5 m, so mean retreat = 18.1 m over 10 years (1)\n• 18.1 ÷ 10 = 1.81 m per year (1)\nAccept total retreat ÷ 50 post-years = 1.81 m/yr.` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. A student investigated whether there was a significant relationship between distance from the snout of a glacier and the mean size of clasts on its outwash plain (sandur) in Iceland. Mean clast long-axis length was measured at 10 sites.\nThe sum of the squared differences in rank was Σd² = 298.\nFormula: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n)\n\nCalculate the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (rs) for this data. You must show your working. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Substitution: rs = 1 − (6 × 298) ÷ (1000 − 10) = 1 − 1788 ÷ 990 (1)\n• rs = −0.81 (1)\nNote: 1 − 1.806 = −0.806; the answer must be negative for the second mark.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. You have carried out fieldwork on a shingle spit. The long-axis lengths (mm) of 11 pebbles sampled systematically along the crest of the spit were (data written for this practice question):\n34, 57, 22, 40, 27, 49, 18, 36, 25, 44, 31\n\nCalculate the interquartile range of the pebble lengths. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Data placed in rank order (18, 22, 25, 27, 31, 34, 36, 40, 44, 49, 57) and lower quartile = 25 mm (3rd value) and upper quartile = 44 mm (9th value) identified (1)\n• IQR = 44 − 25 = 19 mm (1)` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 2.\nFigure 2 is a pair of photographs of Imja Tsho, a proglacial lake at the snout of the Imja Glacier in the Khumbu region of Nepal. In the 1960s the photograph shows only a few small meltwater ponds on the glacier surface. By the 2010s the photograph shows a single lake of about 1.3 km² dammed by a high, unvegetated moraine ridge, with the debris-covered glacier snout ending in an ice cliff at the lake's edge (approximate figures).\n\nSuggest one reason why the lake has grown in size.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for development, up to 3 marks. For example:\n• Rising air temperatures in the Himalaya have increased ablation, so the glacier has thinned and retreated (1), leaving a hollow behind the terminal moraine that fills with meltwater (1), which is trapped by the moraine dam (1).\n• The ice cliff at the lake's edge calves into the lake (1) because warmer lake water undercuts it (1), so the lake extends up-valley as the snout retreats in a positive feedback (1).\n• Small supraglacial ponds absorb more solar radiation than ice because of their low albedo (1), so they melt the surrounding ice and merge (1) into one larger lake (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 3 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 3 is a line graph of the maximum depth of the active layer (m) above permafrost at a monitoring site near Utqiagvik, Alaska, 1995–2025. The line fluctuates from year to year but rises overall from about 0.35 m in 1995 to about 0.50 m in 2025.\n\nSuggest one reason for the change in active layer depth shown.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for development, up to 3 marks. For example:\n• Arctic air temperatures have risen faster than the global average (Arctic amplification) (1), so summer thaw penetrates further into the ground (1), deepening the layer that melts each year above the permafrost (1).\n• Reduced snow and sea-ice cover lowers albedo (1), so more solar energy is absorbed by the ground surface (1), raising ground temperatures (1).\n• Disturbance of the insulating vegetation/peat layer by buildings, roads or vehicle tracks (1) increases heat transfer into the ground (1), so thaw depth increases (1).\nYear-to-year fluctuation may be credited if linked to variations in summer temperature or snow cover.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 2 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 2 shows beach width measured on either side of a timber groyne on a coast where longshore drift moves sediment from west to east.\n\nSide of groyne | Mean beach width at high tide (m)\nWest (up-drift) side | 38\nEast (down-drift) side | 14\n\nSuggest one reason for the difference in beach width.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for development, up to 3 marks. For example:\n• The groyne acts as a barrier to longshore drift (1), so sediment moving eastwards accumulates against its west side (1), while the east side is starved of new sediment and continues to lose material to the drift (1).\n• On the down-drift side, waves continue to remove sediment (1) but it is not replaced (1), so the beach narrows and the cliffs/sea wall behind are exposed (1) (terminal groyne effect).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 4 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 4 shows mean annual surface velocity measured at stakes along the centre line of a valley glacier in Norway. The equilibrium line is approximately 2.5 km from the head of the glacier.\n\nDistance from glacier head (km) | Mean surface velocity (m/yr)\n0.5 | 12\n1.5 | 38\n2.5 | 61\n3.5 | 47\n4.5 | 21\n\nAnalyse the pattern of glacier velocity shown in Figure 4.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid point of analysis, up to 4 marks. For example:\n• Velocity increases from the head to a maximum of 61 m/yr at 2.5 km, a fivefold increase from 12 m/yr (1).\n• Maximum velocity coincides with the equilibrium line, where the greatest volume of ice passes through the cross-section (1).\n• Velocity then falls by about two-thirds, to 21 m/yr at 4.5 km, towards the snout (1).\n• This reflects extending flow above the ELA (ice thickening and accelerating as accumulation adds mass) and compressing flow below it as ablation removes mass and ice thins (1).\n• The pattern is asymmetrical: velocity rises by 49 m/yr over the 2 km above the ELA but falls by only 40 m/yr over the 2 km below it; the snout (21 m/yr) is faster than the head (12 m/yr). Accept other valid comparisons using the data (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. In the Dry Valleys region of Antarctica, glaciers are cold-based, with basal ice well below the pressure melting point. Glaciers in the Alps are mostly warm-based.\n\nExplain one reason why cold-based glaciers produce fewer erosional landforms than warm-based glaciers.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation, up to 4 marks. For example:\n• Cold-based glaciers are frozen to their beds, so there is little or no basal sliding (1). Movement is mainly by slow internal deformation of the ice (1), so there is little relative movement between ice and bedrock (1), meaning abrasion and plucking are very limited and features such as striations and roches moutonnées rarely form (1).\nOr:\n• There is no meltwater at the base of a cold-based glacier (1), so there is no freeze–thaw at the ice–rock interface to loosen joints for plucking (1) and no meltwater to remove debris or lubricate sliding (1), so erosion rates are much lower (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 3.\nFigure 3 is a simplified map of the Isle of Purbeck coast, Dorset. Between Ballard Point (chalk) in the north and Durlston Head (Portland limestone) in the south, the coast is cut across bands of rock at right angles. Swanage Bay, between the two headlands, is cut into weaker Wealden clays and sands. Waves approach the coast from the south-east.\n\nDraw an annotated diagram to show how wave refraction affects the distribution of wave energy along this coastline.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each of the following, up to 4 marks:\n• Diagram shows headlands and a bay with wave crests (orthogonals or wave fronts) approaching the coast (1).\n• Wave fronts bend as they enter shallower water off the headlands, slowing down and becoming parallel to the shore (1).\n• Annotation that wave energy is concentrated/converges on the headlands, increasing erosion there (e.g. hydraulic action, abrasion on Ballard Point / Durlston Head) (1).\n• Annotation that energy is dispersed/diverges in the bay, producing lower-energy waves and deposition of a beach in Swanage Bay (1).\nAnnotations must explain, not simply label, for full marks.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. The Holderness coast, East Yorkshire, is about 60 km long and is made mainly of glacial till (boulder clay). Recession rates vary along the coast, from very little at the chalk headland of Flamborough Head to over 2 m per year in places further south.\n\nExplain one reason why rates of cliff recession vary along the Holderness coast.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation, up to 4 marks. For example:\n• Lithology: Flamborough Head is resistant chalk, whereas most of the coast is unconsolidated till (1). Till is weakly bonded clay with sand and boulders (1), which is easily eroded by hydraulic action and becomes saturated and slumps (1), so recession is far faster on till than chalk (1).\nOr:\n• Beach width/sediment supply: where beaches are narrow, waves reach the cliff foot more often (1); the till produces mostly fine sediment which is carried offshore, so little beach builds up (1); sections down-drift of the Mappleton groynes or Hornsea sea wall are starved of sediment (1), so erosion increases there (1).\nOr: management (defended vs undefended stretches), or exposure to a long fetch from the north-east, if developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. The Sundarbans is the world's largest mangrove forest, covering about 10,000 km² of the Ganges–Brahmaputra delta in Bangladesh and India. It lies on a low-lying coast regularly affected by tropical cyclones, such as Cyclone Sidr (2007) and Cyclone Amphan (2020).\n\nExplain one reason why the mangrove forests of the Sundarbans reduce coastal flood risk.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation, up to 4 marks. For example:\n• Mangroves dissipate wave and storm-surge energy (1). Dense stilt roots and trunks create friction with the moving water (1), reducing wave height and surge velocity before they reach settlements and embankments inland (1), so the depth and extent of flooding behind the forest are reduced (1).\nOr:\n• Mangrove roots trap fine sediment from the delta's rivers (1), so the land surface builds up (accretes) (1), helping it keep pace with relative sea-level rise and subsidence (1), which reduces the frequency of inundation (1).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 5.\nFigure 5 is a line graph of temperature difference (°C) from the present day, reconstructed from the Vostok ice core in Antarctica, for the last 400,000 years. The line shows a repeated pattern of about four long, gradual cooling periods, each reaching about 6–8 °C below the present, each followed by a rapid rise in temperature to short warm periods about 0–2 °C above the present. Each cycle lasts about 100,000 years.\n\nExplain the causes of the long-term changes in temperature and ice cover shown in Figure 5.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Milankovitch cycles: eccentricity of Earth's orbit (about 100,000 years) matches the cycle length shown; obliquity/axial tilt (about 41,000 years) and precession (about 21,000–26,000 years) change the seasonal distribution of solar radiation.\n• Reduced summer insolation in high northern latitudes allows winter snow to survive, building ice sheets (glacials); increased insolation causes deglaciation (interglacials).\n• Positive feedbacks amplify small orbital changes: ice–albedo feedback; changes in atmospheric CO₂ and methane (lower in glacials as oceans absorb more CO₂).\n• Long, gradual cooling and rapid warming shown reflect slow ice-sheet growth and rapid collapse with feedbacks.\n• Other factors, e.g. variations in solar output and large volcanic eruptions, cause shorter-term fluctuations within the overall pattern.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 6.\nFigure 6 is a photograph of Red Tarn on Helvellyn, in the Lake District. It shows a deep, armchair-shaped hollow facing north-east, with a steep, rocky back wall over 200 m high. A small circular lake fills the floor of the hollow and is held in by a low rock lip at its outlet. Two sharp, narrow ridges (Striding Edge and Swirral Edge) run along either side of the hollow.\n\nExplain the glacial processes responsible for the formation of the landforms shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Corrie formation began with nivation in a pre-existing north-east facing hollow, where snow survived because of shading from the sun and drifting by prevailing south-westerly winds.\n• Snow compacted to firn and then glacier ice; rotational movement of the corrie glacier.\n• Plucking at the back wall, aided by freeze–thaw weathering in joints and the bergschrund, steepened the back wall.\n• Abrasion by debris frozen into the basal ice deepened the floor; reduced erosion at the front where ice thinned and moved upwards left a rock lip, now damming Red Tarn.\n• Arêtes (Striding Edge, Swirral Edge) formed where two corries/glaciers eroded back-to-back or side-by-side, with frost shattering sharpening the ridges.\n• Landforms are relict, formed during the Devensian and Loch Lomond Stadial.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 7 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 7 shows the equilibrium line altitude (ELA) of a valley glacier in the Swiss Alps.\n\nYear | ELA (m above sea level)\n1990 | 2,850\n2000 | 2,920\n2010 | 3,010\n2020 | 3,080\n\nExplain why the equilibrium line altitude of the glacier has changed and the possible effects on the glacier.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The ELA is the altitude where annual accumulation equals annual ablation; it has risen by 230 m in 30 years.\n• Rising summer temperatures increase ablation (melting, sublimation) at higher altitudes; warmer winters mean more precipitation falls as rain, reducing accumulation.\n• Reduced albedo as snow cover thins and dust/debris darkens the surface increases melting (positive feedback).\n• Effects: smaller accumulation zone and larger ablation zone, so a negative net mass balance; the glacier thins and the snout retreats.\n• Reduced flow velocity and supply of ice to the snout; eventual stagnation of the lower glacier; formation of proglacial lakes.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 8.\nFigure 8 is an aerial photograph of the Tuktoyaktuk Peninsula in northern Canada, an area of continuous permafrost with about 1,350 pingos. It shows Ibyuk Pingo, a dome-shaped, ice-cored hill about 49 m high rising from a flat, lake-dotted plain. Several shallow, circular lakes and drained lake basins surround it. The top of one smaller pingo nearby has collapsed to form a crater.\n\nExplain how the landforms shown in Figure 8 are formed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Closed-system (hydrostatic) pingos form in continuous permafrost, typically in drained lake basins as shown.\n• When a lake drains or shrinks, the unfrozen ground (talik) beneath it is exposed to freezing from above and from the surrounding permafrost.\n• Pore water is trapped and expelled towards the centre; it freezes to form a growing lens of ice, which pushes up the overlying sediment into a dome.\n• Growth is slow (centimetres a year), producing large features such as Ibyuk over centuries.\n• Stretching of the overlying sediment causes cracks at the summit; exposure of the ice core leads to melting and collapse, forming a crater and eventually a rampart (ognil).\n• Open-system (hydraulic) pingos in discontinuous permafrost may be mentioned for comparison.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 9.\nFigure 9 is a photograph of a lowland area in front of a retreating glacier in south-east Iceland. It shows a long, narrow, winding ridge of sand and gravel, about 10 m high, running across the valley floor in the direction of former ice flow. Beside it are several small, irregular mounds of sorted gravel and some water-filled, circular hollows. A wide, braided river crosses a flat plain of gravel in the foreground.\n\nExplain the role of meltwater in the formation of the landforms shown in Figure 9.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The ridge is an esker, deposited by meltwater flowing in a sub-glacial or englacial tunnel; deposition occurs as the channel is confined and the sediment load is high, and the feature is revealed when the ice retreats.\n• Mounds are kames, formed where meltwater deposited sediment in crevasses or depressions on or against the ice, collapsing when the ice melted.\n• Water-filled hollows are kettle holes, formed where blocks of stagnant ice, buried in outwash, melted and the ground subsided.\n• The flat gravel plain is an outwash plain (sandur), deposited by braided proglacial meltwater streams whose velocity falls as they spread out, with sorting so that coarser material is nearer the ice and finer further away.\n• Fluvioglacial sediment is sorted and stratified with rounded clasts, unlike unsorted, angular till.\n• Seasonal variations in meltwater discharge explain stratification and changing braided channels.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 4.\nFigure 4 is a simplified geology map of the coast at Lulworth Cove, Dorset. Bands of rock run parallel to the coastline: a narrow outer band of resistant Portland and Purbeck limestone along the shore, a wider band of weak Wealden clays and sands behind it, and resistant chalk forming the back of the cove. The cove is almost circular, with a narrow entrance through the limestone. Stair Hole, just to the west, shows a small breach in the limestone with eroded clays behind it.\n\nExplain how geological structure and lithology have influenced the coastal landforms shown in Figure 4.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• This is a concordant coast: rock bands run parallel to the coastline, so the resistant limestone acts as a protective barrier.\n• Wave erosion (hydraulic action, abrasion), possibly exploiting faults/joints, breached the limestone, as Stair Hole shows at an earlier stage.\n• Once breached, the weak Wealden clays and sands were rapidly eroded by waves and by subaerial processes (slumping), widening the cove behind the narrow entrance.\n• Wave diffraction through the narrow entrance spreads energy in a circular pattern, producing the rounded shape.\n• The resistant chalk at the back slows further erosion, limiting the cove's landward extent.\n• Folded/tilted strata (Lulworth Crumple) show the influence of structure (dip) on cliff profiles.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 5.\nFigure 5 is a photograph of a till cliff about 15 m high on the Holderness coast after a wet winter. The upper part of the cliff has slipped down in curved, back-tilted blocks with grass still on top. Below these is a lobe of wet, muddy material spreading onto a narrow sandy beach. Small streams of water seep from the cliff face, and waves are breaking at the foot of the cliff at high tide.\n\nExplain why mass movement contributes to rapid cliff retreat on this coast.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Till is unconsolidated and contains clay; after heavy rainfall it becomes saturated, increasing weight and pore-water pressure and reducing internal friction (shear strength).\n• Rotational slumping occurs along a curved slip plane, producing the back-tilted blocks shown.\n• Saturated material flows as mudflow, forming the lobe on the beach.\n• Seepage shows groundwater flowing through the cliff, weakening it further.\n• Marine erosion at the cliff foot (hydraulic action, abrasion at high tide) removes slumped debris and undercuts the cliff, so slopes are kept steep and unstable — a cycle linking subaerial and marine processes.\n• The narrow beach provides little protection, so slumped material is quickly removed and fine sediment is carried offshore.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 6.\nFigure 6 is a map of Spurn Head, at the southern end of the Holderness coast. It shows a narrow, curving ridge of sand and shingle about 5 km long extending south-west from the coast across the mouth of the Humber Estuary. The tip of the ridge is hooked towards the west. Mudflats and salt marsh lie in the sheltered water behind the ridge. The dominant waves approach from the north-east.\n\nExplain the formation of the landform shown in Figure 6.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• Spurn Head is a spit formed by longshore drift moving sediment southwards along the Holderness coast, sourced from eroded till cliffs.\n• Where the coastline changes direction at the Humber mouth, sediment continues to be deposited in deeper, lower-energy water, extending the spit.\n• Swash-aligned waves from the north-east and backwash under gravity move sediment along the shore in a zig-zag.\n• The hooked/recurved tip forms as waves are refracted round the end of the spit, and from secondary wave directions.\n• River flow from the Humber prevents the spit closing the estuary.\n• Sheltered conditions behind the spit allow deposition of fine silt and clay, forming mudflats colonised by halophytes to create salt marsh.\n• Spurn is dynamic: it has been breached by storm surges (e.g. December 2013), showing it is not in permanent equilibrium.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 7.\nFigure 7 is a photograph of the west coast of the Isle of Arran, Scotland. It shows a flat, grassy platform about 5–10 m above the present high-tide line, used by a coastal road. Behind the platform is a steep, vegetated former cliff line containing caves, such as King's Cave. The present-day beach and rocky shore lie seaward of the platform.\n\nExplain the role of sea-level change in the formation of the landforms shown in Figure 7.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The platform is a raised beach and the former cliff line is a relict (fossil) cliff with caves: features of an emergent coast.\n• During the Devensian glacial, the weight of ice sheets over Scotland depressed the crust.\n• After deglaciation, isostatic recovery (rebound) raised the land relative to the sea, lifting former wave-cut platforms, beaches and cliffs above the reach of present waves.\n• Eustatic sea-level rise after the ice melted initially flooded the coast; at times sea level and land level were stable long enough for waves to cut platforms and caves, then isostatic uplift outpaced eustatic rise.\n• The relict cliffs are now degraded by subaerial weathering and vegetated as marine erosion no longer acts on them.\n• Isostatic rebound continues today in Scotland, while southern England is subsiding.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 8 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 8 shows results from a vegetation transect across a sand dune system at Studland, Dorset.\n\nDistance from strandline (m) | Zone | Vegetation cover (%) | Soil pH | Main species\n0–20 | Embryo dunes | 5 | 8.4 | Sea rocket, sand couch grass\n20–60 | Fore (yellow) dunes | 40 | 7.9 | Marram grass\n60–120 | Grey dunes | 85 | 7.0 | Red fescue, lichens, mosses\n120–200 | Dune slack and heath | 95 | 6.2 | Heather, creeping willow\n200+ | Woodland | 100 | 5.5 | Birch, pine\n\nExplain the changes in vegetation along the transect shown in Figure 8.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 3):\n• The transect shows a psammosere: plant succession on sand, with older dunes further inland.\n• Pioneer species near the strandline tolerate salt spray, wind, drought and burial; sea rocket and sand couch trap sand to form embryo dunes.\n• Marram grass grows up through accumulating sand, stabilising the yellow dunes; cover is still only 40%.\n• As plants die, humus is added: soil becomes more acidic (pH falls from 8.4 to 5.5, as shell-derived calcium carbonate is leached), and moisture/nutrient retention improves, allowing less specialised species (fescue, lichens, mosses) to colonise grey dunes.\n• Dune slacks have a high water table, allowing moisture-loving species such as creeping willow; heather on acidic soils.\n• Woodland represents the climatic climax community, with 100% cover; each stage modifies conditions for the next.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find limited connections (AO2).\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find some logical connections (AO2).\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to the resource to find fully logical and relevant connections (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Glaciers in the Alps are mostly warm-based, while many glaciers in Antarctica and Greenland's interior are cold-based.\n\nExplain how glacier movement processes vary between warm-based and cold-based glaciers.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Warm-based (temperate) glaciers have basal ice at the pressure melting point, so meltwater is present at the bed.\n• Basal sliding dominates: meltwater lubricates the bed; regelation creep (pressure melting up-stream of obstacles and refreezing down-stream); enhanced basal creep around obstacles.\n• Velocities are relatively high (tens to hundreds of metres a year) and vary seasonally, with faster flow in summer when more meltwater reaches the bed; surges may occur.\n• Cold-based (polar) glaciers are frozen to the bed, so there is little or no basal sliding.\n• Movement is mainly by internal deformation: intergranular movement and laminar flow as ice crystals deform under stress; velocities are much lower (a few metres a year).\n• Factors affecting both: gradient, ice thickness, bedrock and debris, temperature; extending and compressing flow.\n• Polythermal glaciers combine both regimes.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. In Alaska, permafrost underlies about 80% of the state, and infrastructure such as the Trans-Alaska Pipeline and the towns of the North Slope is built on frozen ground.\n\nExplain why climate change is increasing the threats to fragile active and periglacial landscapes.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Rising temperatures (amplified in polar regions) increase ablation, causing negative mass balance, glacier thinning and retreat.\n• Permafrost thaw deepens the active layer, causing ground subsidence (thermokarst), damage to roads, buildings and pipelines, and slope failures.\n• Thawing permafrost releases stored CO₂ and methane, a positive feedback accelerating warming.\n• Formation and growth of proglacial lakes increases the risk of glacial lake outburst floods (GLOFs) in mountain regions.\n• Changes to ecosystems: species migration, loss of habitat for cold-adapted species, shrub encroachment into tundra.\n• Reduced meltwater supply in the longer term threatens water supply and HEP downstream.\n• Changes to the water cycle and to fragile, slow-recovering soils and vegetation reduce resilience.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. You have carried out fieldwork in a glaciated lowland area, such as the Vale of York, where exposures of both till and fluvioglacial sand and gravel are found.\n\nExplain how sediment and till fabric analysis can be used to distinguish between glacial and fluvioglacial deposits.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Till fabric analysis: measure the orientation (azimuth) and dip of the long axes of a sample of clasts (e.g. 50) in a till exposure, plotting results on a rose diagram.\n• In till deposited by ice, long axes are commonly aligned parallel to the direction of ice flow, so a strong preferred orientation is shown; fluvioglacial deposits show orientation related to water flow or weaker patterns.\n• Clast shape: measure roundness using a Powers' roundness index and size (long/intermediate/short axes); till clasts are angular to sub-angular because they were carried in or under the ice without much attrition; fluvioglacial clasts are rounded by water transport.\n• Sorting: till is unsorted (clay to boulders mixed); fluvioglacial deposits are sorted by size and stratified in layers, reflecting variations in meltwater discharge.\n• Striations on till clasts indicate abrasion at the glacier base.\n• Sampling (systematic or random), safety at exposures, and statistical tests (e.g. comparing mean roundness) improve reliability.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. The coast of England and Wales is divided into 11 major sediment cells. Cell 2 runs from Flamborough Head to the Wash and includes the Holderness coast and Spurn Head.\n\nExplain how sediment cells operate as coastal systems.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• A sediment (littoral) cell is a stretch of coast within which sediment movement is largely self-contained, bounded by headlands or deep water; it is treated as a closed system although in reality it is partly open.\n• Inputs: sediment from cliff erosion (e.g. Holderness till), rivers, offshore sources, and wind-blown sand; energy from waves, tides and wind.\n• Transfers/flows: longshore drift, onshore–offshore movement, transport by traction, saltation, suspension and solution.\n• Stores/sinks: beaches, spits (Spurn Head), sand dunes, offshore bars and banks, salt marsh (Humber Estuary).\n• Outputs: sediment lost to deep water or beyond the cell boundary.\n• Sediment budget: balance of inputs and outputs determines whether the coast advances or erodes.\n• Dynamic equilibrium and feedback: e.g. erosion builds beaches which then protect cliffs (negative feedback); human intervention in one place (groynes at Mappleton) alters supply down-drift.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Bangladesh has a coastline of low-lying deltaic land, much of it less than 5 m above sea level, and millions of people live on the coastal plain and in the Sundarbans region.\n\nExplain why some coastal communities face an increasing risk of flooding.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Physical factors: low-lying land and gentle offshore gradients; deltaic land subsides as sediment compacts.\n• Eustatic sea-level rise from thermal expansion and melting of land ice raises the base level for storm surges and high tides.\n• Storm surges from tropical cyclones (e.g. the funnel shape of the Bay of Bengal concentrates surge height); depressions in the North Sea in the UK (1953, 2013).\n• Possible increase in cyclone intensity with warmer sea surface temperatures.\n• Human factors: reduced sediment supply from dams upstream; groundwater abstraction increasing subsidence; removal of mangroves for shrimp farms reduces natural protection.\n• Rising population density and urbanisation on coasts increase exposure; poverty and weak infrastructure increase vulnerability.\n• Local factors: inadequate or failing embankments (polders).\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Old Harry Rocks, on the chalk headland at Handfast Point in Dorset, includes a series of caves, arches and stacks, with a wide wave-cut platform exposed at low tide.\n\nExplain how marine erosion processes create distinctive landforms on rocky coastlines.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8):\n• Erosion processes: hydraulic action (wave pressure compresses air in cracks), abrasion/corrasion, attrition and solution (corrosion, especially of chalk and limestone).\n• Destructive, high-energy waves with long fetch and strong backwash are most effective; wave refraction concentrates energy on headlands.\n• Wave-cut notch forms at the cliff base between high and low tide; the overhang collapses and the cliff retreats, leaving a wave-cut platform.\n• Erosion exploits lines of weakness (joints, faults, bedding planes): cracks widen into caves; caves on either side of a headland meet to form an arch; arch collapses to leave a stack; stack eroded to a stump.\n• Lithology and structure control rate and form: resistant chalk forms steep cliffs and stacks; dip of strata affects cliff profile.\n• Subaerial processes (weathering, mass movement) contribute to cliff retreat alongside marine erosion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Understanding addresses a narrow range of geographical ideas, which lack detail.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Understanding addresses a range of geographical ideas, which are not fully detailed and/or developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Understanding addresses a broad range of geographical ideas, which are detailed and fully developed.` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 10 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 10 shows characteristics of four glaciers.\n\nGlacier | Basal thermal regime | Mean surface gradient (°) | Mean ice thickness (m) | Mean surface velocity (m/yr)\nA (Alps) | Warm-based | 12 | 180 | 110\nB (Alps) | Warm-based | 6 | 250 | 75\nC (Svalbard) | Polythermal | 8 | 150 | 30\nD (Antarctica) | Cold-based | 10 | 200 | 4\n\nUsing Figure 10 and your own knowledge, assess the relative importance of factors that control the rate of glacier movement.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Movement processes: basal sliding, regelation creep, internal deformation; factors: gradient, thickness, temperature/thermal regime, meltwater, bedrock type, debris content.\nAO2:\n• Thermal regime appears most important: warm-based glaciers A and B move at 110 and 75 m/yr, compared with 4 m/yr for cold-based D, even though D is thicker than A and steeper than B.\n• Gradient matters within a regime: A (12°) moves faster than B (6°) even though B is thicker.\n• Thickness increases stress and internal deformation, but D at 200 m still moves slowly, so thickness alone does not explain velocity.\n• Polythermal C is intermediate (30 m/yr), suggesting meltwater availability at the bed is key.\n• Limitations: only four glaciers; mean values hide seasonal and spatial variation; bedrock and meltwater not shown; correlation is not causation.\n• Judgement: thermal regime (via meltwater and basal sliding) is the dominant control, with gradient and thickness modifying velocity.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 2.\nFigure 2 describes Imja Tsho, a glacial lake in the Khumbu region of Nepal, about 10 km up-valley from the Sherpa villages of Dingboche and Pangboche and the trekking route to Everest Base Camp. The lake grew from a few small ponds in the 1960s to about 1.3 km² by the 2010s, and is dammed by an ice-cored moraine. In 2016, an outlet channel with sluice gates was built, lowering the lake level by about 3 m (approximate figures).\n\nUsing Figure 2 and your own knowledge, assess the threats posed by glacial lake outburst floods (GLOFs) to people living in glaciated mountain regions.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• GLOFs occur when moraine or ice dams fail, through overtopping by waves from ice or rock avalanches, melting of ice cores, or piping; they release large volumes rapidly down-valley.\n• Climate change is increasing the number and size of glacial lakes in the Himalaya and Andes.\nAO2:\n• Threats in Figure 2: settlements, trekking lodges, trails, bridges and farmland down-valley at Dingboche and Pangboche; tourism economy reliant on the Everest route; HEP schemes and infrastructure further downstream.\n• Ice-cored moraine is vulnerable to melting, increasing risk over time.\n• Scale of threat depends on lake volume, dam stability, distance and valley shape, and population density; high-altitude valleys have small, scattered populations but few alternative livelihoods.\n• Management reduces the threat: lowering the lake by 3 m reduced the volume of water available; early warning systems; hazard mapping; but it may only partially reduce risk as the lake continues to grow.\n• Comparison with other hazards (avalanches, landslides) or other places (e.g. Peru, Lake Palcacocha) may contextualise.\n• Judgement: GLOFs are a growing, high-impact but low-frequency threat; significance depends on local vulnerability and management.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. The Lake District became a National Park in 1951 and a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017. It receives around 15 million visitors a year (approximate figure). Its relict glacial landscape includes corries, arêtes, glacial troughs and ribbon lakes, and is used for farming, forestry, water supply and tourism.\n\nWith reference to the Lake District, assess the extent to which human activity threatens relict glaciated landscapes.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Relict glaciated landscapes have environmental, cultural and economic value; threats can be natural (climate change, weathering, storms) or human (tourism, farming, development, water abstraction).\nAO2:\n• Tourism: footpath erosion on popular routes (e.g. Helvellyn, Scafell Pike), car parking and traffic congestion, litter, pressure on small settlements and housing (second homes).\n• Farming: sheep grazing has shaped the landscape but overgrazing limits woodland and biodiversity; conversely, its decline threatens cultural landscapes.\n• Water supply: reservoirs such as Thirlmere and Haweswater altered valleys and settlements.\n• Forestry: conifer plantations change landscape character.\n• Management reduces threats: National Park planning controls, footpath repair (e.g. Fix the Fells), sustainable transport schemes, rewilding projects.\n• Natural threats may be significant: extreme rainfall events (Storm Desmond, 2015) causing landslides and erosion.\n• Judgement: human activity is a significant but largely managed threat; the extent varies with location (honeypots vs remote valleys).\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Study Figure 9.\nFigure 9 describes a coastal protection scheme at Mappleton on the Holderness coast. In 1991, two rock groynes and a rock revetment were built to protect the village and the coastal road (B1242), at a cost of about £2 million. The table below shows mean annual cliff recession at three locations (data written for this practice question).\n\nLocation | 1980–1990 (m/yr) | 1995–2005 (m/yr)\nMappleton (protected) | 1.9 | 0.1\n1 km south of Mappleton | 1.8 | 3.6\n3 km south of Mappleton | 1.7 | 2.4\n\nUsing Figure 9 and your own knowledge, assess the costs and benefits of hard engineering approaches at Mappleton.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Hard engineering: groynes trap longshore drift to build beaches; rock revetments/rip-rap absorb wave energy; cost–benefit analysis compares the value of property protected with the cost of the scheme.\nAO2:\n• Benefits: recession at Mappleton fell from 1.9 to 0.1 m/yr, protecting about 50 homes (approximate) and the B1242 road linking villages; a wider beach built up between the groynes.\n• Costs: about £2 million capital cost plus maintenance; recession 1 km south doubled from 1.8 to 3.6 m/yr and increased at 3 km south (1.7 to 2.4 m/yr) because the groynes starve down-drift beaches of sediment (terminal groyne effect), affecting farmland and properties such as Cowden Farm.\n• Winners and losers: villagers and road users benefit; landowners down-drift lose land without compensation.\n• Wider sediment cell effects: reduced sediment supply could affect Spurn Head and the Humber.\n• Limitations of data: only three sites; other factors (storms, beach width) vary between periods.\n• Judgement: locally cost-effective, but with significant external costs; suggests hard engineering shifts rather than solves the problem.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Coastal vegetation includes sand-dune grasses such as marram, salt-marsh plants such as glasswort and cordgrass, and mangrove forests such as those in the Sundarbans of Bangladesh and India.\n\nWith reference to examples, assess the importance of vegetation in stabilising coastlines.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 3, AO2 9):\nAO1:\n• Vegetation stabilises sediment through roots, traps sediment, reduces wind speed at the surface, dissipates wave energy and adds organic matter; succession in psammoseres and haloseres.\nAO2:\n• Sand dunes: marram grass traps and binds sand, building dune ridges that act as a natural barrier (e.g. Studland, Sefton coast); dune stabilisation schemes use planting and fencing.\n• Salt marsh: halophytes trap silt, raising marsh level and reducing wave energy; protects estuaries such as the Humber and the Blackwater estuary; used in managed realignment.\n• Mangroves: reduce wave and surge energy during cyclones; their removal for shrimp farming in the Sundarbans has increased flood risk.\n• Limitations: vegetation cannot resist extreme storms (2013 surge breached Spurn and eroded dunes); trampling, grazing and development degrade it; sea-level rise may drown salt marsh (coastal squeeze).\n• Other factors such as geology, sediment supply and wave energy may be more important on high-energy rocky coasts.\n• Judgement: vegetation is very important on low-energy depositional coasts and can be a cost-effective soft engineering option, but less important on rocky, high-energy coasts.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Glaciated landscapes such as Alaska, the Alps and the Lake District support activities including oil extraction, tourism, farming, forestry and hydro-electric power, as well as fragile ecosystems and the cultural traditions of indigenous peoples.\n\nEvaluate the view that the economic value of glaciated landscapes is more important than their environmental value.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Economic value: tourism (skiing, trekking, cruises, e.g. Alaska and the Alps), mining and oil (Prudhoe Bay and the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, completed 1977, about 1,300 km long), HEP and water supply, farming and forestry.\n• Environmental value: biodiversity and fragile ecosystems (tundra, Arctic National Wildlife Refuge), role in the water and carbon cycles (permafrost carbon store), regulation of global climate via albedo.\n• Cultural value: indigenous peoples (Inuit, Gwich'in), spiritual and aesthetic value, World Heritage status.\nAO2:\n• For the view: economic activities generate revenue, jobs and taxes (e.g. Alaska Permanent Fund dividends from oil revenue); tourism sustains remote communities in the Alps.\n• Against the view: environmental value is global and long term, whereas many economic benefits go to TNCs and outsiders; pipeline spills and habitat disruption; tourism degrades the landscape that attracts visitors.\n• Economic and environmental values are interdependent: tourism depends on intact landscapes; glacier loss threatens ski resorts and HEP.\n• Values differ by player: governments, TNCs, environmental groups, indigenous groups; the view depends on scale and timescale.\n• Management approaches (protection vs sustainable use; ANWR debate) reflect how players weigh these values.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Approaches to managing glaciated landscapes range from strict protection, such as the Antarctic Treaty (1959) and its Environmental Protocol (1991), which bans mineral resource activities, to the economic development of areas such as the North Slope of Alaska.\n\nEvaluate the view that strict protection is the most effective way to manage glaciated landscapes.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Spectrum of management: total protection, sustainable management, exploitation; players include governments, international bodies (Antarctic Treaty system), TNCs, NGOs, indigenous groups.\n• Examples: Antarctica (no mining, limited scientific and regulated tourism, IAATO guidelines), Alaska (ANWR, Trans-Alaska Pipeline with elevated sections over permafrost), the Lake District National Park, Alpine conventions.\nAO2:\n• For the view: Antarctica has remained largely undamaged; ban on mining prevents irreversible damage to a fragile, slow-recovering environment; protection preserves scientific value.\n• Against: strict protection is possible only where few people live and there is limited economic pressure; in inhabited areas (Alps, Alaska) livelihoods depend on resource use and tourism; sustainable approaches (zoning, visitor management, pipeline design) can balance use and conservation.\n• Protection cannot address global threats: climate change affects even protected areas (Antarctic ice shelves, Alpine glaciers), so local protection may be ineffective without global mitigation.\n• Enforcement challenges: tourism growth in Antarctica; future renegotiation of the Protocol; political pressure to open ANWR to drilling.\n• Effectiveness depends on criteria (environmental, economic, social) and timescale.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Glaciated Landscapes and Change. Upland areas such as Snowdonia and the Cairngorms, and lowland areas such as the Vale of York and East Anglia, were all shaped by ice sheets and glaciers during the Pleistocene.\n\nEvaluate the view that erosional processes are more important than depositional processes in creating distinctive glaciated landscapes.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Erosional processes: plucking, abrasion, frost shattering; landforms at micro (striations), meso (roches moutonnées, crag and tail) and macro (corries, arêtes, pyramidal peaks, troughs, hanging valleys, truncated spurs) scales.\n• Depositional processes: lodgement and ablation till; landforms such as drumlins, moraines (terminal, lateral, medial, recessional), erratics, till plains; fluvioglacial landforms (eskers, kames, kettles, outwash plains).\nAO2:\n• For the view: upland landscapes (Snowdonia, Lake District) are dominated by erosional landforms; erosion produces the most dramatic large-scale relief; deposition depends on prior erosion to supply sediment.\n• Against: lowland landscapes (Vale of York moraines, drumlin fields in the Eden Valley, till plains of East Anglia and Holderness) are dominated by deposition, covering large areas.\n• Processes interact: erosion and deposition operate in the same glacier system at different locations (zones of erosion up-glacier, deposition near the snout); some landforms (crag and tail, drumlins) involve both.\n• Importance depends on scale, location, thermal regime (warm-based glaciers erode more), and periglacial and post-glacial modification.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement, e.g. that relative importance varies between upland and lowland landscapes.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Shoreline Management Plans (SMPs) in England and Wales set one of four policies for each stretch of coast: hold the line, advance the line, managed realignment or no active intervention. Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM) aims to manage whole coastlines and sediment cells rather than individual locations.\n\nEvaluate the view that hard engineering approaches to coastal management are no longer sustainable.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Hard engineering: sea walls, groynes, rock armour/rip-rap, revetments, offshore breakwaters; soft engineering: beach nourishment, cliff regrading and drainage, dune stabilisation; sustainable approaches: SMPs, ICZM, managed realignment.\n• Sustainability: economic (cost–benefit), social (community needs), environmental (sediment cells, habitats).\nAO2:\n• For the view: high capital and maintenance costs (sea walls over £5,000 per metre, approximate); interrupts sediment flow causing down-drift erosion (Mappleton); coastal squeeze of habitats; rising sea levels and stronger storms make holding the line increasingly expensive; SMPs shift many stretches to no active intervention (e.g. Happisburgh, Norfolk).\n• Against: hard engineering remains essential where high-value assets and dense populations are at risk (Thames Barrier, Dawlish railway sea wall rebuilt after 2014, Easington gas terminal); cost–benefit analysis may justify it; combined approaches (rock armour plus nourishment) can be sustainable.\n• Developing countries (Bangladesh embankments) may lack alternatives; in the Sundarbans a mix of embankments and mangrove restoration is used.\n• Sustainability depends on location, timescale and the players involved; ICZM integrates hard engineering where appropriate.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Rates of coastal recession vary greatly: the till cliffs of Holderness retreat on average by around 1.5–2 m per year, while the granite cliffs of Land's End in Cornwall retreat by only millimetres per year.\n\nEvaluate the view that geology is the most important factor in determining rates of coastal recession.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Geology: lithology (resistance of rock: granite, chalk, clay/till), structure (concordant/discordant, dip, joints and faults).\n• Other factors: wave energy and fetch, storm frequency, tidal range, sediment supply and beach width, subaerial weathering and mass movement, sea-level change, human activity (defences, dredging).\nAO2:\n• For the view: contrast between Holderness till and Land's End granite or Flamborough Head chalk shows lithology strongly controls rates; structure (Dorset coast) influences landforms and erosion.\n• Against: on Holderness, rates vary along the same lithology, due to beach width, ridges and lows (ords), and defences; long fetch and North Sea storms increase energy.\n• Human activity: groynes at Mappleton and sea walls at Hornsea increase down-drift recession; offshore dredging may reduce beach protection.\n• Climate and sea level: rising sea level and more frequent storms (2013–14) can cause rapid recession even on resistant coasts; wet winters increase slumping.\n• Sediment budgets and littoral cells determine whether beaches protect cliffs.\n• Factors interact: geology sets the potential rate, while other factors determine the actual rate at a given time and place.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. Global mean sea level is projected to rise by several tens of centimetres by 2100. Low-lying coasts such as the Sundarbans of Bangladesh and India, the Maldives and the Norfolk Broads face different combinations of sea-level rise, storm surges, erosion and subsidence.\n\nEvaluate the view that sea-level rise is the greatest threat to coastal communities.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Causes of sea-level rise: eustatic (thermal expansion, melting land ice), isostatic and tectonic changes; local subsidence.\n• Other threats: storm surges and tropical cyclones, coastal erosion, saltwater intrusion, human activity (mangrove clearance, dams reducing sediment).\nAO2:\n• For the view: sea-level rise is long-term, global and irreversible on human timescales; it amplifies all other threats (higher surge base levels, increased erosion, salinisation of soils and water in the Sundarbans); threatens the existence of low-lying states such as the Maldives (highest point about 5 m) and may create environmental refugees.\n• Against: storm surges cause the most immediate damage and deaths (Cyclone Sidr 2007 in Bangladesh; North Sea 1953); erosion threatens communities on Holderness regardless of sea level; local subsidence (deltas) may exceed eustatic rise; human factors such as poverty, governance and mangrove loss determine vulnerability.\n• The threat varies by location, development level and adaptive capacity: developed countries (Netherlands, UK) can defend or adapt; developing countries less so.\n• Threats interact: sea-level rise is a 'threat multiplier' rather than the sole threat.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` },
      { q: `Section B — Coastal Landscapes and Change. At Medmerry, West Sussex, the Environment Agency completed a managed realignment scheme in 2013, building new embankments inland and allowing the sea to flood farmland to create intertidal habitat. At Happisburgh, Norfolk, the SMP policy of no active intervention has led to the loss of homes on eroding cliffs.\n\nEvaluate the view that sustainable coastal management produces more winners than losers.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 5, AO2 15):\nAO1:\n• Sustainable approaches: SMPs (managed realignment, no active intervention), ICZM, soft engineering; cost–benefit analysis and the role of the Environment Agency, local councils and DEFRA.\n• Players: residents, landowners/farmers, businesses, conservation groups (RSPB), government, insurers.\nAO2:\n• Winners: at Medmerry, about 350 properties (approximate) and a road gained a higher standard of protection; new salt-marsh habitat supports birds and fisheries; lower long-term maintenance costs; tourism and recreation benefits; down-drift sediment supply maintained when cliffs erode naturally.\n• Losers: farmers lost agricultural land at Medmerry; at Happisburgh homeowners lost properties, often with limited compensation and falling property values; blight affects communities; local businesses suffer.\n• Distribution of winners and losers varies by scale: national taxpayers and the environment gain while small numbers of local people bear concentrated losses.\n• Adaptation measures (e.g. Pathfinder roll-back funding at Happisburgh) can reduce losses.\n• Timescales: short-term losses may give long-term gains as sea levels rise.\n• Conclusion weighs the evidence and reaches a substantiated judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and an unsupported or generic conclusion (AO2).\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence, and a conclusion with some evidence that is not balanced (AO2).\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding, which is mostly relevant and accurate (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce an interpretation that is partially balanced, with a conclusion supported by evidence that may not be fully balanced (AO2).\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout (AO1). Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent and balanced interpretation, and a conclusion that is supported by a balanced argument (AO2).` }
    ]
  },
  'T3': {
    name: 'The Water Cycle & Water Insecurity',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity. Study Figure 1, which shows estimated global freshwater stores: total freshwater 35.03 million km³, of which ice caps, glaciers and permanent snow 24.06 million km³, fresh groundwater 10.53 million km³, lakes, wetlands, rivers, soil moisture and atmosphere 0.44 million km³.\n\nCalculate the percentage of global freshwater stored as fresh groundwater. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (10.53 ÷ 35.03) × 100 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 30.1% (1)\nAward 2 marks for a correct answer of 30.1% even if working is not shown in full. Do not accept 30% (incorrect number of decimal places) for the second mark.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 2 (data written for this practice question). Figure 2 is a storm hydrograph for the River Kent at Sedgwick following a frontal storm. Peak rainfall occurred at 14:00 on 3 February. Discharge rose from a baseflow of 6 cumecs to a peak of 148 cumecs, which was recorded at 05:30 on 4 February.\n\nCalculate the lag time for this storm event. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working, e.g. time from peak rainfall (14:00, 3 February) to peak discharge (05:30, 4 February) = 10 hours to midnight + 5.5 hours (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 15.5 hours / 15 hours 30 minutes (1)` },
      { q: `Study Figure 3 (data written for this practice question). Figure 3 shows the combined volume of water stored in the six main reservoirs supplying a city in a semi-arid region, measured at the end of the wet season: 2014, 870 million m³; 2015, 690 million m³; 2016, 520 million m³; 2017, 340 million m³; 2018, 230 million m³.\n\nCalculate the percentage decrease in reservoir storage between 2014 and 2018. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (870 − 230) ÷ 870 × 100 or 640 ÷ 870 × 100 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 73.6% (1)` },
      { q: `Study Figure 4 (data written for this practice question). Figure 4 shows the annual water budget for a small upland drainage basin in north-west England: precipitation 1,240 mm; actual evapotranspiration 455 mm; change in storage over the year 0 mm.\n\nCalculate the runoff from the basin as a percentage of annual precipitation. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: runoff = 1,240 − 455 = 785 mm, then (785 ÷ 1,240) × 100 (1)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 63.3% (1)` },
      { q: `Study Figure 5 (data written for this practice question). Figure 5 shows the mean monthly discharge (m³/s) of a river draining a glaciated Alpine catchment: Jan 18, Feb 17, Mar 21, Apr 38, May 96, Jun 162, Jul 175, Aug 141, Sep 82, Oct 44, Nov 27, Dec 20. Mean monthly precipitation in the catchment is broadly similar throughout the year.\n\nSuggest one reason why the river regime peaks in June and July.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for development/application to Figure 5.\n• Rising temperatures in late spring and summer cause snowmelt and glacier ablation (1), releasing water stored as ice/snow during winter (1), so discharge rises from about 38 m³/s in April to a peak of 175 m³/s in July even though precipitation does not increase (1).\n• In winter precipitation falls as snow and is held in the cryosphere store (1), so it does not reach the channel until temperatures rise (1), delaying runoff until early summer (1).\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 6 (data written for this practice question). Figure 6 shows average domestic water use per person per day: United Kingdom 142 litres; United States 300 litres; India 90 litres; Ethiopia 20 litres.\n\nSuggest one reason why domestic water use per person varies between these countries.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for development.\n• Level of development/income (1): wealthier households in the USA and UK have piped supplies and more water-using appliances such as dishwashers, power showers and washing machines (1), and in the USA many homes water gardens and lawns or fill swimming pools (1).\n• Access to infrastructure (1): in Ethiopia many rural households have no piped supply and must collect water from wells or standpipes (1), so the effort and time involved limit use to essentials such as drinking and cooking (1).\n• Price of water (1): where water is cheap or unmetered there is little incentive to conserve (1), whereas metering/tariffs reduce use (1).\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 7. Figure 7 is a photograph of the lower part of a drainage basin on the edge of a large town. It shows a recently built housing estate with tarmac roads, roofs and car parks, connected to storm drains which discharge directly into a small river. Upstream of the town the land is pasture with scattered woodland.\n\nSuggest one way in which the land use shown could affect the flood risk downstream.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid point and up to 2 further marks for development.\n• Impermeable surfaces such as tarmac and roofs prevent infiltration (1), so most rainfall becomes rapid surface runoff (1), which shortens lag time and raises peak discharge, increasing flood risk downstream (1).\n• Storm drains carry water directly and quickly into the river (1), reducing the time water spends in stores (1), so a sharper, higher hydrograph peak is produced (1).\n• Removal of vegetation reduces interception and evapotranspiration (1), increasing the volume of water reaching the channel (1).\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 8. Figure 8 is a pair of maps showing typical rainfall anomalies in the Pacific basin during an El Niño event: much drier than average conditions over eastern Australia and Indonesia, and much wetter than average conditions over coastal Peru and Ecuador.\n\nExplain one reason why El Niño events can lead to drought in eastern Australia.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation.\n• During El Niño the trade winds weaken or reverse (1), so warm surface water in the western Pacific moves east towards South America (1). Sea surface temperatures off eastern Australia therefore fall and the area of rising, convectional air shifts east (1). Descending air and high pressure develop over eastern Australia, suppressing cloud formation and rainfall, leading to meteorological drought (1).\n• Reduced rainfall combined with high temperatures increases evapotranspiration (1), so soil moisture and river flow fall (1), turning meteorological drought into hydrological/agricultural drought (1).\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `You have carried out fieldwork to investigate whether there is a relationship between the percentage of urban land cover and lag time in eight small drainage basins following the same storm. Figure 9 (data written for this practice question) shows your results.\nBasin A: urban 4%, lag time 14.0 h, rank urban 8, rank lag 1, d² 49\nBasin B: urban 12%, lag time 11.5 h, rank urban 7, rank lag 3, d² 16\nBasin C: urban 18%, lag time 12.5 h, rank urban 6, rank lag 2, d² ?\nBasin D: urban 25%, lag time 9.0 h, rank urban 5, rank lag 4, d² 1\nBasin E: urban 33%, lag time 8.0 h, rank urban 4, rank lag 6, d² 4\nBasin F: urban 41%, lag time 8.5 h, rank urban 3, rank lag 5, d² ?\nBasin G: urban 52%, lag time 5.5 h, rank urban 2, rank lag 7, d² 25\nBasin H: urban 60%, lag time 4.0 h, rank urban 1, rank lag 8, d² 49\nFormula: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n). Critical values for n = 8: 0.738 (0.05 significance level) and 0.881 (0.01 significance level).\n\nCalculate the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (rs) for this data and state the significance of the result. You must show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for the missing d² values: Basin C = 16, Basin F = 4 (1)\n1 mark for Σd² = 164 (1)\n1 mark for correct substitution: rs = 1 − (6 × 164) ÷ (512 − 8) = 1 − 984 ÷ 504 (1)\n1 mark for rs = −0.95 (accept −0.952) AND a statement that this is a strong negative correlation which is significant at the 0.01 level / 99% confidence, so the null hypothesis can be rejected (1)` },
      { q: `Study Figure 10. Figure 10 is a photograph of an area of the Amazon basin in Rondônia, Brazil, where tropical rainforest has been cleared for cattle pasture. A small area of intact forest remains in the background.\n\nExplain one way in which deforestation affects flows in the drainage basin water cycle.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a way and up to 3 further marks for explanation.\n• Interception and evapotranspiration are reduced (1) because the multi-layered canopy is removed and replaced by grass (1); less water is returned to the atmosphere, so less is recycled as convectional rainfall over the region (1), potentially reducing precipitation further downwind and lengthening dry seasons (1).\n• Surface runoff increases (1) because raindrops hit bare or compacted ground directly and cattle trampling reduces infiltration capacity (1); water reaches channels more quickly (1), increasing peak discharge, soil erosion and sediment load (1).\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 11 (data written for this practice question). Figure 11 shows total renewable freshwater resources per person per year in five countries, 2000 and 2020.\nCountry P: 2,450 m³ (2000), 1,520 m³ (2020)\nCountry Q: 1,100 m³ (2000), 640 m³ (2020)\nCountry R: 6,800 m³ (2000), 5,900 m³ (2020)\nCountry S: 480 m³ (2000), 310 m³ (2020)\nCountry T: 1,850 m³ (2000), 1,790 m³ (2020)\nThresholds: below 1,700 m³ = water stress; below 1,000 m³ = water scarcity; below 500 m³ = absolute scarcity.\n\nAnalyse the changes in water availability shown in Figure 11.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each valid analytical point, up to 4 marks, using evidence from Figure 11.\n• Water availability per person fell in all five countries between 2000 and 2020 (1).\n• The scale of decline varies: Country P fell by 930 m³ (about 38%), whereas Country T fell by only 60 m³ (about 3%) (1).\n• Countries P and T have moved below the 1,700 m³ threshold into water stress; Country Q has moved from stress into scarcity (1).\n• Country S was already in absolute scarcity in 2000 and has fallen further, to 310 m³ (1).\n• Country R remains well above the stress threshold despite a decline of 900 m³ (1).\n• Inference: as renewable supply is broadly fixed, the declines suggest rapid population growth, especially in P, Q and S (1).\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `The Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam (GERD) on the Blue Nile began filling its reservoir in 2020. Egypt depends on the Nile for around 90% of its freshwater, and Sudan lies between Ethiopia and Egypt.\n\nExplain one reason why the management of transboundary rivers such as the Nile can lead to conflict.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation.\n• Upstream control of flow (1): a dam in Ethiopia allows the upstream state to control the timing and volume of water released downstream (1); Egypt fears reduced flow during reservoir filling or in drought years, threatening irrigation and drinking supplies (1); without a binding agreement on filling rates and releases, tension rises between the states (1).\n• Competing demands and legal claims (1): older agreements (1929, 1959) allocated most Nile water to Egypt and Sudan and excluded upstream states (1); Ethiopia argues it has a right to use the water for hydroelectric power and development (1), so there is no agreed framework acceptable to all riparian states (1).\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 12. Figure 12 is a systems diagram of the global hydrological cycle. It shows the main stores (oceans about 97% of all water; cryosphere about 2%; groundwater about 0.7%; surface water, soil moisture, biosphere and atmosphere each well under 0.1%) linked by flows of evaporation, condensation, precipitation, advection, surface runoff and groundwater flow, all driven by solar energy.\n\nExplain why the global hydrological cycle is described as a closed system.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• A closed system has inputs and outputs of energy but no gain or loss of matter; the total quantity of water on Earth (about 1,386 million km³) is effectively fixed.\n• Water is transferred between stores (oceans, cryosphere, groundwater, atmosphere, biosphere) by flows such as evaporation, precipitation and runoff.\n• The cycle is driven by solar energy (evaporation) and gravity (precipitation, runoff, percolation).\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• Figure 12 shows that the oceans dominate storage, so most evaporation and precipitation occur over the oceans; advection carries some moisture over land, where it is balanced by runoff back to the sea.\n• Water moves between stores at different rates, so residence times vary from about 9 days in the atmosphere to thousands of years in deep groundwater and ice sheets; the quantity changes between stores (e.g. more stored in ice during glacials) but not in total.\n• Energy enters as solar radiation and leaves as long-wave radiation, so energy, not water, crosses the system boundary.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 12 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 12 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 12 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 13 (data written for this practice question). Figure 13 shows storm hydrographs for two neighbouring drainage basins after the same 40 mm rainfall event. Basin X (steep, circular, underlain by impermeable granite with thin soils and moorland) has a lag time of 4 hours and a peak discharge of 95 cumecs. Basin Y (gentle slopes, elongated, underlain by permeable chalk with deep soils and mixed woodland) has a lag time of 18 hours and a peak discharge of 22 cumecs.\n\nExplain how physical factors have affected the shape of these storm hydrographs.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Geology: impermeable rock prevents percolation, so water moves quickly as overland flow and throughflow; permeable rock allows percolation and groundwater storage, releasing water slowly as baseflow.\n• Relief and basin shape: steep slopes speed runoff; in a circular basin water from all parts arrives at the gauging point at a similar time; an elongated basin spreads arrival.\n• Soils and vegetation: thin or saturated soils have low storage capacity; deep soils and woodland increase infiltration, interception and evapotranspiration.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• Basin X has a short lag time (4 hours) and high peak (95 cumecs) because granite and thin moorland soils limit infiltration, giving a steep rising limb.\n• Basin Y's longer lag (18 hours) and lower peak (22 cumecs) reflect chalk geology and woodland, which delay and reduce the volume of storm flow, giving a flatter hydrograph with a higher share of baseflow.\n• Shape and slope add to the contrast: water converges quickly in the steep, circular Basin X.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 13 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 13 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 13 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 14 (data written for this practice question). Figure 14 shows mean monthly discharge (m³/s) for two rivers.\nRiver A (temperate maritime basin, UK): Jan 62, Feb 58, Mar 47, Apr 33, May 24, Jun 17, Jul 14, Aug 16, Sep 22, Oct 38, Nov 51, Dec 60.\nRiver B (tropical wet–dry savanna basin, West Africa): Jan 9, Feb 6, Mar 5, Apr 7, May 15, Jun 48, Jul 160, Aug 310, Sep 285, Oct 120, Nov 35, Dec 15.\n\nExplain the differences between the regimes of these two rivers.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• A river regime is the annual variation in discharge, controlled mainly by climate (precipitation and temperature) and modified by geology, soils, vegetation and human activity.\n• In temperate maritime climates precipitation falls all year, but evapotranspiration is much higher in summer.\n• Tropical wet–dry climates have a short, intense rainy season linked to the seasonal shift of the ITCZ, and a long dry season with high evaporation.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• River A has a winter maximum (about 62 m³/s in January) and summer minimum (14 m³/s in July), because high summer evapotranspiration and soil moisture deficits reduce runoff, while winter soils are saturated.\n• River B has a far more extreme regime: from 5 m³/s in March to 310 m³/s in August, reflecting the arrival of the ITCZ and monsoonal rainfall; the peak lags the onset of rain as soils and stores first fill.\n• River B's very low dry-season flow suggests limited groundwater baseflow and high evaporation losses.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 14 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 14 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 14 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 15 (data written for this practice question). Figure 15 is a water budget graph for a location with a Mediterranean climate. Precipitation exceeds potential evapotranspiration from November to March (peak surplus in January). From April, potential evapotranspiration rises above precipitation; soil moisture is used up by June, and there is a large deficit from June to September. Soil moisture is recharged in October and November.\n\nExplain the changes in soil moisture shown in Figure 15.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• A water budget compares precipitation with evapotranspiration; when P > PEVT there is a surplus and soil moisture stores are full, with excess becoming runoff.\n• Soil moisture utilisation occurs when PEVT > P and plants draw on soil water; once the store is exhausted a deficit occurs.\n• Recharge occurs when P again exceeds PEVT and the soil store refills before surplus returns.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• The winter surplus (November–March) reflects frontal rainfall from mid-latitude depressions and low temperatures, so soils are at field capacity.\n• From April rising temperatures and long sunshine hours increase PEVT while rainfall declines under subtropical high pressure, so soil moisture is used up by June.\n• The June–September deficit means plants experience water stress and irrigation is needed; October–November rainfall recharges the soil before surplus returns.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 15 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 15 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 15 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `In 2017–18 Cape Town, South Africa, came close to 'Day Zero', when the city's taps would have been turned off. Rainfall in the catchments of its main supply dams was well below average for three consecutive winters (2015–2017), while the city's population had grown to over 4 million.\n\nExplain why both physical and human factors contributed to the water crisis in Cape Town.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Drought is an extended period of below-average precipitation leading to reduced river flow, reservoir and groundwater storage.\n• Physical factors: shifts in atmospheric circulation, such as a southward shift of the winter storm track, reduce frontal rainfall in Mediterranean-type climates; high temperatures increase evaporation.\n• Human factors: population growth and urbanisation increase demand; dependence on a single source; slow investment in new supply.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• Three successive dry winters meant the surface reservoirs that supply most of Cape Town's water were not refilled, so storage fell to very low levels.\n• Rapid population growth raised demand faster than supply infrastructure was expanded; heavy dependence on surface reservoirs, with limited groundwater or desalination capacity, made the city vulnerable.\n• Irrigated agriculture in the Western Cape competed for the same water; the crisis was averted only through severe restrictions (around 50 litres per person per day) and reduced agricultural allocations.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to the context to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to the context to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to the context to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `In 2019–20, after the hottest and driest year on record in Australia, bushfires burned millions of hectares of forest in New South Wales and Victoria.\n\nExplain the impacts of drought on ecosystems.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 6 marks)\n• Wetlands dry out as water tables fall and inflows decline, reducing habitat for birds, amphibians and fish; e.g. wetlands of the Murray–Darling basin during the Millennium Drought.\n• Reduced river flows raise water temperatures and lower dissolved oxygen, causing fish deaths and algal blooms.\n• Forests suffer water stress, reducing photosynthesis and net primary productivity; trees become more vulnerable to pests and die-back.\n• Drought increases the frequency and intensity of wildfires, e.g. in Australia and California, destroying habitats.\n• Long-term or repeated drought can shift ecosystems, e.g. forest replaced by savanna, and contribute to desertification in semi-arid areas such as the Sahel.\n• Ecosystem resilience varies: some species are adapted to drought, but the length and intensity of drought determine whether recovery occurs.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of impacts.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of how drought affects ecosystems.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with a detailed explanation of a range of ecosystem impacts.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 16. Figure 16 shows monthly rainfall for south-east Queensland, Australia, from 2010 to 2011, compared with the long-term average. Rainfall was well above average from September 2010 to January 2011, during a strong La Niña event. Major flooding affected Brisbane in January 2011.\n\nExplain how La Niña conditions can increase the risk of flooding in eastern Australia.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• La Niña is an intensification of normal Walker circulation: stronger trade winds push warm surface water west across the Pacific.\n• Warmer than usual sea surface temperatures off eastern Australia and Indonesia increase evaporation and produce strong rising air and low pressure.\n• This leads to heavy convectional and cyclonic rainfall, increasing the risk of floods.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• Figure 16 shows several months of above-average rainfall from September 2010, so soils became saturated and reservoirs filled before January 2011.\n• Once infiltration capacity was exceeded, further rainfall became rapid surface runoff, producing very high peak discharges and the Brisbane floods.\n• Floodplain urbanisation in Brisbane increased exposure, so the hazard became a disaster.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 16 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 16 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 16 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 17. Figure 17 is a line graph showing global freshwater withdrawals by sector, 1900–2010. Total withdrawals rise from under 600 km³ per year in 1900 to about 4,000 km³ per year by 2010. Agriculture accounts for about 70% of withdrawals throughout; industrial and municipal withdrawals rise most rapidly after 1950.\n\nExplain why global demand for water has increased.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Population growth, from under 2 billion in 1900 to about 7 billion in 2010, increases demand for drinking water, sanitation and food.\n• Rising incomes and changing diets (more meat and dairy) increase the water footprint of food.\n• Industrialisation and energy production (cooling water for thermal power stations) raise industrial demand.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• Figure 17 shows agriculture remains the largest user; the Green Revolution expanded irrigation, e.g. in India and China, raising withdrawals.\n• The rapid post-1950 rise in industrial and municipal use links to urbanisation and economic development in emerging economies.\n• Demand has risen faster than population because per capita use rises with development.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 17 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 17 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 17 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `Explain how climate change is affecting stores in the hydrological cycle.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 6 marks)\n• Cryosphere: rising temperatures cause glaciers and ice sheets (Greenland, West Antarctica) to lose mass and reduce snow cover, transferring water to the oceans.\n• Oceans: the ocean store increases in volume through meltwater and thermal expansion, contributing to sea level rise.\n• Atmosphere: warmer air holds about 7% more water vapour per 1 °C of warming, increasing the atmospheric store and the intensity of precipitation.\n• Soil moisture: higher evapotranspiration reduces soil moisture in many mid-latitude and subtropical regions, increasing drought risk.\n• Permafrost thaw releases stored water and changes drainage in Arctic regions.\n• Groundwater recharge may decrease where rainfall falls in more intense bursts and runs off rather than infiltrating.\n• Glacier-fed rivers (e.g. in the Himalayas) may see higher flows in the short term, then lower flows as glaciers shrink.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of changes to stores.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of changes to stores.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, explaining changes to a range of stores.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 18. Figure 18 is a map showing the change in water level in the High Plains (Ogallala) Aquifer, USA, since the 1950s. Large areas of the southern High Plains in Texas and western Kansas show declines of more than 30 m, while parts of Nebraska show little change or small rises.\n\nExplain the consequences of over-abstraction of groundwater from aquifers such as the Ogallala.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Over-abstraction occurs when pumping exceeds the natural rate of recharge, so the water table falls.\n• Consequences include higher pumping costs, wells running dry, land subsidence, reduced baseflow to rivers and wetlands, and saltwater intrusion in coastal aquifers.\n• Fossil water in deep aquifers may take thousands of years to recharge, so depletion is effectively permanent.\nAO2 (3 marks)\n• Figure 18 shows the largest declines in Texas and Kansas, where centre-pivot irrigation of crops such as maize and cotton draws heavily on the aquifer in a semi-arid climate with low recharge.\n• As levels fall, farmers face rising energy costs, some switch to dryland farming, and rural economies and communities that depend on irrigated farming decline.\n• Nebraska shows smaller changes because of higher recharge and stricter local management.\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies limited knowledge to Figure 18 to find few connections.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to Figure 18 to find some logical connections.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to Figure 18 to find fully logical and relevant connections.` },
      { q: `In winter 2013–14 the Somerset Levels, UK, were flooded for several weeks after the wettest January in England and Wales since records began. In December 2015 Storm Desmond produced record 24-hour rainfall in Cumbria, flooding Carlisle and Keswick.\n\nExplain how physical processes can lead to river flooding.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8 marks)\n• Intense or prolonged precipitation: frontal depressions and atmospheric rivers (Storm Desmond) or long sequences of storms (Somerset 2013–14) deliver more water than the basin can store.\n• Antecedent conditions: saturated soils and high water tables mean infiltration capacity is exceeded quickly, generating saturated overland flow.\n• Snowmelt, sometimes combined with rain on frozen ground, produces rapid runoff.\n• Basin characteristics: steep relief, impermeable geology and thin soils (Cumbrian fells) shorten lag time and raise peak discharge; flat, low-lying land with high tides limits drainage (Somerset Levels).\n• Monsoon rainfall and tropical cyclones produce very large flood events, e.g. Bangladesh.\n• Flash floods result from intense convectional storms where infiltration-excess overland flow dominates.\n• Physical processes interact: e.g. high tides and storm surges at river mouths prevent floodwater from draining.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of physical processes, with few connections between them.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of how physical processes lead to flooding, partly developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. A detailed, well-developed explanation of how a range of physical processes interact to cause flooding, supported by examples.` },
      { q: `In the Pickering catchment, North Yorkshire, trees have been planted and leaky woody dams built upstream to reduce flood risk, while elsewhere in the UK new housing estates have been built on greenfield land.\n\nExplain how changes in land use can affect stores and flows in a drainage basin.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8 marks)\n• Deforestation reduces interception, infiltration and evapotranspiration and increases surface runoff and soil erosion, e.g. in the Amazon or Himalayan foothills.\n• Afforestation has the opposite effect, increasing interception storage and lag time; it is used in natural flood management, e.g. Pickering, North Yorkshire.\n• Urbanisation creates impermeable surfaces and storm drains, increasing runoff and peak discharge and reducing groundwater recharge.\n• Agriculture: ploughing, compaction by machinery and livestock, and land drainage increase runoff; irrigation transfers water from rivers and aquifers to soils and the atmosphere.\n• Reservoirs create large new surface stores, increase evaporation and regulate downstream flow.\n• Groundwater abstraction reduces aquifer storage and baseflow.\n• Effects depend on scale and on interactions with basin characteristics such as geology and relief.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of the effects of land use change.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of how land use change affects stores and flows.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. A detailed explanation of how a range of land use changes affect both stores and flows.` },
      { q: `Parts of the Lake District receive over 2,000 mm of precipitation a year, mostly from Atlantic depressions forced over the fells, while eastern England receives under 700 mm and many of its heaviest falls come from summer thunderstorms.\n\nExplain why the causes of precipitation lead to variations in drainage basin inputs.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8 marks)\n• Precipitation is the main input to the drainage basin; its type, amount, intensity, seasonality and variability all affect basin flows.\n• Convectional precipitation: intense heating causes rising air, giving short, very intense storms (e.g. tropical afternoon storms, UK summer thunderstorms) that can exceed infiltration capacity and generate flash floods.\n• Frontal (cyclonic) precipitation: warm air rising over cold air at fronts in mid-latitude depressions gives prolonged, moderate rainfall, which saturates soils over large areas, e.g. UK winter floods.\n• Orographic (relief) precipitation: moist air forced over uplands, giving high totals on windward slopes (e.g. over 2,000 mm a year in parts of the Lake District) and rain shadows to leeward.\n• Seasonal shifts of pressure belts and the ITCZ give monsoon regimes, e.g. South Asia.\n• Snowfall delays inputs until melt.\n• Variability between years, linked to ENSO, alters basin inputs.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of the causes of precipitation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation, partly linked to variations in inputs.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. A detailed explanation of how different causes of precipitation produce variation in the amount, intensity and timing of inputs.` },
      { q: `During the Millennium Drought (about 1997–2009) in south-east Australia, river flows in the Murray–Darling basin fell to record lows while irrigation for crops such as rice and cotton continued.\n\nExplain why drought can be caused by both meteorological and human factors.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8 marks)\n• Meteorological drought results from a lack of precipitation, often linked to persistent high pressure (blocking anticyclones), shifts in the ITCZ or storm tracks, and ENSO cycles (e.g. El Niño and drought in Australia and Indonesia).\n• Meteorological drought develops into agricultural drought (soil moisture deficit), hydrological drought (low river flows, reservoir and groundwater levels) and socio-economic drought.\n• Human factors intensify hydrological drought: over-abstraction from rivers and aquifers, e.g. the Murray–Darling basin during the Millennium Drought (about 1997–2009).\n• Deforestation and overgrazing reduce evapotranspiration and local moisture recycling and degrade soils, increasing desertification risk, e.g. in the Sahel.\n• Population growth and irrigation demand mean a smaller fall in rainfall causes water shortages.\n• Human-induced climate change increases temperatures and evapotranspiration, making droughts more intense.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of causes.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of both meteorological and human causes, with some links between them.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. A detailed explanation of both meteorological and human causes and how they interact, supported by examples.` },
      { q: `The Aral Sea in Central Asia was once the fourth largest lake in the world. Since the 1960s, diversion of the Amu Darya and Syr Darya rivers to irrigate cotton has caused it to shrink to a fraction of its former area.\n\nExplain why water insecurity has economic and environmental consequences.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8 marks)\n• Water insecurity occurs when there is not enough clean water of adequate quality to meet human and environmental needs; it can be physical or economic scarcity.\n• Economic consequences: reduced agricultural output and food insecurity, since irrigated agriculture produces a large share of global food; reduced industrial production; reduced hydroelectric power output during droughts; higher costs of water treatment and supply.\n• Health consequences reduce productivity, e.g. waterborne diseases such as cholera, and time spent collecting water, especially by women and girls, reduces school attendance and income.\n• Environmental consequences: over-abstraction lowers water tables and dries wetlands; reduced river flows harm aquatic ecosystems; the Aral Sea shrank dramatically after rivers were diverted for cotton irrigation, leaving salt- and dust-laden land.\n• Salinisation of soils where poorly drained irrigation raises water tables.\n• Pollution from industry, agriculture and untreated sewage degrades water quality.\n• Economic and environmental consequences interact, e.g. the collapse of the Aral Sea fishing industry.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of consequences.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of both economic and environmental consequences.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. A detailed explanation of both economic and environmental consequences, with links between them, supported by examples.` },
      { q: `Explain how the drainage basin functions as an open system.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content (AO1 8 marks)\n• An open system has inputs and outputs of both energy and matter that cross the system boundary, the watershed.\n• Inputs: precipitation (rain, snow, hail), whose amount, intensity and type vary.\n• Stores: interception (vegetation), surface storage (puddles, lakes), soil moisture, groundwater (aquifers) and channel storage.\n• Flows/transfers: infiltration, throughflow, percolation, overland flow (infiltration-excess and saturated), groundwater flow and channel flow.\n• Outputs: evaporation, transpiration (together evapotranspiration) and river discharge to the sea; some groundwater may leave the basin.\n• The water balance P = Q + E ± ΔS links inputs, outputs and storage change.\n• Physical factors (geology, soils, relief, vegetation) and human activity (urbanisation, abstraction, reservoirs) control how quickly water moves through the system.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Limited explanation of the system.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Some explanation of inputs, stores, flows and outputs.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. A detailed explanation of how inputs, stores, flows and outputs interact, with clear understanding of why the basin is an open system.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 19. Figure 19 is a map of China's South–North Water Transfer Project. It shows the Eastern Route, which takes water from the lower Yangtze northwards, partly along the Grand Canal, and the Central Route, which takes water from the Danjiangkou Reservoir on the Han River (a tributary of the Yangtze) about 1,400 km north to Beijing and Tianjin. Both routes have been operating since 2013–14. The Western Route is still at the planning stage. The North China Plain has about a third of China's population but well under a fifth of its water resources.\n\nAssess the costs and benefits of large-scale water transfer schemes such as this one.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Water transfers move water from areas of surplus to areas of deficit through canals, tunnels and pipelines; they are a hard engineering approach to increasing supply.\n• Examples include the South–North Water Transfer Project, the Colorado River aqueducts supplying southern California, and the Lesotho Highlands Water Project.\n• Costs include very high capital costs, energy for pumping, displacement of people, ecological damage and potential political tension.\nAO2 (9 marks)\n• Benefits: secure supply for megacities such as Beijing, reducing over-abstraction of groundwater on the North China Plain and allowing aquifers to recover; supports industry and economic growth.\n• Costs: about 330,000 people were relocated when the Danjiangkou Dam was raised; reduced flow in the Han River downstream affects water quality, navigation and ecosystems.\n• Eastern Route water required costly treatment because of industrial pollution along the Grand Canal.\n• Transfers address supply but not demand; they may encourage continued inefficient use in receiving areas.\n• Costs and benefits are distributed unequally: donor regions bear environmental and social costs while receiving cities gain the benefits.\n• Climate change may reduce flows in donor basins, threatening long-term reliability.\n• Balanced judgement: the scheme brings major short-term benefits for water security in northern China, but its sustainability depends on demand management and pollution control.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited relevance and/or support. Offers a judgement unsupported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced or incomplete judgement with limited coherence.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement based on a reasoned argument, though not fully balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent interpretation supported by evidence. Offers a rational, balanced judgement drawing on a full range of evidence.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 20. Figure 20 shows annual rainfall in the Murray–Darling basin, Australia, compared with the long-term average, and the volume of water diverted for irrigation, from about 1995 to 2012. Rainfall was below average in most years from 1997 to 2009 (the Millennium Drought). Irrigation diversions remained high in the early years of the drought before falling sharply after 2006. Water reaching the Murray mouth fell to almost nothing in several years.\n\nAssess the relative importance of physical and human factors in causing the hydrological drought shown in Figure 20.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Hydrological drought is a sustained reduction in river flow, reservoir and groundwater storage.\n• Physical causes: reduced rainfall linked to ENSO and Indian Ocean Dipole patterns, high temperatures and evaporation.\n• Human causes: abstraction for irrigation, river regulation by dams and weirs, land use change.\nAO2 (9 marks)\n• Figure 20 shows rainfall below average for about a decade, so the physical trigger was a prolonged meteorological drought.\n• However, high irrigation diversions in the early drought years removed a large share of the remaining flow, so human use turned a dry period into a severe hydrological drought; flow at the Murray mouth almost ceased.\n• Before the drought the basin was already heavily allocated, so reduced rainfall had a greater effect on river flows.\n• Climate change is likely to have increased temperatures and evaporation, blurring the distinction between physical and human causes.\n• The fall in diversions after 2006 shows a human response to physical scarcity; the 2012 Murray–Darling Basin Plan aimed to return water to the environment.\n• Judgement: physical factors triggered the drought, but human factors determined its severity and its impact on the river.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited relevance and/or support. Offers a judgement unsupported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced or incomplete judgement with limited coherence.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement based on a reasoned argument, though not fully balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent interpretation supported by evidence. Offers a rational, balanced judgement drawing on a full range of evidence.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 21. Figure 21 is a world map showing projected changes in annual precipitation by 2081–2100 under a high-emissions scenario. High latitudes and parts of the tropics show increases of more than 20%, while the Mediterranean, southern Africa, south-west Australia and Central America show decreases of 10–30%. Stippling shows that confidence is low over many tropical land areas.\n\nAssess the extent to which climate change will reduce the reliability of the hydrological cycle.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• Rising temperatures intensify the hydrological cycle: more evaporation, more atmospheric water vapour and more intense precipitation.\n• Changes include shifts in precipitation patterns, reduced snow and glacier storage, changes in river regimes and more frequent extremes (droughts and floods).\n• Projections carry uncertainty from emissions scenarios, climate models and natural variability such as ENSO.\nAO2 (9 marks)\n• Figure 21 suggests a 'wet gets wetter, dry gets drier' pattern; reduced precipitation in the Mediterranean and southern Africa will make supply less reliable in already water-stressed regions.\n• Increases at high latitudes may bring more frequent flooding rather than improved reliability, since rain falls in more intense bursts.\n• Loss of glacier and snow stores (Himalayas, Andes, Alps) will reduce dry-season flows in the longer term, reducing reliability for hundreds of millions of people.\n• Low confidence over many tropical areas means the extent of change is uncertain; natural variability such as ENSO may dominate in the short term.\n• Reliability also depends on human factors: storage, management and demand.\n• Judgement: climate change will significantly reduce reliability in some regions, especially subtropical and glacier-fed basins, but the scale is uneven and uncertain.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited relevance and/or support. Offers a judgement unsupported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced or incomplete judgement with limited coherence.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement based on a reasoned argument, though not fully balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent interpretation supported by evidence. Offers a rational, balanced judgement drawing on a full range of evidence.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 22. Figure 22 is a diagram of an Integrated Water Resource Management (IWRM) approach for a river basin. It shows a basin authority linking farmers, cities, industry, hydroelectric power producers and conservation groups. Measures shown include water metering, allocation caps for irrigators, wetland restoration, wastewater recycling and stakeholder consultation.\n\nAssess the effectiveness of integrated approaches to managing water supply.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• IWRM coordinates the management of water, land and related resources across a whole basin to balance economic, social and environmental needs.\n• It typically combines demand management (metering, pricing, efficient irrigation, recycling) with protection of ecosystems and the involvement of stakeholders.\n• Examples: the Murray–Darling Basin Plan (2012), the EU Water Framework Directive, the Mekong River Commission.\nAO2 (9 marks)\n• Figure 22 shows a wide range of stakeholders; involving them can reduce conflict and give long-term, more sustainable outcomes than single large projects.\n• Allocation caps and water trading in the Murray–Darling basin returned water to the environment, but faced strong opposition from farming communities and evidence of non-compliance.\n• Metering and recycling reduce demand: e.g. Singapore's NEWater recycling and demand management.\n• Effectiveness depends on governance, funding, data and enforcement; it is harder to achieve in poorer countries or where basins cross national borders.\n• Integrated approaches are slow to deliver results compared with engineering projects, and may not cope with severe drought on their own.\n• Judgement: integrated approaches are effective in the long term, especially where strong institutions exist, but success is uneven and they often need to be combined with some new supply.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited relevance and/or support. Offers a judgement unsupported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced or incomplete judgement with limited coherence.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement based on a reasoned argument, though not fully balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent interpretation supported by evidence. Offers a rational, balanced judgement drawing on a full range of evidence.` },
      { q: `Study Figure 23 (data written for this practice question). Figure 23 shows the Southern Oscillation Index (SOI) and annual rainfall anomalies for a station in eastern Australia, 2005–2016. Strongly negative SOI (El Niño) years 2006, 2009 and 2015 had rainfall 25–40% below average. Strongly positive SOI (La Niña) years 2010 and 2011 had rainfall 45–60% above average. In other years rainfall was within 15% of the average.\n\nAssess the importance of the El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO) in causing hydrological extremes.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (3 marks)\n• ENSO is a natural oscillation in Pacific ocean–atmosphere circulation (Walker circulation) between El Niño, neutral and La Niña phases, typically every 2–7 years.\n• El Niño is associated with drought in Australia, Indonesia and southern Africa and flooding in Peru and Ecuador; La Niña broadly reverses the pattern.\n• Other causes of extremes include persistent blocking anticyclones, monsoon variability, other ocean–atmosphere patterns (e.g. Indian Ocean Dipole) and human factors.\nAO2 (9 marks)\n• Figure 23 shows a clear association: El Niño years had rainfall 25–40% below average and La Niña years 45–60% above, so ENSO is a major driver of both deficits and surpluses at this station.\n• The 2010–11 La Niña coincided with the Queensland floods, while El Niño years link to drought and bushfire risk.\n• However, the data covers one station and 12 years only; correlation does not prove causation.\n• Many extremes are unrelated to ENSO, e.g. UK floods linked to the jet stream and atmospheric rivers, or the European heat and drought of 2018.\n• Human factors (abstraction, land use, floodplain development) determine whether a hydrological extreme becomes a disaster.\n• Judgement: ENSO is very important in the Pacific basin and some teleconnected regions, but less important globally than a combination of other atmospheric and human factors.\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited relevance and/or support. Offers a judgement unsupported by evidence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced or incomplete judgement with limited coherence.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement based on a reasoned argument, though not fully balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent interpretation supported by evidence. Offers a rational, balanced judgement drawing on a full range of evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nAround 2 billion people lack safely managed drinking water. Some live in arid regions such as the Middle East, others in places with ample rainfall but little infrastructure, such as parts of sub-Saharan Africa.\n\nWith reference to named examples, evaluate the view that physical factors are more important than human factors in causing water insecurity.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Water insecurity: lack of reliable access to sufficient safe water; physical scarcity (demand exceeds available supply) and economic scarcity (lack of investment or capacity to access water).\n• Physical factors: climate (low and variable rainfall, high evaporation), drought, salt-water incursion, geology and groundwater availability.\n• Human factors: population growth, over-abstraction, pollution, poverty and lack of infrastructure, governance and pricing.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• Physical factors are important in arid and semi-arid regions such as the Middle East and North Africa, where renewable supplies are low; drought linked to ENSO or blocking highs can trigger crises.\n• However, much of sub-Saharan Africa faces economic rather than physical scarcity: water exists but there is a lack of wells, pipes and treatment.\n• Human over-abstraction and diversion have caused insecurity where physical supply was once adequate: the Aral Sea, the Ogallala Aquifer, the Colorado River.\n• Pollution (e.g. the Ganges, industrial pollution in China) reduces the usable supply, so quality as well as quantity causes insecurity.\n• Human-induced climate change blurs the distinction, intensifying physical drought.\n• Cape Town 2017–18 combined a physical drought with population growth and slow infrastructure investment.\n• Conclusion: physical factors set the background level of risk, but human factors usually determine whether people become water insecure; the balance varies with level of development and location.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nIsrael now obtains much of its domestic water from large coastal desalination plants, while Singapore combines imported water, reservoirs, desalination and recycled NEWater in its "Four National Taps" strategy.\n\nUsing named examples, evaluate the view that large-scale hard engineering projects are the most effective way of increasing water security.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Hard engineering approaches: mega-dams and reservoirs (Three Gorges Dam, GERD), inter-basin water transfers (South–North Water Transfer Project), large desalination plants (e.g. in Israel and the Gulf states).\n• Sustainable approaches: demand management (metering, pricing), water conservation, recycling (Singapore NEWater), rainwater harvesting, efficient irrigation (drip irrigation), IWRM.\n• Water security means reliable access to enough safe water at an affordable cost.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• Hard engineering can deliver large volumes quickly and provide storage against seasonal and inter-annual variability; desalination supplies a large share of Israel's domestic water.\n• But costs are high: capital and energy costs (desalination is energy-intensive and produces brine), displacement of people, loss of ecosystems, sediment trapping and evaporation from reservoirs.\n• Large projects can create transboundary tension, e.g. GERD and Egypt.\n• Hard engineering often benefits cities and industry rather than the poorest rural communities, who may gain more from small-scale schemes such as wells, rainwater harvesting and local NGO projects (e.g. WaterAid).\n• Demand management and recycling deal with the causes of insecurity and are more sustainable; but they may not be enough in fast-growing, arid regions.\n• Most effective strategies combine approaches, e.g. Singapore's 'Four National Taps' (local catchment, imported water, NEWater, desalination).\n• Conclusion: hard engineering can be effective in the short term and in wealthy states, but is rarely the most effective approach on its own; sustainability and equity favour integrated approaches.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nThe Indus Waters Treaty (1960) divided the rivers of the Indus basin between India and Pakistan. The Mekong River Commission was set up in 1995 by four downstream states, but China, where the river rises, is not a full member.\n\nWith reference to named transboundary river basins, evaluate the view that international agreements have been successful in managing transboundary water resources.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Around 260 river basins are shared by two or more countries; transboundary water can be a source of conflict or cooperation.\n• Agreements include the Indus Waters Treaty (1960, India and Pakistan), the Mekong River Commission (1995), the Nile Basin Initiative (1999), the Colorado River Compact (1922) and the US–Mexico treaty of 1944, the UN Watercourses Convention (in force 2014) and the Helsinki Rules.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• The Indus Waters Treaty survived several wars between India and Pakistan for over 60 years, suggesting agreements can succeed; however, India announced in 2025 that it was holding the treaty in abeyance, showing their vulnerability to wider political tensions.\n• The Mekong River Commission promotes data sharing, but China, the upstream state, is not a full member, and dams in China and Laos have altered flows and sediment loads in Cambodia and Vietnam.\n• The Nile: earlier agreements favoured Egypt and Sudan; the Nile Basin Initiative has not produced a binding agreement, and GERD negotiations stalled.\n• The Colorado River is over-allocated because the 1922 Compact was based on unusually wet years; little water now reaches the delta in Mexico.\n• Many agreements lack enforcement mechanisms, ignore groundwater and do not account for climate change.\n• Conclusion: agreements have often prevented open conflict and encouraged cooperation, but have been less successful in achieving fair or sustainable allocation, especially where upstream states are powerful.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nSince the 1960s the rivers feeding the Aral Sea have been diverted for irrigation, while glaciers in the Himalayas and Andes that feed major rivers have lost a large share of their mass.\n\nWith reference to named drainage basins, evaluate the view that human activity has had a greater impact than climate change on drainage basin stores and flows.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Drainage basin stores (interception, soil moisture, groundwater, surface and channel storage) and flows (infiltration, throughflow, overland flow, evapotranspiration, channel flow).\n• Direct human impacts: deforestation, urbanisation, agriculture and land drainage, reservoirs, groundwater abstraction.\n• Climate change impacts: changing precipitation totals and intensity, higher evapotranspiration, reduced snow and glacier storage, changing regimes.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• Human impacts can be large and rapid at a local to regional scale: Amazon deforestation reduces moisture recycling; urbanisation shortens lag times; over-abstraction lowers water tables (Ogallala) and has effectively removed the Colorado and Aral Sea inflows.\n• Reservoirs and transfers dramatically alter river regimes, e.g. the Nile below the Aswan High Dam.\n• Climate change acts at a global scale and its effects are growing: glacier retreat in the Himalayas and Andes changes regimes for whole basins; more intense precipitation increases flood risk (e.g. UK winter storms).\n• Climate change is itself a human impact, so the distinction is partly artificial.\n• The relative importance varies: in many heavily managed basins direct human activity dominates today, while in glacier-fed or high-latitude basins climate change dominates; over longer time scales climate change is likely to become more significant.\n• Conclusion: a substantiated judgement on the balance, recognising variation in scale and time.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nIn 2022 monsoon floods in Pakistan affected over 30 million people. In 2011 drought in the Horn of Africa contributed to famine in parts of Somalia.\n\nWith reference to named examples, evaluate the view that the impacts of drought are more serious than the impacts of flooding.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Drought: a slow-onset hazard lasting months to years, with meteorological, agricultural, hydrological and socio-economic stages.\n• Flooding: usually a rapid-onset hazard (flash floods, river floods), though some floods last weeks (Somerset Levels 2014, Pakistan 2022).\n• Impacts are economic, social and environmental, and vary with level of development and vulnerability.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• Drought impacts are widespread and long-lasting: crop failure, food insecurity and famine (e.g. the Horn of Africa, 2011), loss of livestock, wildfire, migration; the Millennium Drought cut agricultural output in the Murray–Darling basin.\n• Drought is hard to define and to respond to quickly, and recovery of groundwater and ecosystems is slow.\n• Flooding causes sudden loss of life and damage to property and infrastructure, e.g. Pakistan 2022 affected over 30 million people; UK floods (Storm Desmond) caused damage costing billions of pounds; waterborne disease follows.\n• Floods also bring benefits: they recharge groundwater, deposit fertile silt and sustain wetlands, as with the annual monsoon floods in Bangladesh.\n• Seriousness depends on scale, duration and vulnerability; in developed countries flood damage costs are often higher, while in developing countries drought may cause more deaths and longer-term hardship.\n• Climate change is increasing both hazards, and they can occur in succession (e.g. Queensland: Millennium Drought followed by 2010–11 floods).\n• Conclusion: a judgement on whether drought impacts are generally more serious, recognising the variation by place, time scale and type of impact.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nIn Kenya, community groups supported by NGOs have built sand dams on seasonal rivers to store water in riverbed sediment. Meanwhile, megacities such as Lagos and Delhi struggle to supply piped water to rapidly growing populations.\n\nUsing named examples, evaluate the view that sustainable approaches offer the best solution to water insecurity in developing and emerging countries.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Sustainable approaches: water conservation, recycling and grey water, rainwater harvesting, efficient (drip) irrigation, small-scale community schemes (wells, hand pumps, sand dams), catchment protection, IWRM.\n• Alternatives: large dams and transfers, desalination, groundwater development.\n• Water insecurity in developing countries is often linked to economic scarcity, poverty and lack of infrastructure.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• Small-scale, low-cost schemes (e.g. NGO sand dams in Kenya, rainwater harvesting in Rajasthan) are affordable, use appropriate technology and involve local communities, so they are maintained.\n• Drip irrigation and better soil management reduce agricultural demand, the largest user.\n• However, small schemes cannot supply rapidly growing megacities such as Lagos or Delhi; large-scale infrastructure and treatment are needed.\n• Emerging economies such as China and India have used large dams and transfers to support growth, though at environmental and social cost.\n• Sustainable approaches depend on governance, funding and education; corruption or weak institutions can undermine them.\n• Climate change may reduce the reliability of local sources, requiring storage and diversification.\n• Conclusion: sustainable approaches are often the best solution for rural communities and for long-term security, but most countries require a combination of approaches suited to their level of development.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` },
      { q: `Section C — The Water Cycle and Water Insecurity.\n\nThe Indus, Ganges and Brahmaputra rivers depend partly on meltwater from Himalayan glaciers and are shared by several countries, including China, India, Pakistan and Bangladesh.\n\nWith reference to named examples, evaluate the view that climate change will be the main cause of water conflicts in the future.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (5 marks)\n• Water conflicts range from local disputes between users (farmers, cities, industry, environment) to tensions between states sharing rivers and aquifers.\n• Climate change affects supply through changing precipitation, more frequent drought, glacier loss and higher evaporation.\n• Other causes: population growth, economic development and rising demand, upstream dam building, pollution, weak governance and historical allocations.\nAO2 (15 marks)\n• Climate change is likely to reduce supply in already stressed regions (Mediterranean, Middle East, southern Africa) and reduce glacier-fed dry-season flows in the Indus, Ganges and Brahmaputra basins, increasing competition.\n• Drought may have contributed to tensions in some regions, e.g. drought in Syria (2006–10) is argued by some to have contributed to unrest, though this is disputed.\n• However, many current disputes stem from development decisions rather than climate: GERD on the Nile, Chinese and Laotian dams on the Mekong, over-allocation of the Colorado.\n• Population growth and rising demand increase pressure regardless of climate change.\n• Most transboundary interactions are cooperative; strong institutions and treaties can reduce the risk of conflict even as supply falls.\n• Conclusion: climate change will act as a 'threat multiplier', increasing the risk of conflict, but demand, development and governance are likely to remain at least as important.\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence. Attempts to apply understanding to deconstruct information but provides limited evidence that it has been understood. Offers judgements that are not supported by evidence and are not balanced.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is supported by some evidence. Offers an unbalanced conclusion, supported by limited evidence.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a judgement which is supported by evidence and is mostly balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding to produce a full, coherent interpretation that is relevant and supported by evidence. Offers a rational, substantiated and balanced conclusion, fully supported by evidence.` }
    ]
  },
  'T4': {
    name: 'The Carbon Cycle & Energy Security',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 1 gives the concentration of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere. The pre-industrial concentration (c.1750) was about 280 parts per million (ppm). The annual mean concentration measured at Mauna Loa, Hawaii, in 2023 was about 421 ppm.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in atmospheric CO₂ concentration between pre-industrial times and 2023. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: (421 − 280) ÷ 280 × 100, or 141 ÷ 280 × 100 (1)\n• Answer: 50.4% (1)\nAccept 50.4 with or without the % sign. Do not award the answer mark for 50% or 50.36% (not to one decimal place).` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 2 is a table of primary energy consumption per capita (gigajoules, GJ) for six countries, 2023 (data written for this practice question, approximately based on published values):\nQatar 700 | Canada 360 | United Kingdom 105 | China 115 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nCalculate the range in primary energy consumption per capita for the countries shown. You must show your working. Give your answer in GJ.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: highest minus lowest, 700 − 9 (1)\n• Answer: 691 GJ (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 3 shows electricity generation in a European country in one year (data written for this practice question). Total electricity generated was 320 terawatt hours (TWh). Of this, 112 TWh was generated from renewable sources (wind, solar, hydroelectric and bioenergy).\n\nCalculate the percentage of this country's electricity that was generated from renewable sources. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: 112 ÷ 320 × 100 (1)\n• Answer: 35% (accept 35.0%) (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 4 shows the area of forest cleared each year in the Brazilian Legal Amazon, as recorded by satellite monitoring (approximate figures, for this practice question):\n2019: 10,129 km² | 2020: 10,851 km² | 2021: 13,038 km² | 2022: 11,594 km²\n\nCalculate the mean annual area of forest cleared between 2019 and 2022. You must show your working. Give your answer in km².`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: (10,129 + 10,851 + 13,038 + 11,594) ÷ 4, or 45,612 ÷ 4 (1)\n• Answer: 11,403 km² (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 5 is a photograph of an oil sands (tar sands) surface mine near Fort McMurray in Alberta, Canada. It shows a wide, flat area of bare, dark ground stripped of vegetation, with large trucks and excavators, and a tailings pond of grey water in the background. Boreal forest is visible on the horizon.\n\nExplain one environmental impact of extracting unconventional fossil fuels such as oil sands.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying an impact and up to 2 further marks for explaining it (1+2).\nFor example:\n• Clearance of boreal forest and peatland (muskeg) to reach the bitumen (1) removes a major terrestrial carbon store (1) so carbon stored in trees and peat is released as CO₂ and the area can no longer act as a sink (1)\n• Large volumes of water are taken from rivers such as the Athabasca (1) and returned as contaminated tailings in ponds (1), which can leak toxic chemicals into groundwater and harm wildlife and downstream communities (1)\n• Extraction and upgrading of bitumen is very energy intensive (1) because steam or heat is needed to separate it from sand (1), so emissions per barrel are higher than for conventional oil (1)\nCredit other valid impacts, e.g. habitat fragmentation affecting caribou.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 2 is a table of primary energy consumption per capita (gigajoules, GJ), 2023 (data written for this practice question, approximately based on published values):\nQatar 700 | Canada 360 | United Kingdom 105 | China 115 | India 27 | Nigeria 9\n\nSuggest one reason why energy consumption per capita varies between these countries.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and up to 2 further marks for development or use of the data (1+2).\nFor example:\n• Level of economic development (1): higher-income countries such as Canada have more cars, appliances and energy-intensive services per person (1), whereas in Nigeria many people lack access to electricity and rely on fuelwood (1)\n• Climate (1): Canada's cold winters mean high demand for space heating (1) and Qatar's hot climate means very high demand for air conditioning and desalination (1)\n• Resource endowment and price (1): Qatar has abundant, cheap natural gas (1) so energy is subsidised and energy-intensive industries such as LNG processing are located there (1)\n• Economic structure (1): China's large manufacturing sector raises consumption above the UK, which has a service-based economy (1) despite the UK's higher income (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nThe oceans currently absorb roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide emitted by human activity each year.\n\nExplain one reason why the ability of the oceans to absorb carbon dioxide may decline in the future.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a reason and up to 2 further marks for explanation (1+2).\nFor example:\n• Warmer surface water holds less dissolved CO₂ (1) because gas solubility falls as temperature rises (1), so the physical (solubility) pump transfers less carbon from the atmosphere into the ocean (1)\n• Increased stratification of a warmer ocean (1) reduces mixing between surface and deep water (1), so less carbon-rich surface water is carried down to the deep ocean store and the surface becomes saturated (1)\n• Ocean acidification (1) reduces the carbonate ions available to phytoplankton, corals and shellfish to build calcium carbonate (1), weakening the carbonate pump and biological sequestration (1)\n• Changes to thermohaline circulation (1) such as a slowdown of the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation would reduce downwelling of cold, CO₂-rich water in the North Atlantic (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 6 shows GDP per capita and primary energy consumption per capita for eight countries (data written for this practice question):\nCountry | GDP per capita (US$ '000) | Energy per capita (GJ)\nNorway | 87.0 | 370\nCanada | 53.0 | 360\nUnited Kingdom | 48.0 | 105\nBrazil | 9.0 | 60\nChina | 12.5 | 115\nIndia | 2.5 | 27\nNigeria | 2.2 | 33\nMexico | 13.5 | 62\nSpearman's rank correlation coefficient: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n). Critical value for n = 8 at the 0.05 significance level: 0.643; at the 0.01 level: 0.833.\n\nCalculate the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient for this data and state whether the relationship is significant. You must show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct ranks (rank 1 = highest): GDP — Norway 1, Canada 2, UK 3, Mexico 4, China 5, Brazil 6, India 7, Nigeria 8; Energy — Norway 1, Canada 2, China 3, UK 4, Mexico 5, Brazil 6, Nigeria 7, India 8 (1)\n• Σd² = 8 (d² values: UK 1, Mexico 1, China 4, India 1, Nigeria 1) (1)\n• rs = 1 − (6 × 8) ÷ (512 − 8) = 1 − 48 ÷ 504 = 0.905 (accept 0.90 or 0.9) (1)\n• 0.905 exceeds the critical values at both 0.05 (0.643) and 0.01 (0.833), so there is a significant positive relationship at the 0.01 level / 99% confidence (1)\nAllow error carried forward for the significance statement.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 7 shows the primary energy mix of a country in one year (data written for this practice question): oil 38%, natural gas 34%, coal 4%, nuclear 7%, renewables 17%.\n\nDraw a divided bar graph to show the energy mix of this country. Use a bar 10 cm long to represent 100%.`, marks: 4, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `• Bar drawn 10 cm long with a scale or axis marked 0–100% (1)\n• Segments accurately plotted: oil 3.8 cm, gas 3.4 cm, coal 0.4 cm, nuclear 0.7 cm, renewables 1.7 cm (allow ±1 mm) (2: all five correct = 2, three or four correct = 1)\n• Key or labels provided for every segment, with distinct shading, and a title (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIn the long-term geological carbon cycle, carbon moves between the atmosphere, oceans and the lithosphere over millions of years.\n\nExplain how chemical weathering transfers carbon from the atmosphere to the lithosphere.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid point, or for development of a point, up to 4 marks.\n• Atmospheric CO₂ dissolves in rainwater to form a weak carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) (1)\n• This acid reacts with rocks such as limestone and silicate rocks (carbonation), breaking them down (1)\n• The products, including bicarbonate (hydrogen carbonate) ions, are carried in solution by rivers to the oceans (1)\n• Marine organisms use the dissolved carbonate to build calcium carbonate shells and skeletons (1)\n• When they die these settle on the sea floor and are compacted over millions of years into limestone and chalk, a lithospheric store (1)\n• Weathering of silicate rocks gives a net long-term removal of atmospheric CO₂; it is faster where uplift exposes fresh rock, e.g. the Himalayas (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nThe Strait of Hormuz, between Iran and Oman, is a narrow shipping channel through which roughly a fifth of the world's oil is transported each year.\n\nExplain two reasons why energy pathways such as this can threaten the energy security of consuming countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award up to 2 marks for each of two reasons (1 for the reason + 1 for explanation) (2×2).\nFor example:\n• Choke points can be blocked by conflict or political tension (1), e.g. threats by Iran to close the Strait, which would sharply cut supply and raise world oil prices (1)\n• Long pathways cross several countries or seas (1), so transit states can interrupt flows for political leverage, e.g. disputes over Russian gas pipelines through Ukraine (1)\n• Infrastructure is vulnerable to attack, accident or sabotage (1), e.g. damage to the Nord Stream pipelines in 2022 or attacks on tankers (1)\n• Importing countries that rely on a single pathway or supplier (1) have little spare capacity, so disruption causes shortages and price spikes (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nPhytoplankton in the surface ocean carry out around half of the Earth's photosynthesis.\n\nExplain how the biological pump transfers carbon from the atmosphere to the deep ocean.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark per valid point, or for development of a point, up to 4 marks.\n• Phytoplankton in the sunlit (euphotic) surface layer take in dissolved CO₂ during photosynthesis, converting it into organic carbon (1)\n• This lowers CO₂ in surface water, so more CO₂ diffuses in from the atmosphere (1)\n• Carbon passes along the food chain to zooplankton and fish, and is released in faeces and dead organisms (1)\n• This organic matter sinks as 'marine snow' to the deep ocean and sea floor (1)\n• Some is buried in sediments, storing carbon for hundreds to millions of years; some decomposes at depth where it remains in deep water for centuries (1)` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nVolcanic activity, such as the eruptions of Mount Etna in Sicily, releases carbon dioxide from the Earth's interior.\n\nExplain the role of geological processes in returning carbon to the atmosphere.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• Carbon is stored in sedimentary rocks such as limestone and in fossil fuels in the lithosphere — the largest store on Earth\n• At subduction zones, carbon-rich ocean sediments and crust are carried into the mantle; heat releases CO₂ which rises in magma\n• Volcanic outgassing at plate boundaries and hotspots releases CO₂ from magma during and between eruptions; vents and fumaroles release CO₂ continuously\n• Mid-ocean ridges also outgas CO₂ to the ocean and atmosphere\n• Metamorphism of carbonate rocks releases CO₂\n• Uplift exposes rocks, which can release carbon by weathering of organic-rich shales\n• These fluxes are small (around 0.1 GtC per year) and slow, balancing chemical weathering over millions of years\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Explanation of processes is partial.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Explanation of how geological processes return carbon is detailed and well developed.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 8 is a line graph of monthly mean atmospheric CO₂ concentration (ppm) at Mauna Loa, Hawaii, 1960–2023 (the 'Keeling Curve'). The line rises from about 317 ppm in 1960 to about 421 ppm in 2023, with the rise becoming steeper over time. Superimposed on the rising trend is a regular annual zig-zag of about 6 ppm: concentration peaks each May and is lowest each September–October.\n\nExplain the patterns shown in Figure 8.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Combustion of fossil fuels for energy, industry and transport releases carbon stored in the lithosphere\n• Land use change, especially tropical deforestation, releases carbon from biomass and soils\n• Photosynthesis removes CO₂ and respiration and decomposition return it\nAO2\n• The long-term rise of around 100 ppm reflects human emissions exceeding uptake by ocean and land sinks\n• The steepening of the curve reflects accelerating emissions with industrialisation of emerging economies such as China and India\n• The annual cycle reflects the Northern Hemisphere's much larger land area and vegetation: CO₂ falls during the northern summer growing season as photosynthesis exceeds respiration, and rises in autumn and winter when leaves fall and decomposition dominates\n• The peak in May occurs at the end of the northern winter, before leaf growth draws CO₂ down\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to provide limited explanation of the patterns in Figure 8.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to find some relevant connections with the trend and/or seasonal pattern.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to find fully relevant connections that explain both the long-term trend and the seasonal pattern.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 9 shows estimated carbon stored in vegetation and soil in three biomes (tonnes of carbon per hectare; data written for this practice question):\nBiome | Vegetation | Soil (top 1 m)\nTropical rainforest (Amazon) | 200 | 120\nTemperate grassland (Great Plains) | 10 | 240\nArctic tundra (northern Alaska) | 5 | 350 (much of it in permafrost)\n\nExplain why the size of terrestrial carbon stores varies between these biomes.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Net primary productivity depends on temperature, light and precipitation\n• Decomposition rates are controlled by temperature and moisture\n• Soil carbon accumulates where inputs of dead organic matter exceed decomposition\nAO2\n• The rainforest has the largest vegetation store because high temperatures and rainfall all year give very high NPP and tall, dense forest biomass\n• But its soil store is relatively small because rapid decomposition and nutrient cycling in hot, wet conditions stop organic matter accumulating\n• Grassland has little above-ground biomass but deep, dense root systems and seasonal drought/cold slow decay, building thick, carbon-rich soils (e.g. chernozems)\n• Tundra vegetation is low and sparse due to the short growing season, but cold and waterlogged soils mean decomposition is extremely slow, so carbon has built up over thousands of years and is frozen in permafrost\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to provide limited explanation of Figure 9.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant. Applies knowledge to find some relevant connections between processes and the data.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to find fully relevant connections, explaining both vegetation and soil stores for the biomes.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nThe Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) and its allies (OPEC+) produce a large share of the world's crude oil and agree production quotas.\n\nExplain how different players can affect the energy security of countries that import oil.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• OPEC/OPEC+ can cut or raise output to influence world prices, e.g. the 1973 oil embargo or production cuts in 2020 and 2023; price rises reduce affordability for importers\n• Energy TNCs (e.g. ExxonMobil, Shell, BP, Saudi Aramco) control exploration, refining and distribution; investment decisions determine future supply and access to new reserves\n• Governments of producing countries can use energy as a political tool, e.g. Russia reducing gas supplies to Europe in 2022\n• Governments of importing countries shape security through diversification, strategic reserves (e.g. the US Strategic Petroleum Reserve), trade deals and subsidies for alternatives\n• Consumers influence demand and therefore the level of import dependence, through transport and heating choices\n• The combined actions of players affect availability, accessibility and affordability — the components of energy security\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Explanation of the role of players is partial.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of how a range of players affect energy security.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 10 is a map of major natural gas pipelines from Russia to Europe before 2022. It shows the Nord Stream pipelines under the Baltic Sea direct to Germany, the Yamal–Europe pipeline across Belarus and Poland, the Brotherhood pipeline system across Ukraine and the TurkStream pipeline under the Black Sea to Turkey. A label states that in 2021 Russia supplied about 40% of the European Union's imported gas.\n\nExplain why this pattern of energy pathways created risks for European energy security.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Energy security means reliable, affordable and accessible supply\n• Pipelines are fixed pathways linking a supplier to consumers and are costly to replace\n• Transit countries and geopolitical relations affect flows\nAO2\n• Heavy dependence on one supplier (around 40% of imports) gave Russia political leverage over the EU\n• Several routes cross transit states (Ukraine, Belarus) where disputes, such as the Russia–Ukraine gas disputes of 2006 and 2009, interrupted supplies\n• Following the invasion of Ukraine in 2022 flows were cut and the Nord Stream pipelines were damaged, causing record gas and electricity prices\n• Countries such as Germany with little LNG import capacity were especially exposed and had to build new terminals rapidly\n• Undersea pipelines are vulnerable to sabotage and difficult to protect\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to provide limited explanation of Figure 10.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant. Applies knowledge to find some relevant connections with the pathways shown.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to find fully relevant connections between the pathways shown and the risks to energy security.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nBrazil is one of the world's largest producers of bioethanol, made mainly from sugar cane grown in São Paulo state, and most cars in Brazil can run on ethanol blends.\n\nExplain the costs and benefits of biofuels as an alternative to fossil fuels.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks).\nIndicative content:\nBenefits\n• Renewable, and in theory carbon-neutral because crops absorb CO₂ as they grow\n• Reduce dependence on imported oil, improving energy security (Brazil's Proálcool programme from the 1970s)\n• Create rural employment and income and use existing vehicle technology\nCosts\n• Land for fuel crops competes with food production, which can raise food prices (food versus fuel)\n• Expansion of crops can cause indirect land use change, pushing cattle ranching and soya into the Cerrado and Amazon and releasing stored carbon\n• Growing, fertilising, harvesting and processing require energy, so net emissions savings vary\n• Monocultures reduce biodiversity and need large amounts of water, affecting the water cycle\n• Second-generation biofuels from waste reduce some of these costs but are less developed\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; may focus mainly on costs or on benefits.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of both costs and benefits.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nPermafrost soils in the Arctic, such as those of the North Slope of Alaska and northern Siberia, are estimated to hold around 1,400–1,600 billion tonnes of organic carbon — more than is currently in the atmosphere.\n\nExplain how a warming climate may affect the carbon stored in Arctic tundra.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• The Arctic is warming around three to four times faster than the global average, partly due to the ice–albedo feedback\n• Thawing permafrost deepens the active layer, allowing microbes to decompose organic matter that has been frozen for thousands of years\n• Decomposition in dry conditions releases CO₂; in waterlogged, anaerobic conditions it releases methane, a more powerful greenhouse gas\n• Thermokarst lakes and collapsing ground speed up release\n• This is a positive feedback: release of greenhouse gases causes further warming and more thaw\n• Some offset as warming lengthens the growing season and shrubs spread northwards ('Arctic greening'), increasing photosynthesis\n• Increased wildfire in tundra and boreal forest releases carbon from vegetation and peat\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of processes and feedbacks.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 11 shows projected global surface temperature change by 2081–2100, compared with 1850–1900, under five IPCC emissions scenarios (approximate values from the IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, 2021):\nVery low emissions (SSP1-1.9): 1.4°C (range 1.0–1.8)\nLow (SSP1-2.6): 1.8°C (1.3–2.4)\nIntermediate (SSP2-4.5): 2.7°C (2.1–3.5)\nHigh (SSP3-7.0): 3.6°C (2.8–4.6)\nVery high (SSP5-8.5): 4.4°C (3.3–5.7)\n\nExplain why there is uncertainty about future global temperatures.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Future emissions depend on human factors: population growth, economic growth, energy mix and technology, and the success of mitigation policies and agreements\n• The climate system contains feedbacks (water vapour, ice–albedo, permafrost, forest dieback) whose strength is not fully known\n• Possible tipping points could cause abrupt, irreversible change\nAO2\n• The spread between scenarios (1.4°C to 4.4°C) shows that the largest uncertainty is human — which emissions pathway is followed\n• The ranges within each scenario (e.g. 3.3–5.7°C for SSP5-8.5) reflect physical uncertainty about climate sensitivity and feedbacks\n• Ranges widen under higher emissions because feedbacks and tipping points become more likely\n• Models are simplifications and depend on assumptions about future sinks such as oceans and forests\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to provide limited explanation of Figure 11.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant. Applies knowledge to find some relevant connections with the data.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to find fully relevant connections, distinguishing human and physical sources of uncertainty shown in Figure 11.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIn 2005, 2010 and 2015–16 the Amazon rainforest experienced severe droughts, during which tree death increased and parts of the forest temporarily released more carbon than they absorbed.\n\nExplain how changes to the carbon cycle in tropical rainforests can affect the water cycle.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• Rainforest trees return large amounts of water to the atmosphere by transpiration; much Amazon rainfall is recycled from the forest itself\n• Loss of biomass through deforestation, drought-induced tree death or fire reduces transpiration and interception\n• This reduces atmospheric moisture and convectional rainfall, lengthening dry seasons downwind (including the 'flying rivers' supplying southern Brazil)\n• Less interception and root uptake increase surface runoff, soil erosion and flood risk, and reduce infiltration and groundwater recharge\n• Higher temperatures from warming increase evaporation and drought stress\n• Positive feedback: drier forest stores less carbon, becomes more fire-prone, and may shift towards savanna (dieback tipping point)\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; links between the two cycles are partial.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of links between the carbon and water cycles.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 12 is a photograph of the Deepwater Horizon drilling rig on fire in the Gulf of Mexico in April 2010. Thick black smoke rises from the rig and fire boats are spraying water onto it. The caption states that the rig was drilling a well in about 1,500 m of water, that 11 workers were killed and that the well leaked for 87 days.\n\nExplain the environmental costs of developing deep water oil reserves.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Deep water reserves require drilling through great depths of water and rock under high pressure and low temperatures\n• Accidents are harder to control and blowouts are difficult to stop at depth\n• Extraction adds carbon from the lithosphere to the atmosphere when the oil is burned\nAO2\n• The 87-day leak shown released around 4 million barrels of oil, contaminating Gulf coastlines from Louisiana to Florida\n• Oil and chemical dispersants harmed marine ecosystems, including dolphins, turtles, seabirds, coral and fisheries\n• Coastal marshes in the Mississippi delta were damaged, accelerating erosion\n• Even without accidents, deep water development disturbs the sea bed, produces drilling waste and has high energy costs, and extends dependence on fossil fuels\n• Costs extended to local fishing and tourism economies\n\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to provide limited explanation of Figure 12.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant. Applies knowledge to find some relevant connections with the resource.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to find fully relevant connections between the resource and the environmental costs of deep water oil.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nThe oceans hold around 38,000 billion tonnes of carbon, about fifty times as much as the atmosphere.\n\nExplain the role of physical and biological processes in sequestering carbon in the oceans.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• Physical (solubility) pump: CO₂ dissolves in surface water, especially cold polar water where solubility is higher\n• Thermohaline circulation: cold, dense, salty water sinks in the North Atlantic and around Antarctica, carrying dissolved carbon to the deep ocean where it may remain for centuries\n• Upwelling returns carbon-rich deep water to the surface elsewhere, releasing CO₂\n• Biological pump: phytoplankton photosynthesise in the euphotic zone; carbon passes through food webs and sinks as dead organisms and faecal pellets ('marine snow')\n• Carbonate pump: organisms such as coccolithophores, foraminifera and corals build calcium carbonate shells; these sink and form sediments that become limestone and chalk\n• Some carbon is buried in sea-floor sediments, linking to the geological cycle\n• Continental shelf ecosystems such as mangroves, salt marshes and seagrass ('blue carbon') store carbon in sediments\n• Uptake is affected by temperature, nutrient supply and ocean chemistry\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Limited explanation of processes.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Explanation covers some processes but may be unbalanced between physical and biological.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Detailed, well-developed explanation of both physical and biological processes.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nBefore the Industrial Revolution, the fluxes of carbon into and out of the atmosphere were approximately in balance for several thousand years.\n\nExplain why a balanced carbon cycle is important in sustaining other Earth systems.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• Atmosphere: CO₂ and methane are greenhouse gases; the natural greenhouse effect keeps the mean surface temperature at about 15°C rather than about −18°C\n• A stable concentration keeps global temperatures within the range that supports existing ecosystems and the water cycle (evaporation, precipitation patterns, ice stores)\n• Oceans: balanced exchange keeps ocean pH stable (about 8.2 pre-industrial); acidification would harm calcifying organisms, coral reefs and food webs\n• Land: photosynthesis and respiration sustain soil health — soil organic carbon supports soil structure, water retention, nutrient cycling and fertility for food production\n• Vegetation stores regulate local climate and the water cycle through transpiration\n• Feedbacks link the carbon cycle to sea level, sea ice and permafrost; imbalance triggers positive feedbacks that destabilise these systems\n• Human wellbeing depends on these systems for food, water and stable climate\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; explanation of links to other systems is partial.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of links to atmosphere, oceans and land systems.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIn 2023 China accounted for more than half of the world's coal consumption, but it also installed more solar panels in that year than the rest of the world combined.\n\nExplain why the energy mix varies between countries.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• Physical availability of domestic resources: coal in China, India and Poland; gas in Qatar and Russia; hydroelectric potential in Norway and Brazil; geothermal in Iceland\n• Cost and technology: access to capital and technology determines whether nuclear or large-scale renewables are possible\n• Level of development: lower-income countries often rely on fuelwood and charcoal; industrialising countries rely on cheap coal\n• Political decisions: France's nuclear programme after the 1973 oil crisis; Germany's Energiewende and nuclear phase-out; UK coal phase-out completed in 2024\n• Energy security concerns: diversification to reduce imports\n• Environmental attitudes and climate commitments under the Paris Agreement\n• Climate and geography: wind resources in the North Sea, solar in the Middle East and Australia\n• Historical legacy and existing infrastructure lock countries into particular sources\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; explanation of factors is partial or lacks examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of a range of physical, economic and political factors supported by examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIn 2024 the UK's Hinkley Point C nuclear power station in Somerset was under construction, with costs estimated at over £30 billion.\n\nExplain the costs and benefits of nuclear power as an alternative to fossil fuels.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks).\nIndicative content:\nBenefits\n• Very low carbon emissions during operation, helping to meet carbon targets\n• Reliable baseload electricity, not dependent on weather, complementing wind and solar\n• High energy density; uranium is available from politically stable suppliers such as Canada and Australia, improving energy security\n• Long plant lifespans (60 years or more) and job creation\nCosts\n• Very high construction costs and frequent delays and overruns (e.g. Hinkley Point C)\n• Long-term storage of radioactive waste remains unresolved; no permanent UK repository yet\n• Risk of accidents with major impacts (Chernobyl 1986, Fukushima 2011) affects public perception\n• Decommissioning is slow and costly\n• Concerns about security and nuclear proliferation; uranium mining has environmental impacts\n• Some countries (Germany) have chosen to phase out nuclear, while others (France) depend heavily on it\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; may focus mainly on costs or on benefits.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of both costs and benefits supported by examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIn Indonesia and Malaysia large areas of tropical peat swamp forest have been drained and cleared for oil palm plantations, partly to supply palm oil for food and biofuel.\n\nExplain how growing demand for food and energy resources threatens the carbon and water cycles.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• Rising population and incomes increase demand for food, timber and biofuels, driving land use change\n• Clearance of tropical forest (Amazon cattle ranching and soya; South-east Asian oil palm) removes biomass carbon stores and reduces sequestration\n• Draining peatlands exposes peat to oxygen, so it decomposes and releases CO₂; dry peat is prone to fires, e.g. the 2015 Indonesian fires\n• Ploughing grasslands and intensive farming reduce soil organic carbon\n• Reduced transpiration lowers atmospheric moisture and rainfall; more runoff increases flooding and soil erosion\n• Drainage lowers water tables and alters river regimes\n• Irrigation for crops depletes aquifers and rivers\n• Arctic oil and gas exploration and tar sands extraction disturb permafrost, peat and boreal forest\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; explanation may focus on one cycle only.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of threats to both the carbon and water cycles supported by examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nBetween 2016 and 2024 the Great Barrier Reef off Queensland, Australia, experienced repeated mass coral bleaching events linked to marine heatwaves.\n\nExplain how ocean warming and acidification threaten marine ecosystems and human wellbeing.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks).\nIndicative content:\n• The oceans absorb heat and CO₂; dissolved CO₂ forms carbonic acid, lowering pH (by about 0.1 since pre-industrial times)\n• Lower carbonate ion availability makes it harder for corals, shellfish and plankton such as pteropods to build calcium carbonate shells and skeletons\n• Higher water temperatures cause corals to expel symbiotic algae (bleaching); repeated bleaching leads to coral death and loss of biodiversity\n• Warming shifts fish populations polewards and reduces oxygen in water\n• Damage to plankton affects the base of marine food webs\n• Human wellbeing: hundreds of millions of people rely on fish protein; reef loss threatens fisheries and tourism income (e.g. Queensland tourism)\n• Reefs protect coasts from waves; their loss increases coastal flood and erosion risk for low-lying communities such as the Maldives\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant; explanation of impacts on ecosystems and/or people is partial.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout, with detailed explanation of impacts on both ecosystems and human wellbeing.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 13 shows estimated annual carbon fluxes to and from the atmosphere in the 2010s (approximate values, billion tonnes of carbon per year, GtC/yr):\nFlux | To atmosphere | From atmosphere\nOcean–atmosphere exchange | 80 | 82.5\nPhotosynthesis / respiration and decomposition on land | 117 | 120\nFossil fuel combustion and cement | 9.5 | —\nLand use change (mainly deforestation) | 1.1 | —\nVolcanic outgassing | 0.1 | —\nThe atmosphere gains about 5 GtC per year overall.\n\nUsing Figure 13 and your own knowledge, assess the relative importance of natural and human fluxes in changing the size of the atmospheric carbon store.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Natural fluxes include photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, ocean diffusion and volcanic outgassing; human fluxes include fossil fuel combustion, cement production and land use change\n• Atmospheric CO₂ has risen from about 280 ppm to over 420 ppm since pre-industrial times\nAO2\n• Natural fluxes are much larger in size (over 80 and over 115 GtC/yr) than human fluxes (about 10.6 GtC/yr)\n• However, natural fluxes are almost balanced, and in Figure 13 they are net sinks: the oceans remove about 2.5 GtC/yr more than they emit and land about 3 GtC/yr more\n• Human fluxes are one-way additions from long-term stores; about half of human emissions remain in the atmosphere, explaining the 5 GtC/yr gain\n• Volcanic outgassing is tiny and cannot explain the rise\n• Natural fluxes may become more important in future if warming weakens sinks or triggers releases from permafrost, peat and forest fires\n• Judgement: natural fluxes are larger in magnitude but human fluxes are the main cause of change in the store\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence from Figure 13, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 14 shows the share of electricity generation by source for three countries, 2023 (data written for this practice question, approximately based on published values):\nCountry | Fossil fuels | Nuclear | Hydroelectric | Other renewables\nChina | 65% | 5% | 13% | 17%\nFrance | 8% | 65% | 10% | 17%\nNorway | 2% | 0% | 88% | 10%\n\nUsing Figure 14 and your own knowledge, assess the importance of physical factors in explaining the energy mix of different countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Energy mix is shaped by physical factors (resource availability, relief, climate) and human factors (economics, technology, politics, energy security, environmental policy)\nAO2\n• Physical factors are very important for Norway: mountainous relief, high precipitation and glacial lakes give 88% hydroelectricity\n• China's large domestic coal reserves explain the high fossil fuel share, though hydroelectricity (e.g. Three Gorges Dam) also reflects physical potential\n• France's 65% nuclear is a political decision after the 1973 oil crisis to reduce imports, as France has few fossil fuel resources — so a lack of physical resources combined with human decisions\n• China's rapid growth in other renewables reflects government policy, investment and manufacturing capacity, not just physical potential\n• Norway also has large oil and gas reserves but exports them — a political and economic choice\n• Judgement: physical factors set the possibilities, but economic and political decisions are often decisive\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence from Figure 14, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 15 shows United States crude oil production (million barrels per day; approximate values): 2008: 5.0 | 2012: 6.5 | 2016: 8.8 | 2020: 11.3 | 2023: 12.9. A note states that most of the increase came from shale (tight) oil extracted by hydraulic fracturing ('fracking') in areas such as the Permian Basin of Texas and New Mexico. In the UK, fracking at Preston New Road, Lancashire, was halted in 2019 after it triggered an earth tremor of magnitude 2.9.\n\nUsing Figure 15 and your own knowledge, assess the extent to which the energy security benefits of unconventional fossil fuels outweigh their environmental costs.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Unconventional sources include shale oil and gas, tar sands (Alberta) and deep water oil (Gulf of Mexico, offshore Brazil)\n• They require more complex and energy-intensive extraction\nAO2\nBenefits\n• US production more than doubled between 2008 and 2023 (5.0 to 12.9 million barrels per day), making the USA the world's largest oil producer and a net exporter of petroleum, reducing dependence on OPEC\n• Domestic supply increases availability and can reduce prices; jobs and tax revenue in producing regions\n• Shale gas replacing coal in US electricity has reduced some CO₂ emissions\nCosts\n• Fracking uses large volumes of water, risks groundwater contamination and can trigger induced seismicity (Preston New Road)\n• Methane leaks reduce the climate benefit of gas\n• Tar sands destroy boreal forest and peat and create toxic tailings; deep water risks spills (Deepwater Horizon 2010)\n• All extend fossil fuel dependence, adding lithospheric carbon to the atmosphere and risking 'stranded assets'\n• Judgement: benefits are significant at national scale and short term, but global environmental costs are long term; the balance depends on the context (US versus UK public opposition)\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence from Figure 15, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 16 gives information about three carbon capture and storage (CCS) projects (approximate values):\n• Sleipner, Norwegian North Sea (since 1996): CO₂ separated from natural gas and injected into a saline aquifer beneath the sea bed; about 1 million tonnes of CO₂ stored per year.\n• Boundary Dam, Saskatchewan, Canada (since 2014): CO₂ captured from a coal-fired power station; designed to capture 1 million tonnes per year but has often captured less. Much of the captured CO₂ is sold for enhanced oil recovery.\n• Gorgon, Western Australia (since 2019): CO₂ injected from a gas processing plant; has stored well below its target because of technical problems.\nFor comparison, global energy-related CO₂ emissions are about 37 billion tonnes per year.\n\nUsing Figure 16 and your own knowledge, assess the potential of carbon capture and storage to reduce global carbon emissions.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• CCS captures CO₂ from power stations or industry, compresses and transports it, and stores it in geological formations (depleted oil and gas fields, saline aquifers)\n• It is one of several radical technologies, alongside hydrogen fuel cells, electric vehicles and direct air capture\nAO2\n• Sleipner shows that long-term geological storage is technically possible and safe over decades\n• CCS could decarbonise hard-to-abate sectors such as cement and steel, and BECCS (e.g. Drax, UK) could create negative emissions\n• However, the scale is tiny: projects of about 1 million tonnes per year compared with about 37 billion tonnes of emissions — tens of thousands of times smaller\n• Boundary Dam and Gorgon show under-performance, high costs and technical problems\n• Use of captured CO₂ for enhanced oil recovery extracts more fossil fuel, reducing net benefit\n• Capture uses energy (an 'energy penalty'), and risks of leakage and public opposition exist\n• May be used to justify continued fossil fuel use rather than switching to renewables\n• Judgement: CCS has some potential for specific sectors but is unlikely to make a major contribution without large investment and carbon pricing\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence from Figure 16, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nFigure 17 shows the area of forest cleared each year in the Brazilian Legal Amazon (approximate values, km²): 2004: 27,800 | 2008: 12,900 | 2012: 4,600 | 2016: 7,900 | 2019: 10,100 | 2021: 13,000 | 2023: 9,000. A note states that a 2021 study found parts of the south-eastern Amazon now emit more carbon than they absorb, because of clearance, fire and drought.\n\nUsing Figure 17 and your own knowledge, assess the extent to which deforestation threatens the role of the Amazon rainforest in the carbon and water cycles.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• The Amazon stores large amounts of carbon in biomass and soils and is a major carbon sink\n• It recycles moisture through transpiration, generating much of its own rainfall and supplying moisture to other parts of South America\nAO2\n• Clearance fell sharply from 2004 to 2012 (27,800 to 4,600 km²), showing that policy (monitoring, protected areas, soy moratorium) can reduce the threat\n• It rose again to about 13,000 km² by 2021, linked to weaker enforcement and expansion of cattle ranching and soya, before falling in 2023 — showing the threat depends on political decisions\n• Cumulative loss of around 17–20% of the original forest raises concern about a tipping point to savanna\n• The south-eastern Amazon becoming a carbon source shows the sink role is already weakening locally\n• Reduced transpiration lowers rainfall and lengthens dry seasons, increasing drought and fire risk — a positive feedback\n• Other threats also matter: climate change and droughts (2005, 2010, 2015–16, 2023), mining, dams and road building\n• Judgement: deforestation is a major threat, but its significance is amplified by its interaction with climate change\n\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence and support from evidence, and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence from Figure 17, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIn 2023 oil, coal and natural gas together still supplied around 80% of the world's primary energy.\n\nEvaluate the view that reliance on fossil fuels to drive economic development will remain the global norm.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Energy consumption is closely linked to economic development; fossil fuels are energy-dense, storable and supported by existing infrastructure\n• Energy security, energy mix and the role of players (OPEC, TNCs, governments, consumers)\n• Alternatives: renewables, nuclear, biofuels and radical technologies\nAO2\nFor the view\n• Fossil fuels' share of primary energy has fallen only slowly in decades; absolute use is still rising\n• Emerging economies (India, Indonesia, sub-Saharan Africa) rely on coal and oil for industrialisation and rising consumption\n• Unconventional sources (US shale, Alberta tar sands, deep water off Brazil) have extended reserves\n• Powerful players — OPEC+, state oil companies, energy TNCs — have strong interests in continued production\n• Transport, aviation, shipping, steel, cement and plastics are hard to decarbonise\nAgainst the view\n• Rapid falls in the cost of solar, wind and batteries; China's record renewable installations\n• Countries such as the UK (coal phase-out in 2024), Denmark and Norway show decoupling of growth from emissions\n• Paris Agreement commitments, carbon pricing and net zero targets\n• Electric vehicles reducing oil demand growth; energy efficiency\n• Some developing countries may 'leapfrog' to off-grid solar\nEvaluation\n• Likely to remain the norm in the short to medium term, but the pace of change varies between countries; the future depends on policy and technology\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nBetween 2010 and 2023 the cost of electricity from new solar photovoltaic plants fell by around 90%, and offshore wind farms such as Dogger Bank in the North Sea are among the largest in the world.\n\nEvaluate the view that switching to renewable energy is the most effective way for countries to achieve energy security.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Energy security: availability, accessibility, affordability and reliability of supply\n• Renewable sources (wind, solar, hydro, tidal, geothermal), biofuels, nuclear, unconventional fossil fuels, energy efficiency and diversification\nAO2\nFor the view\n• Domestic renewables reduce dependence on imports and exposure to price shocks (e.g. European gas crisis 2022)\n• Inexhaustible and increasingly cheap; examples such as Denmark (wind), Norway and Brazil (hydro), Iceland (geothermal)\n• Also meets carbon targets, so improves long-term security against climate impacts\nAgainst the view\n• Intermittency of wind and solar requires storage, backup or interconnectors; hydro is vulnerable to drought (e.g. Brazil 2021, China 2022)\n• Supply chains for panels, batteries and critical minerals (lithium, cobalt, rare earths) are concentrated in a few countries, notably China — a new security risk\n• Nuclear provides reliable low-carbon baseload (France)\n• Some countries gain security from domestic fossil fuels (US shale, Qatari gas), and diversification of suppliers and pathways (LNG terminals in Germany)\n• Reducing demand through efficiency and insulation is often the cheapest option\n• High upfront costs and grid investment for developing countries; local opposition to onshore wind\nEvaluation\n• Renewables are a central part of long-term energy security, but are most effective as part of a diversified mix with storage, interconnection and demand reduction; effectiveness depends on physical resources and wealth\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nThe Netherlands is investing in its 'Delta Programme' to protect against rising sea levels, while also committing to cut greenhouse gas emissions by 55% by 2030 compared with 1990.\n\nEvaluate the view that mitigation is more important than adaptation in responding to the impacts of a warming climate.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Mitigation reduces greenhouse gas emissions or increases sinks: carbon taxation, renewable switching, energy efficiency, afforestation, CCS, international agreements\n• Adaptation reduces vulnerability to impacts: water conservation and management, resistant crops, flood defences, changing land use, managed retreat\n• Impacts include sea-level rise, drought, extreme weather, ocean acidification and ecosystem change\nAO2\nFor the view\n• Mitigation tackles the cause; without it, warming could exceed limits to adaptation (e.g. small island states such as Tuvalu or the Maldives)\n• Avoids positive feedbacks and tipping points (permafrost, Amazon dieback)\n• Global benefit; Paris Agreement 1.5–2°C goal\nAgainst the view\n• Warming is already 'locked in' because of past emissions; adaptation is needed now regardless\n• Adaptation brings direct, local benefits and can be cheaper and quicker, e.g. Dutch Delta Programme, drought-resistant crops in the Sahel, Bangladesh cyclone shelters\n• Mitigation is slow and depends on global cooperation; free-rider problem\n• Lower-income countries with low historic emissions prioritise adaptation and climate finance\n• Some strategies do both (mangrove restoration, afforestation, sustainable urban design)\nEvaluation\n• Both are essential and complementary; mitigation is more important in the long term, but adaptation is more urgent for vulnerable places\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nIPCC projections for global warming by 2100 range from about 1.4°C to 4.4°C above pre-industrial levels, depending on the emissions scenario.\n\nEvaluate the view that human factors are a greater source of uncertainty than physical factors in predicting future changes to the carbon cycle and climate.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Future emissions pathways depend on population, economic growth, energy mix, technology and policy\n• Physical uncertainty: climate sensitivity, feedbacks (water vapour, clouds, ice–albedo), carbon sinks and tipping points\nAO2\nHuman factors\n• The difference between scenarios (SSP1-1.9 to SSP5-8.5) is larger than the spread within any one scenario, especially by 2100\n• Uncertainty over population growth (8 billion in 2022, projections for 2100 vary widely)\n• Economic development of emerging economies and their energy choices\n• Whether pledges under the Paris Agreement are met; political changes (e.g. US withdrawals from the agreement)\n• Rate of technology change — renewables, CCS, electric vehicles\nPhysical factors\n• Climate sensitivity is still uncertain (likely range about 2.5–4°C per doubling of CO₂)\n• Weakening of ocean and land sinks, permafrost methane, Amazon dieback and changes to the thermohaline circulation could be abrupt and non-linear\n• Cloud feedbacks and ice sheet dynamics are hard to model\n• Over shorter time periods natural variability (El Niño) dominates\nEvaluation\n• Human factors dominate long-term uncertainty, but physical uncertainty grows under high emissions; the two interact because feedbacks depend on how much is emitted\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nThe ocean absorbs around a quarter of human CO₂ emissions each year, and land ecosystems such as forests and soils absorb around a further 30%.\n\nEvaluate the view that the oceans are more important than terrestrial stores in regulating the composition of the atmosphere.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Ocean processes: solubility pump, biological pump, carbonate pump, thermohaline circulation; phytoplankton and oxygen production\n• Terrestrial processes: photosynthesis, respiration, decomposition, soil carbon, peat and permafrost; long-term geological cycle via weathering\nAO2\nFor the view\n• The ocean is by far the largest active store (about 38,000 GtC) and exchanges around 80 GtC per year with the atmosphere\n• Deep ocean stores carbon for centuries to millennia; marine sediments feed the geological cycle\n• Phytoplankton produce around half of global oxygen\n• Ocean uptake has buffered about a quarter of human emissions\nAgainst the view\n• Terrestrial photosynthesis/respiration fluxes (about 120 GtC per year) are larger than ocean exchange and drive the seasonal CO₂ cycle\n• Land sinks currently absorb a similar or larger share of human emissions than the oceans\n• Soils, peatlands and permafrost hold more carbon than vegetation and atmosphere combined\n• Over geological timescales the lithosphere (sedimentary rocks) is the largest store and silicate weathering is the main long-term thermostat\n• Land sinks are vulnerable to deforestation, fire and drought; ocean sinks to warming, stratification and acidification\nEvaluation\n• Each is important at different timescales; the oceans dominate long-term buffering, land drives short-term variability; both are threatened by warming\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nUnder the 2015 Paris Agreement almost every country submitted Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) to limit warming to well below 2°C. Sweden has had a carbon tax since 1991, and the European Union has operated an Emissions Trading System since 2005.\n\nEvaluate the view that global agreements are the most effective way to mitigate the impacts of human activity on the carbon cycle.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Mitigation strategies: carbon taxation and trading, renewable switching, energy efficiency, afforestation, CCS\n• Global agreements: Kyoto Protocol (1997), Paris Agreement (2015), COP meetings, REDD+\nAO2\nFor the view\n• Climate change is a global problem requiring collective action; agreements set common targets and a review ('ratchet') process\n• Paris has near-universal participation, unlike Kyoto, which excluded binding targets for developing countries and lacked US ratification\n• Agreements mobilise climate finance and technology transfer for developing countries\n• Global methane pledge and phase-down language on coal at COP26 and COP28\nAgainst the view\n• NDCs are voluntary and not enforceable; current pledges imply around 2.5–3°C of warming\n• Political changes weaken commitment (US withdrawals)\n• National and regional policies deliver actual cuts: UK Climate Change Act (2008) and coal phase-out, Sweden's carbon tax, EU ETS, China's renewable investment\n• Local and city-scale actions (C40 cities), businesses and individuals reduce demand\n• Afforestation and protection of forests (e.g. Brazil's enforcement against Amazon clearance) depend on national governance\n• Technology and falling costs may matter more than agreements\nEvaluation\n• Global agreements provide the framework and signals, but are only effective when implemented through national policy and local action\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` },
      { q: `Section C — Physical Systems and Sustainability.\nNorway, a major oil and gas exporter, aims for all new cars sold to be zero-emission, and in 2023 about 80% of new cars sold there were fully electric.\n\nEvaluate the view that radical technologies such as carbon capture and storage, hydrogen fuel cells and electric vehicles offer the best solution to the problem of rising carbon emissions.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks).\nIndicative content:\nAO1\n• Radical technologies: CCS and BECCS, hydrogen fuel cells, electric vehicles, direct air capture, geo-engineering proposals\n• Other approaches: renewable switching, energy efficiency, behavioural change, carbon pricing, afforestation and protecting carbon stores\nAO2\nFor the view\n• Electric vehicles reduce oil demand and urban pollution; Norway shows rapid uptake is possible with incentives\n• CCS could address emissions from cement, steel and power that are hard to decarbonise (Sleipner)\n• Green hydrogen could decarbonise heavy industry, shipping and heavy transport\n• Technologies allow economic growth to continue with lower emissions\nAgainst the view\n• CCS is expensive, small-scale and has under-performed (Boundary Dam, Gorgon); captured CO₂ is often used for enhanced oil recovery\n• Most hydrogen is currently made from natural gas ('grey' hydrogen); green hydrogen is costly\n• EVs are only low-carbon if electricity is low-carbon; battery minerals have environmental and social costs (e.g. cobalt in the DR Congo)\n• Technologies are slow to scale and may delay action ('moral hazard')\n• Proven, cheaper options exist now: renewables, efficiency, reducing deforestation, protecting peatlands, dietary change\n• High costs mean many developing countries cannot adopt them without support\nEvaluation\n• Radical technologies are a necessary part of reaching net zero, particularly for hard-to-abate sectors, but are not the best single solution; they must accompany renewable switching, efficiency and protection of natural carbon stores\n\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Applies knowledge to produce an unsupported or generic conclusion, drawn from an argument that is unbalanced or lacks coherence.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is occasionally relevant and may include some inaccuracies. Applies knowledge to produce an interpretation with limited coherence, support from evidence and a conclusion that is partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Demonstrates geographical knowledge and understanding which is mostly relevant and accurate. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation that is mostly supported by evidence, and a conclusion drawn from an argument that is generally balanced.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant geographical knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation supported by evidence, and a rational, substantiated conclusion drawn from a balanced argument.` }
    ]
  },
  'T5': {
    name: 'Globalisation',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 1 shows the KOF Globalisation Index score for Vietnam in two years (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1: KOF Globalisation Index (0–100), Vietnam — 1995: 39.2; 2020: 66.8.\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in Vietnam's KOF Globalisation Index score between 1995 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: (66.8 − 39.2) ÷ 39.2 × 100, or 27.6 ÷ 39.2 × 100 (1)\n• Answer: 70.4% (1)\nAccept 70.4 without the % sign. An answer of 70% or 70.41% gains the working mark only.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 1 is a table showing foreign direct investment (FDI) inflows in one year for eight selected countries (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1: FDI inflows (US$ billion) — Singapore 141.0; Ireland 25.0; Brazil 46.0; India 44.0; Vietnam 18.0; Bangladesh 3.5; Chad 0.6; Kenya 0.5.\n\nCalculate the range in FDI inflows for the countries shown. You must show your working. Give your answer in US$ billion.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: 141.0 − 0.5 (highest Singapore minus lowest Kenya) (1)\n• Answer: US$140.5 billion (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 1 shows the percentage of the population using the internet in six countries in 2022 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1: Internet users (% of population) — South Korea 95; UK 88; Brazil 72; Pakistan 39; Ethiopia 21; Niger 13.\n\nCalculate the mean percentage of the population using the internet for these six countries. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: (95 + 88 + 72 + 39 + 21 + 13) ÷ 6 = 328 ÷ 6 (1)\n• Answer: 54.7% (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 1 gives population data for a Gulf state that has grown rapidly through oil wealth, construction and global finance (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1: Total population 10.2 million; international migrants (foreign-born residents) 8.8 million.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the total population who are international migrants. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for correct working and 1 mark for the correct answer.\n• Working: 8.8 ÷ 10.2 × 100 (1)\n• Answer: 86.3% (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 2 is a photograph of the Port of Felixstowe, Suffolk, the UK's busiest container port. It shows a very large container ship stacked many layers high with standard steel shipping containers, several tall gantry cranes lifting containers onto the quayside, and rows of lorries and a railway freight terminal behind the port.\n\nStudy Figure 2.\nSuggest one way in which developments in transport, such as those shown in Figure 2, have accelerated globalisation.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid suggestion and up to 2 further marks for developing it, to a maximum of 3.\nPossible suggestions:\n• Containerisation standardises cargo so it can be moved between ship, rail and road without unpacking (1) which cuts loading times and labour costs (1) so the cost of shipping goods long distances falls, making global supply chains profitable (1).\n• Larger container ships give economies of scale (1) so the transport cost per item is very low (1), allowing manufacturing to locate in low-wage countries such as China and still sell in the UK (1).\n• Integrated port, rail and road links (1) speed up the flow of products (1), supporting just-in-time production across countries (1).\nCredit other valid suggestions linked to the resource.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 2 is a bar chart showing personal remittances received as a percentage of GDP in 2022 for selected countries (data written for this practice question): Tajikistan 51%; Tonga 44%; Nepal 23%; Philippines 9%; India 3%; China 0.1%.\n\nStudy Figure 2.\nSuggest one reason why remittances form a large share of GDP in some countries.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid suggestion and up to 2 further marks for developing it, to a maximum of 3.\nPossible suggestions:\n• A large share of the working-age population has migrated overseas (1), for example from Tajikistan to Russia or Nepal to the Gulf states (1), because of a lack of well-paid jobs at home, so money sent back is large relative to the economy (1).\n• The home economy is small or has a low GDP (1) so even modest sums sent by migrants form a large percentage (1); Tonga has a very small population and economy (1).\n• Governments may encourage labour export (1), e.g. the Philippines trains workers for overseas jobs (1), as a deliberate development strategy (1).\nDo not credit simple description of the data.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 2 is a line graph showing share of world manufacturing output (%), 1990–2020 (data written for this practice question). The line for China rises from about 3% in 1990 to about 29% in 2020, overtaking the USA around 2010. The USA line falls gently from about 23% to about 17%. The UK line falls from about 5% to about 2%.\n\nStudy Figure 2.\nSuggest one reason for the change in China's share of world manufacturing output.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid suggestion and up to 2 further marks for developing it, to a maximum of 3.\nPossible suggestions:\n• China's 'open door' policy from 1978 and the creation of Special Economic Zones such as Shenzhen (1) attracted FDI from TNCs (1) through tax breaks and relaxed regulations (1).\n• A large supply of low-cost labour through rural–urban migration (1) reduced production costs (1) so TNCs offshored manufacturing to China (1).\n• China's accession to the WTO in 2001 (1) lowered barriers to its exports (1), giving access to global markets (1).\n• Heavy state investment in infrastructure, ports and power (1) ... (1)(1).` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. A student investigated whether there is a significant relationship between the level of globalisation and wealth. Figure 1 shows the ranks of 10 countries for KOF Globalisation Index score and GDP per capita (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 1 (country: KOF rank, GDP per capita rank) — A: 1, 2; B: 2, 1; C: 3, 4; D: 4, 3; E: 5, 6; F: 6, 5; G: 7, 9; H: 8, 7; I: 9, 8; J: 10, 10.\nFormula: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n)\n\nCalculate the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (rs) for this data. Give your answer to two decimal places. You must show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for each of the following:\n• Values of d calculated and squared: 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, 0 (1)\n• Σd² = 12 (1)\n• Substitution: 1 − (6 × 12) ÷ (1000 − 10) = 1 − 72 ÷ 990 (1)\n• rs = 0.93 (1)\nAllow follow-through from an incorrect Σd² for the final two marks.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. The AT Kearney/Foreign Policy Globalization Index (published 2001–2007) ranked countries using measures of economic integration, personal contact, technological connectivity and political engagement. In its rankings, small, wealthy states such as Singapore and Ireland were consistently placed near the top.\n\nExplain one reason why indices such as the AT Kearney index may not give an accurate picture of how globalised a country is.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explaining it, to a maximum of 4.\nPossible reasons:\n• Measures are relative to size (1): trade or FDI as a proportion of GDP favours small open economies such as Singapore (1), while very large economies such as the USA or China appear less globalised although they dominate global flows in absolute terms (1); so rank does not reflect global influence (1).\n• The choice and weighting of indicators is subjective (1) ...\n• Data quality varies between countries (1); informal flows such as undocumented migration or informal remittances are not recorded (1) ...\n• National averages hide internal variation (1): coastal cities may be highly connected while rural interiors are switched off (1) ...` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Shenzhen, in Guangdong province, China, was a small market town of about 30,000 people when it was designated one of China's first Special Economic Zones (SEZs) in 1980. It is now a megacity of over 17 million people and a global centre for electronics manufacturing.\n\nExplain one way in which special economic zones have accelerated globalisation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid way and up to 3 further marks for explaining it, to a maximum of 4.\nPossible ways:\n• SEZs offer incentives such as low tax, reduced tariffs and relaxed regulation (1) which attract FDI from TNCs (1) who build factories to make goods for export (1), increasing flows of capital and products between China and the rest of the world (1).\n• SEZs are often located on the coast with port and airport infrastructure (1) linking them into global supply chains (1) ...\n• SEZs attract internal migrants as a low-cost workforce (1) ...\nReference to Shenzhen or another SEZ (e.g. Gujarat, India; Jebel Ali, Dubai) may be credited as development.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Chad, a landlocked country in the Sahel region of Africa, has one of the lowest KOF Globalisation Index scores in the world and very low internet access.\n\nExplain one reason why some places remain 'switched off' from globalisation.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explaining it, to a maximum of 4.\nPossible reasons:\n• Physical isolation (1): landlocked countries such as Chad lack ports (1), increasing the cost of transporting goods to global markets (1), so TNCs are discouraged from investing (1).\n• Conflict and political instability (1) deter FDI and tourism (1) ...\n• Lack of infrastructure such as electricity and broadband (1) excludes people from information flows (1) ...\n• Political choice (1): North Korea deliberately restricts trade and information flows (1) ...\n• Harsh climate and environmental hazards such as drought and desertification (1) ...` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Since the 1990s, much of the world's manufacturing has relocated from developed countries to emerging economies such as China, India and Bangladesh. This is known as the global shift.\n\nExplain one environmental consequence of the global shift for a country receiving manufacturing industry.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid consequence and up to 3 further marks for explaining it, to a maximum of 4.\nPossible consequences:\n• Air pollution (1): rapid industrial growth in China has been powered largely by coal-fired power stations (1), producing smog in cities such as Beijing (1) with serious health effects for residents (1).\n• Water pollution (1): untreated industrial waste, e.g. dyes and chemicals from textile factories in Bangladesh, discharged into rivers (1) ...\n• Loss of land and ecosystems (1) as farmland and wetlands are converted to industrial zones and cities (1) ...\n• Increased carbon emissions (1) making the country a major emitter (1) ...` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 3 is a table showing KOF Globalisation Index sub-scores (0–100) for two countries (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 3 — Switzerland: economic 82, social 88, political 94, overall 90. Ethiopia: economic 38, social 30, political 72, overall 47.\n\nStudy Figure 3.\nExplain why the level of globalisation varies between these two countries.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Switzerland scores highly on all dimensions; Ethiopia scores much lower on economic and social dimensions but relatively high on political globalisation.\n• Economic: Switzerland is a global financial centre and home to TNCs such as Nestlé, with high trade and FDI; Ethiopia has a smaller, more agricultural economy with less trade and investment, although FDI in manufacturing parks has grown.\n• Social: Swiss high internet access, tourism, migration and international media; Ethiopia has low internet access and fewer international flows of information and people.\n• Political: Geneva hosts many IGOs (UN offices, WTO); Addis Ababa hosts the African Union headquarters, explaining Ethiopia's relatively high political score.\n• Wider factors: infrastructure, education and wealth levels, landlocked location.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge; limited use of Figure 3; explanation is generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates some accurate knowledge; makes some reference to the data; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding throughout; makes clear use of Figure 3; explanation is detailed and well developed across dimensions.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. In 2000, around 7% of the world's population used the internet; by 2023 this had risen to over 65%. Global foreign exchange trading now exceeds US$7 trillion per day.\n\nExplain how developments in communication technology and financial systems have accelerated globalisation.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• The internet, broadband and mobile phones allow instant flows of information, creating 'time–space compression' (a shrinking world).\n• TNCs can manage global production networks and supply chains in real time from headquarters.\n• E-commerce allows firms and consumers to trade across borders.\n• Services such as call centres and IT support can be offshored (e.g. to India).\n• Electronic trading and deregulated stock markets allow capital to move instantly between global financial centres such as London, New York and Tokyo.\n• Mobile money (e.g. M-Pesa in Kenya) brings people into the financial system and speeds up remittance flows.\n• Social media spreads cultural flows.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation of how technology affects flows.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed and may focus on only one of communication or finance.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed explanation of how both communication and financial developments increase flows of capital, information, products and services.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. McDonald's operates in over 100 countries. In India it does not sell beef burgers and its menu includes the McAloo Tikki (a spiced potato burger). In Japan it sells the Ebi Filet-O (a shrimp burger).\n\nExplain why transnational corporations (TNCs) use strategies of glocalisation.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Glocalisation means adapting global products or marketing to suit local tastes, cultures, religions or laws.\n• It increases sales and market share by appealing to local consumers, e.g. no beef in India because of Hindu beliefs.\n• It reduces the risk of cultural rejection or consumer boycotts.\n• It may be needed to meet national regulations on labelling, ingredients or advertising.\n• Local sourcing of ingredients can reduce costs and improve the TNC's image.\n• Allows TNCs to compete with local firms who understand the market.\n• Other examples: Coca-Cola local brands; car manufacturers adapting models; Netflix commissioning local content.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some explanation of reasons, partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed explanation of a range of reasons, supported by examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 3 shows the population of the city of Detroit, USA. Detroit was once the centre of the US car industry: 1950 1.85 million; 1980 1.20 million; 2000 0.95 million; 2020 0.64 million.\n\nStudy Figure 3.\nExplain how the global shift has contributed to the changes shown in Figure 3.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 3 shows a fall of about 1.2 million people (around two-thirds) since 1950.\n• Car manufacturing faced competition from cheaper imports (e.g. Japan, later South Korea) and car TNCs relocated production to lower-cost locations such as Mexico, aided by free trade agreements (NAFTA, 1994).\n• Deindustrialisation led to factory closures and unemployment; a negative multiplier effect hit suppliers and services.\n• Falling tax base reduced spending on services, leading to dereliction, crime and the city's bankruptcy in 2013, encouraging out-migration.\n• Other factors include automation and suburbanisation, so the global shift is not the only cause.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited reference to Figure 3.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some use of Figure 3; partly developed links to the global shift.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; clear use of Figure 3; detailed explanation of the links between global shift, deindustrialisation and population loss.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. After Poland and seven other central European countries joined the EU in 2004, the UK allowed their citizens to work in the UK immediately. By 2017 around one million Polish-born people lived in the UK.\n\nExplain why international migration can lead to tensions in host communities.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Perceived competition for jobs, especially low-skilled work, and fears of wage suppression.\n• Pressure on housing, school places and health services in areas of rapid arrival (e.g. Boston, Lincolnshire).\n• Cultural differences in language, religion and lifestyle; concerns about loss of identity and integration.\n• Media and political narratives can amplify concerns, contributing to support for policies such as Brexit (2016).\n• Segregation and limited contact between groups can reinforce prejudice.\n• Tensions may be greater in deprived areas that feel 'left behind' by globalisation.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed explanation of economic, social and cultural reasons, with examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 3 shows the Gini coefficient for China, a measure of income inequality where 0 is perfect equality and 1 is complete inequality (approximate values written for this practice question): 1985 0.30; 1995 0.36; 2005 0.42; 2010 0.44; 2020 0.38.\n\nStudy Figure 3.\nExplain why globalisation has affected the level of inequality within emerging economies such as China.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 3 shows inequality rising rapidly from 1985 to a peak around 2010, then falling slightly by 2020.\n• FDI and export manufacturing concentrated in coastal SEZs and cities (Shenzhen, Shanghai), so incomes there rose much faster than in the rural interior.\n• Skilled workers, entrepreneurs and those with capital gained most; rural farmers gained least.\n• The hukou system limited migrants' access to urban services, widening the gap.\n• The later fall may reflect rising wages, government investment in western provinces (e.g. 'Go West' policy), poverty-reduction programmes and remittances to rural areas.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited reference to Figure 3.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some use of Figure 3; partly developed explanation.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; clear use of Figure 3 including the change in trend; detailed explanation.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Fairtrade-certified bananas are sold in most UK supermarkets. Producers receive a guaranteed Fairtrade Minimum Price and an additional Fairtrade Premium that farmer co-operatives spend on community projects.\n\nExplain how fair trade schemes attempt to reduce the negative impacts of globalisation on producers in developing countries.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Global commodity prices are volatile and dominated by powerful TNCs and supermarkets, so small producers receive a small share of the final price.\n• A minimum price provides a safety net when world prices fall, giving income security.\n• The premium funds schools, clinics, clean water or improved farming methods, decided democratically by co-operatives.\n• Standards on working conditions, child labour and environmental practices address ethical concerns about supply chains.\n• Long-term trading relationships and access to credit give stability.\n• Raises consumer awareness of ethical consumption.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed explanation linking the features of fair trade to the specific problems created by globalisation.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 3 is a table showing the results of a survey of 200 shoppers at a farmers' market in a small English market town (data written for this practice question). Main reason for buying locally produced food: supporting local farmers and businesses 41%; fewer food miles 27%; freshness and quality 22%; knowing how the food is produced 10%.\n\nStudy Figure 3.\nExplain why local sourcing of food may be considered more sustainable than relying on global food supply chains.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 3 shows economic reasons (supporting local businesses, 41%) as most important, followed by environmental (food miles, 27%).\n• Economic: money circulates in the local economy (local multiplier), supporting jobs and reducing leakage to TNCs.\n• Environmental: fewer food miles and less packaging can reduce carbon emissions, though seasonal and transport mode matter (e.g. heated UK greenhouses may emit more than imports).\n• Social: builds community links and trust; knowing how food is produced.\n• Resilience: less dependent on global supply chains, which were disrupted during COVID-19.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited reference to Figure 3.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some use of Figure 3; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; clear use of Figure 3; detailed explanation across dimensions of sustainability.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. China's 'Great Firewall' blocks access to Google, Facebook and many foreign news websites. France requires that at least 40% of songs played on its radio stations are in French.\n\nExplain why some countries attempt to restrict the flows associated with globalisation.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• To protect national culture, language and identity from westernisation/Americanisation (e.g. French music quotas).\n• Political control: authoritarian governments restrict information flows to limit criticism and dissent (China, North Korea).\n• Economic protectionism: tariffs and quotas protect domestic industries and jobs from cheaper imports (e.g. US tariffs on Chinese steel and goods from 2018).\n• Restricting migration to respond to public concern about jobs, services and security (e.g. UK points-based system after Brexit).\n• National security concerns about foreign ownership of strategic infrastructure or technology (e.g. restrictions on Huawei).\n• Supporting domestic tech firms (Baidu, WeChat in China).\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed explanation of a range of reasons, supported by examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. In April 2013 the Rana Plaza building near Dhaka, Bangladesh, collapsed. It housed garment factories producing clothing for European and North American retailers, and more than 1,100 workers were killed. Figure 3 shows the approximate breakdown of the retail price of a £10 T-shirt sold in the UK (data written for this practice question): retailer 59%; brand 12%; transport and duties 10%; materials 13%; factory overheads and profit 5%; garment worker wages 1%.\n\nStudy Figure 3.\nExplain the ethical concerns raised by global supply chains.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Figure 3 shows workers receive about 1% (10p) of the retail price while retailers and brands take over 70%.\n• Low wages and long hours: TNCs and retailers seek the cheapest producers, creating a 'race to the bottom'.\n• Unsafe working conditions and weak enforcement of building and safety regulations (Rana Plaza).\n• Complex subcontracting makes it hard to trace conditions, allowing TNCs to avoid responsibility.\n• Restrictions on trade unions; child labour or forced labour in some supply chains (e.g. cotton).\n• Environmental exploitation (pollution from dyeing) may also be ethical issues.\n• Responses: Bangladesh Accord on Fire and Building Safety (2013), consumer pressure.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited reference to Figure 3.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some use of Figure 3; partly developed explanation.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; clear use of Figure 3; detailed explanation of a range of ethical concerns.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Average tariffs on manufactured goods traded between developed countries fell from around 40% in 1947 to under 4% by the 2000s, through rounds of negotiation under the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT) and its successor, the World Trade Organization (WTO).\n\nExplain the role of the WTO, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Bank in promoting free trade and the flow of capital.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• WTO (1995): negotiates reductions in tariffs, quotas and subsidies; settles trade disputes; membership (e.g. China 2001) opens markets.\n• IMF: provides loans and stability in financial crises, often with conditions requiring liberalisation, privatisation and openness to foreign investment.\n• World Bank: funds infrastructure and development projects that connect countries to global markets; historically Structural Adjustment Programmes encouraged export-led growth.\n• Together they promote a neoliberal model ('Washington Consensus') of free markets, deregulation and privatisation.\n• This increased flows of products, services and capital and encouraged FDI by TNCs.\n• Criticisms: rules may favour developed countries; conditions could increase poverty in the short term.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge; limited explanation of the roles.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge of at least two organisations; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge of all three organisations; well-developed explanation of how each promotes free trade and capital flows.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. An Apple iPhone is designed in California, uses components made in Japan, South Korea, Taiwan and the USA, and has largely been assembled by contract manufacturers such as Foxconn in China, with assembly increasingly also taking place in India.\n\nExplain how TNCs use global production networks and supply chains to increase their profits.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Global production networks split production into stages located where each is cheapest or most efficient (spatial division of labour).\n• Research and design in high-skill developed locations; assembly offshored to low-wage locations.\n• Outsourcing to contract manufacturers reduces capital costs and risk.\n• Economies of scale and just-in-time logistics reduce stockholding costs.\n• Locating in SEZs or low-tax countries reduces costs; transfer pricing and tax havens (e.g. Ireland) reduce tax.\n• Access to new markets close to production (e.g. China's growing middle class).\n• Diversifying suppliers (moving some assembly to India, Vietnam) reduces risk from trade tensions or disruption.\n• Marketing and global branding allow high prices.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed, may lack range.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge; well-developed explanation of a range of strategies, with examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. China's urban population rose from about 20% of the total in 1980 to over 60% by 2020. Many of the migrants moved from inland provinces such as Sichuan and Henan to coastal cities in Guangdong.\n\nExplain how globalisation has contributed to rural–urban migration within emerging economies.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• FDI and export manufacturing created large numbers of jobs in coastal cities and SEZs (pull factor).\n• Urban wages several times higher than rural incomes.\n• Rural push factors: low farm incomes, mechanisation, limited services; global competition in agricultural markets.\n• Improved transport and communication spread information about urban opportunities; remittances from early migrants encourage chain migration.\n• Government policy (relaxation of hukou restrictions) allowed movement.\n• Similar patterns elsewhere: Dhaka garment industry, Indian IT cities such as Bengaluru.\n• Consequences: rapid urban growth, informal housing, 'left behind' elderly and children in rural areas.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge; well-developed explanation linking global economic flows to migration, with examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Bhutan allowed television and the internet for the first time in 1999. Within a few years, international channels, Western fashions and fast food had become popular among young people in the capital, Thimphu.\n\nExplain how cultural globalisation can lead to a loss of cultural diversity.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Cultural globalisation: flows of ideas, values, media, language and consumer brands, often dominated by Western (especially American) sources.\n• Global media, film, music and social media spread a westernised consumer lifestyle; the English language dominates the internet.\n• Global brands (McDonald's, Coca-Cola, Starbucks) replace local businesses, creating 'clone towns' and homogenised landscapes.\n• Minority languages decline; many of the world's languages are endangered.\n• Indigenous cultures (e.g. Inuit, Amazonian peoples) are eroded by contact, tourism, resource extraction and migration of young people.\n• Diet, dress and religious practices change, especially among the young, creating generational tensions.\n• Counter-trend: glocalisation and cultural hybridity mean influences are not one-way.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge; well-developed explanation of the processes that lead to cultural homogenisation, with examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. China became the world's largest emitter of carbon dioxide in about 2006. In January 2013 air pollution in Beijing reached levels many times above the World Health Organization's safe limit for fine particulate matter (PM2.5).\n\nExplain the environmental consequences of rapid economic development in emerging economies.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Air pollution from coal-fired power and heavy industry; health impacts such as respiratory disease.\n• Rising greenhouse gas emissions contribute to global climate change.\n• Water pollution from industrial and agricultural waste; water scarcity from over-abstraction.\n• Land degradation, deforestation and loss of biodiversity as land is converted for industry, cities and export crops (e.g. soya and cattle in Brazil, palm oil in Indonesia).\n• Large infrastructure such as the Three Gorges Dam altering river systems.\n• Growing consumption by a new middle class increases waste and resource use.\n• Some emerging economies are now investing heavily in renewables (China leads in solar).\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge; well-developed explanation of a range of environmental consequences, with examples.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Totnes in Devon became the UK's first 'Transition Town' in 2006. Projects included community energy schemes, local food growing, a local currency (the Totnes Pound) and campaigns to support independent shops.\n\nExplain how transition towns attempt to respond to the impacts of globalisation.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks)\nIndicative content:\n• Transition towns are community-led movements aiming to increase local resilience and reduce dependence on fossil fuels and global supply chains.\n• Local food production (community gardens, farmers' markets) reduces food miles and supports local farmers.\n• Local currencies keep spending within the local economy and support independent businesses rather than TNC chains, protecting local character against 'clone towns'.\n• Community energy (solar co-operatives) reduces carbon emissions and energy imports.\n• Skill-sharing and repair cafés reduce consumption and waste.\n• Builds social capital and local identity.\n• Limitations: small scale, depends on volunteers, often in affluent communities; the Totnes Pound ended in 2019.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; explanation partly developed.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate and detailed knowledge; well-developed explanation of how a range of transition initiatives respond to specific impacts of globalisation.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 4 shows selected data for Shenzhen SEZ, China (approximate values written for this practice question): population 1980 0.03 million, 2000 7 million, 2020 17.6 million; GDP 1980 US$0.03 billion, 2020 US$430 billion; share of China's exports passing through Shenzhen port in 2020 about 10%.\n\nStudy Figure 4.\nAssess the importance of special economic zones in accelerating globalisation in China.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nIndicative content:\nImportance of SEZs:\n• Figure 4 shows extraordinary growth in population and GDP, and a significant share of exports.\n• SEZs (Shenzhen, Zhuhai, Shantou, Xiamen 1980; Hainan 1988) attracted FDI through tax incentives and relaxed rules, and acted as testing grounds for market reforms later extended across China.\n• Linked China into global production networks (Foxconn, electronics).\nOther factors may be more important:\n• Wider 'open door' policy and Deng Xiaoping's reforms from 1978.\n• WTO membership in 2001 gave access to global markets.\n• Huge low-cost workforce from rural–urban migration.\n• State investment in infrastructure, ports and education; later Belt and Road Initiative.\n• Global factors: containerisation, TNCs' search for low costs.\nA good assessment reaches a supported conclusion about the relative importance of SEZs compared with other political and economic decisions.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 4; judgement absent or unsupported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some interpretation of evidence; an unbalanced or partial assessment with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; interpretation of evidence is mostly relevant; a partially balanced assessment and a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; full and coherent interpretation of Figure 4 and other evidence; a balanced assessment with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. In 2023 the 100 largest non-financial TNCs together held assets of over US$15 trillion, and several, such as Apple, Microsoft and Saudi Aramco, had annual revenues greater than the GDP of many countries.\n\nAssess the extent to which TNCs are the main drivers of globalisation.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nIndicative content:\nTNCs as main drivers:\n• Global production networks and supply chains create flows of components, products and capital.\n• FDI into emerging economies; offshoring and outsourcing.\n• Global marketing and brands drive cultural globalisation; glocalisation spreads products.\n• Technology TNCs (Google, Meta) drive information flows.\nOther drivers:\n• Governments and political decisions: free-trade policies, trade blocs (EU, ASEAN), SEZs, privatisation.\n• IGOs: WTO, IMF and World Bank.\n• Technological developments: containerisation, internet, financial systems — though many created by TNCs.\n• Migrants and diasporas creating flows of labour and remittances; consumers' demand.\nJudgement: TNCs are powerful agents but operate within frameworks set by governments and IGOs and depend on technology.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; judgement absent or unsupported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced assessment and a partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; balanced assessment of TNCs against other drivers with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. In 2023, migrants sent an estimated US$650 billion in remittances to low- and middle-income countries, more than the total of official development aid and FDI to those countries combined (excluding China).\n\nWith reference to named examples, assess the positive and negative impacts of international migration on source countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nIndicative content:\nPositive impacts:\n• Remittances raise household incomes and fund education, healthcare and housing (e.g. Philippines, Nepal, India).\n• Reduces unemployment and pressure on resources.\n• Return migrants bring skills, savings and ideas; diaspora investment and trade links.\nNegative impacts:\n• Brain drain of skilled workers (e.g. doctors and nurses from Zimbabwe, the Philippines or Ghana).\n• Demographic imbalance: loss of young working-age people, ageing population (e.g. Poland, Lithuania), family separation.\n• Dependence on remittances leaves economies vulnerable to recession in host countries.\n• Rural areas may be depopulated; farmland abandoned.\nJudgement may depend on the scale and skill level of migration, whether migration is temporary or permanent, and the scale considered (household vs national).\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; judgement absent or unsupported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced assessment and a partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; balanced assessment of both positive and negative impacts using named examples, with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 4 shows retail sales of Fairtrade-certified products in the UK (approximate values written for this practice question): 2000 £33 million; 2008 £700 million; 2014 £1.6 billion; 2022 £2.0 billion. In 2022 this was still under 2% of all UK grocery sales.\n\nStudy Figure 4.\nAssess the effectiveness of fair trade in reducing the inequalities created by globalisation.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nIndicative content:\nEffective:\n• Figure 4 shows rapid growth in sales, so more producers benefit.\n• Minimum price and premium give income security and fund community projects.\n• Improves labour standards and empowers co-operatives; raises consumer awareness and pressures TNCs.\nLimited effectiveness:\n• Very small share of the market (under 2% of UK grocery sales); sales growth has slowed.\n• Only a fraction of producers' output may be sold on Fairtrade terms.\n• Certification costs can exclude the poorest farmers; the main beneficiaries may be better-organised co-operatives.\n• Does not change structural inequalities such as trade rules, tariffs or TNC power; competition from corporate ethical schemes.\n• Most of the retail price still goes to retailers.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 4; judgement absent or unsupported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; some interpretation; partial assessment with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; mostly relevant interpretation; partially balanced assessment and a partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; full interpretation of Figure 4; balanced assessment with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Between 1997 and 2010 the UK lost around 1.7 million manufacturing jobs, while employment in finance, business services and the creative industries in London and the South East grew rapidly.\n\nAssess the extent to which the global shift has created more losers than winners in developed countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nIndicative content:\nLosers:\n• Deindustrialised regions such as the US Rust Belt (Detroit), South Wales, North East England: unemployment, negative multiplier, dereliction, social problems.\n• Low-skilled manual workers face job losses and wage stagnation; regional inequality widens.\nWinners:\n• Consumers benefit from cheaper imported goods.\n• Global cities (London, New York) and knowledge-economy workers gain from finance and services.\n• TNC shareholders gain from lower costs; new jobs in tertiary and quaternary sectors.\n• Some regions have regenerated (e.g. Salford Quays, Manchester; Pittsburgh's shift to health and education).\nJudgement may depend on the scale (national vs regional), timescale, and group of people considered.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated knowledge; judgement absent or unsupported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some accurate knowledge; partial assessment with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partially balanced assessment and a partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; balanced assessment of winners and losers with examples, and a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. The cost of a three-minute telephone call from London to New York fell from over US$300 in 1930 (at today's prices) to almost nothing by the 2010s. Over the same period, many countries joined trade blocs and the WTO, and China and India opened their economies to foreign investment.\n\nEvaluate the view that technological developments, rather than political and economic decisions, have been the main cause of the acceleration of globalisation.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nTechnological developments:\n• Transport: containerisation, jet aircraft, bulk carriers — reduced cost of moving goods and people.\n• Communications: internet, mobile phones, satellites — instant information flows, time–space compression.\n• Financial systems: electronic trading, mobile money.\n• Allowed TNCs to manage global production networks.\nPolitical and economic decisions:\n• Free-trade policies, GATT/WTO tariff cuts, trade blocs (EU single market, NAFTA/USMCA, ASEAN).\n• Deregulation and privatisation (e.g. 1986 'Big Bang' in London), opening of China (1978, SEZs) and India (1991 reforms).\n• IMF and World Bank policies; government incentives for FDI.\nEvaluation:\n• Technology makes globalisation possible but political decisions determine whether it happens (e.g. North Korea has access to technology but chooses isolation).\n• Recent protectionism (US–China tariffs, Brexit) shows political decisions can slow globalisation despite technology.\n• The factors are interdependent; relative importance varies by time period and place.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; isolated interpretation; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; limited interpretation; unbalanced argument with a generic or unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; a reasonably balanced argument and a conclusion partly supported by evidence.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed and coherent evaluation of both groups of causes; a balanced argument with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 5 shows GDP per capita (current US$) for three countries (approximate values written for this practice question): China 1990 320, 2022 12,700; Bangladesh 1990 300, 2022 2,700; Niger 1990 330, 2022 590. In 2022 the UK's GDP per capita was about 46,000.\n\nUsing Figure 5 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that globalisation has narrowed the development gap.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nNarrowed:\n• Figure 5 shows China's GDP per capita rose about 40-fold and Bangladesh's about 9-fold, as emerging economies were integrated into global trade.\n• Hundreds of millions lifted out of extreme poverty (especially in China and India); HDI improvements; NICs (South Korea, Taiwan) now high-income.\n• Technology transfer, FDI and remittances spread wealth.\nNot narrowed / widened:\n• Niger has barely changed: switched-off countries, especially in sub-Saharan Africa, have been left behind.\n• The absolute gap with the UK has grown even where relative growth is rapid.\n• Inequality within countries has often increased (China's Gini coefficient rose; Rust Belt decline in the USA).\n• Wealth concentrated in TNCs and elites; terms of trade disadvantage primary producers.\nEvaluation: the answer depends on the measure (absolute vs relative; income vs HDI), the scale (between vs within countries) and which countries are considered.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; isolated use of Figure 5; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; limited interpretation; unbalanced argument with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; reasonably balanced argument and partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; full and coherent interpretation of Figure 5 and own knowledge; balanced argument with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. K-pop bands from South Korea have topped charts in the USA and Europe, Bollywood films are watched across Africa and the Middle East, and Starbucks has over 6,000 stores in China.\n\nEvaluate the view that cultural globalisation has led to the homogenisation of cultures around the world.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nHomogenisation:\n• Spread of Western, especially American, culture through global media, brands and the English language (westernisation/Americanisation).\n• Global brands and 'clone towns'; similar urban landscapes and consumer lifestyles.\n• Decline of minority languages and indigenous cultures (e.g. Amazonian groups, Inuit).\nAgainst homogenisation:\n• Cultural flows are multi-directional: K-pop, Bollywood, Japanese anime, world foods in the UK.\n• Glocalisation adapts global products to local cultures (McDonald's in India).\n• Hybridisation creates new cultural forms.\n• Resistance and protection of culture: French language quotas, revival of Welsh, indigenous rights movements; restrictions in China, Iran, North Korea.\n• Globalisation can raise awareness of and support for threatened cultures (e.g. through tourism and social media).\nEvaluation: some convergence in consumer culture, but deeper values, religion and identity persist; the degree varies by place, age group and urban/rural location.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; unbalanced argument with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; reasonably balanced argument and partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed evaluation with examples from contrasting places; balanced argument with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. International shipping alone produces close to 3% of global greenhouse gas emissions. Meanwhile, world trade in goods grew from about US$2 trillion in 1980 to about US$25 trillion in 2022.\n\nEvaluate the view that the environmental costs of globalisation outweigh its economic benefits.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nEnvironmental costs:\n• Emissions from transport (shipping, aviation) and from industrialisation in emerging economies; contribution to climate change.\n• Pollution of air and water (China, Bangladesh textiles); 'pollution havens' with weak regulation.\n• Deforestation for export commodities (palm oil, soya, beef in the Amazon); biodiversity loss.\n• Increased consumption and waste; e-waste exported to Ghana (Agbogbloshie).\nEconomic benefits:\n• Huge growth in trade and GDP; poverty reduction in Asia; jobs from FDI.\n• Cheaper goods for consumers; access to technology.\nEvaluation:\n• Benefits and costs fall unevenly: environmental costs often borne by poor communities and future generations; benefits concentrated among TNCs and consumers.\n• Globalisation also spreads green technology (cheap Chinese solar panels) and international agreements (Paris 2015).\n• Responses such as local sourcing and carbon pricing may reduce costs.\n• Judgement depends on timescale, scale and how costs are valued.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; unbalanced argument with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; reasonably balanced argument and partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed evaluation weighing costs against benefits with examples; well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. There are now several hundred Transition initiatives worldwide, and many UK towns have farmers' markets and 'buy local' campaigns. However, the UK still imports around 40% of the food it eats.\n\nEvaluate the view that local responses can be effective in managing the negative consequences of globalisation.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nEffective:\n• Transition towns (Totnes): community energy, local food, local currencies, repair cafés increase resilience and reduce emissions.\n• Local sourcing keeps money in local economies and protects independent shops and local identity.\n• Recycling and reuse schemes reduce waste.\n• Ethical consumption by individuals (fair trade) can influence TNCs.\nLimitations:\n• Small scale compared with global flows; UK still dependent on imports.\n• Often in affluent communities; local food can be more expensive; reliance on volunteers.\n• The Totnes Pound ended in 2019.\n• Global problems such as climate change and supply chain exploitation need national and international action: legislation (UK Modern Slavery Act 2015), WTO rules, Paris Agreement, the Bangladesh Accord.\nEvaluation: local responses are most effective when combined with national and global action; they may raise awareness and change behaviour more than they change global structures.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; unbalanced argument with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; reasonably balanced argument and partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed evaluation of local responses against other scales of response; well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Figure 5 is a table of KOF Globalisation Index scores (0–100) for selected places in 2000 and 2020 (data written for this practice question): Netherlands 86, 90; Singapore 75, 83; China 50, 64; Vietnam 45, 67; Kenya 45, 52; Chad 32, 37.\n\nUsing Figure 5 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that the winners from globalisation are mainly places that were already wealthy and well connected.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nAgree:\n• Figure 5: Netherlands and Singapore were already highly globalised in 2000 and remain at the top.\n• Global hub cities (London, New York, Singapore) dominate finance, TNC headquarters and innovation; cumulative causation reinforces existing advantage.\n• Switched-off places such as Chad have changed little (32 to 37).\nDisagree:\n• Vietnam (+22) and China (+14) show the largest gains: emerging economies have been major winners through FDI, SEZs and export manufacturing.\n• Some formerly wealthy places are losers: deindustrialised regions in developed countries (Rust Belt, North East England).\n• Winners within poorer countries: coastal cities, skilled workers, the new middle class (e.g. Bengaluru).\nEvaluation: 'winners' and 'losers' exist at every scale; existing wealth and connectivity are advantages, but political decisions, location and labour costs have allowed new winners to emerge.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; isolated use of Figure 5; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; limited interpretation; unbalanced argument with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; reasonably balanced argument and partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; full and coherent interpretation of Figure 5 and own knowledge; balanced argument with a well-supported conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section A — Globalisation. Globalisation has been linked to the collapse of the Rana Plaza garment factory in Bangladesh (2013), public concern about immigration in the UK before the 2016 EU referendum, and deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon for soya and cattle exports.\n\nEvaluate the view that the social tensions caused by globalisation are more significant than the environmental tensions.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nIndicative content:\nSocial tensions:\n• Migration-related tensions in host countries (competition for jobs, services, cultural change); rise of nationalism and protectionism (Brexit, US tariffs).\n• Exploitation of workers in global supply chains (Rana Plaza); ethical concerns.\n• Inequality between and within countries; 'left behind' places.\n• Cultural erosion and conflict between global and local values; indigenous peoples displaced.\nEnvironmental tensions:\n• Climate change from transport and industrial emissions — global, long-term and potentially irreversible.\n• Deforestation, pollution, resource depletion, e-waste; conflicts over land in the Amazon.\n• Environmental tensions also cause social impacts (health, displacement), so the two are linked.\nEvaluation: significance depends on timescale (environmental impacts may be longer-lasting), scale, and who is affected; social tensions may be more politically visible in the short term.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated knowledge; argument unsupported, judgement absent.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some accurate knowledge; unbalanced argument with a generic conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge; partial evaluation; reasonably balanced argument and partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge throughout; detailed evaluation of both types of tension, with examples; well-supported conclusion.` }
    ]
  },
  'T6': {
    name: 'Diverse Places',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 1 shows the population of a market town in eastern England that has received large numbers of migrants from EU accession states since 2004 (data written for this practice question).\nPopulation, 2001: 55,750\nPopulation, 2021: 70,500\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in the population of the town between 2001 and 2021. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (70,500 − 55,750) ÷ 55,750 × 100 (= 14,750 ÷ 55,750 × 100)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 26.5%\nAward 2 marks for 26.5% with no working only if the method is clearly implied; 26.46% (not to 1 d.p.) scores 1 mark.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 1 shows the age structure of a rural ward on the North Norfolk coast (data written for this practice question).\nAged 0–15: 1,240\nAged 16–64: 4,860\nAged 65 and over: 3,150\n\nCalculate the dependency ratio for this ward using the formula:\ndependency ratio = (population aged 0–15 + population aged 65 and over) ÷ population aged 16–64 × 100\nGive your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: (1,240 + 3,150) ÷ 4,860 × 100 (= 4,390 ÷ 4,860 × 100)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 90.3` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 1 shows census data for an inner London borough (data written for this practice question).\nTotal usual resident population: 312,000\nResidents aged 65 and over: 18,700\n\nCalculate the percentage of the borough's population aged 65 and over. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct working: 18,700 ÷ 312,000 × 100\n1 mark for the correct answer: 6.0%` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 1 shows the percentage of residents born outside the UK in 11 wards of a large English city, arranged in rank order (data written for this practice question):\n8, 12, 15, 19, 23, 27, 31, 36, 41, 48, 55\n\nCalculate the interquartile range of the data. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying the quartiles: lower quartile = 15 (3rd value, (n + 1) ÷ 4) and upper quartile = 41 (9th value, 3(n + 1) ÷ 4)\n1 mark for the correct answer: 41 − 15 = 26 (percentage points)` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 2 is a population pyramid for Tower Hamlets, an inner London borough, from the 2021 Census. It shows a very large bulge of males and females aged 25–39, a relatively wide base of children aged 0–9, and very narrow bars for all age groups over 65.\n\nStudy Figure 2.\nSuggest one reason for the large proportion of the population aged 25–39.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, +1 for development, +1 for further development (max 3). For example:\n• Young adults move into the borough from elsewhere in the UK and abroad for employment (1) because it is close to major employment centres such as the City of London and Canary Wharf (1) with many graduate jobs in finance and professional services (1).\n• Inner-city housing is dominated by rented flats (1) which suit single people and couples without children (1), whereas families often move out to the suburbs when they need more space (1).\n• International migrants tend to be young working-age adults (1), so past and present migration flows (e.g. from Bangladesh and the EU) (1) add mainly to the 25–39 age group (1).` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 2 shows information about a neighbourhood (LSOA) in Middlesbrough. It is in the most deprived 1% of neighbourhoods in England on the Index of Multiple Deprivation (IMD). An oral history recorded with a resident who has lived there for 40 years describes the area as 'a close community where everyone looks out for each other'.\n\nSuggest one reason why the IMD may not fully represent the lived experience of residents in this neighbourhood.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, +1 for development, +1 for further development (max 3). For example:\n• The IMD only measures quantitative deprivation domains such as income, employment and health (1) and does not record qualitative factors such as sense of community or belonging (1), so residents with strong social networks may experience the area more positively than its ranking suggests (1).\n• The IMD is an average for the whole LSOA (1), which hides variation between individuals and households (1), e.g. long-term residents who own their homes may experience less hardship than newer tenants (1).\n• The IMD is a snapshot based on data collected at one time (1) so may not reflect recent change (1) such as regeneration or new community projects (1).` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 2 shows that in a coastal village in North Norfolk, 41% of residents are aged 65 and over, compared with about 19% for England as a whole (data written for this practice question).\n\nSuggest one reason why the proportion of older people in this village is so high.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `1 mark for a valid reason, +1 for development, +1 for further development (max 3). For example:\n• Retirement in-migration (1): older people from cities are attracted by the perceived 'rural idyll', quiet environment and coastal scenery (1) and often have equity from selling a house in the South East to buy property here (1).\n• Out-migration of young adults (1) because of limited higher education, job opportunities and poor public transport (1), and high house prices driven by second homes make it hard for young people to stay (1).\n• Low birth rates (1) because there are few people of child-bearing age (1), so the population 'ages in place' (1).` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. A student investigating how Brixton in south London is represented collected 50 Instagram and X (Twitter) posts tagged #Brixton from a single weekend in July.\n\nExplain one limitation of using social media posts to represent the character of a place.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a limitation, +1 for each development of the explanation (max 4). For example:\n• Social media users are not representative of the whole population (1): they are mostly younger people (1), so the views of older long-term residents, such as the Caribbean community that settled after Windrush, are under-represented (1). This gives a biased picture that may emphasise nightlife and food markets rather than everyday concerns such as rising rents (1).\n• Posts are selective and curated (1); people post attractive or dramatic images (1)...\n• A single summer weekend is a small, time-specific sample (1)...\nOnly one limitation should be credited; do not credit a list.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. The UK Census is carried out every ten years and is often used to study the ethnic composition of places.\n\nExplain one reason why census data may not give an accurate picture of the cultural diversity of a place.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason, +1 for each development (max 4). For example:\n• Ethnic group is self-identified using fixed categories (1), so people may choose broad options such as 'White Other' or 'Mixed' (1) which hide important differences, e.g. between Polish, Romanian and Portuguese residents (1); it also records ethnicity rather than lived culture, language use or religious practice (1).\n• The census is only every ten years (1) so it quickly becomes out of date in places with rapid migration (1), e.g. after EU enlargement in 2004 (1) or the arrival of migrants from Hong Kong and Ukraine after 2021 (1).\n• Some groups are under-counted (1), e.g. undocumented migrants or people in multiple-occupancy housing (1)...` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. A student investigated whether there is a relationship between the percentage of residents born outside the UK and the percentage of housing that is privately rented in 10 wards of Leicester. The ranks were (data written for this practice question):\nWard: rank % born outside UK / rank % privately rented\nA: 1 / 1\nB: 2 / 3\nC: 3 / 2\nD: 4 / 6\nE: 5 / 4\nF: 6 / 5\nG: 7 / 9\nH: 8 / 7\nI: 9 / 10\nJ: 10 / 8\nSpearman's rank formula: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n)\nCritical value for n = 10 at the 0.05 significance level: 0.564\n\nCalculate the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient for this data and state whether the relationship is significant at the 0.05 level. You must show your working.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `1 mark for correct d² values / Σd² = 18 (d² = 0, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, 4)\n1 mark for correct substitution: rs = 1 − (6 × 18) ÷ (1000 − 10) = 1 − 108 ÷ 990\n1 mark for correct answer: rs = 0.89 (0.891)\n1 mark for correct conclusion: 0.89 is greater than the critical value of 0.564, so there is a significant positive relationship at the 0.05 level (the null hypothesis can be rejected).` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 3 shows the ethnic composition (%) of two contrasting places in England, 2021 (data written for this practice question).\nEthnic group: Place X (inner-city ward, Birmingham) / Place Y (rural parish, Cumbria)\nWhite British: 22 / 96\nOther White: 6 / 2\nAsian or Asian British: 48 / 1\nBlack or Black British: 14 / 0\nMixed / multiple: 6 / 1\nOther: 4 / 0\n\nAnalyse the differences in ethnic composition between Place X and Place Y.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Up to 4 marks for analysis that uses the data and identifies/explains the contrasts (1 mark per valid point; max 2 for description only without data or reasoning). For example:\n• Place Y is almost entirely White British (96%) whereas Place X has only 22% White British, a difference of 74 percentage points (1).\n• Asian or Asian British is the largest group in Place X (48%) but only 1% in Place Y (1), reflecting past chain migration from South Asia to inner Birmingham where cheap terraced housing and factory jobs were available (1).\n• Place X has notable Black (14%) and Mixed (6%) populations compared with almost none in Place Y (1), showing that diversity is concentrated in large urban areas with long histories of international migration and established networks (1).\n• Both places have small 'Other White' populations (6% and 2%), suggesting recent EU migration has reached rural areas too, e.g. for farm and tourism work (1).` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. In 2016, residents of St Ives in Cornwall voted in a neighbourhood plan referendum, with about 83% in favour of restricting new-build homes to people who would live in them as their main residence.\n\nExplain one reason why second homes can cause tensions in rural communities.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `1 mark for identifying a reason, +1 for each development (max 4). For example:\n• Second homes increase demand for a limited housing stock (1), pushing up house prices well above what local people on low, seasonal wages in tourism can afford (1), so young local families are forced to move away (1), causing resentment between long-term residents and wealthy incomers (1).\n• Second homes are empty for much of the year (1), so year-round demand for services falls (1) and village shops, pubs, schools and bus services close (1), leaving permanent residents, especially the elderly, more isolated (1).` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 4 describes two population pyramids from the 2021 Census. Pyramid A (Newham, east London) has a wide base, a very large bulge in the 20–44 age groups and very narrow bars above age 65. Pyramid B (Rother, East Sussex) has a narrow base, narrow bars for ages 20–34 and wide bars for all age groups between 55 and 79.\n\nExplain the differences in the population structures of these two places.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Newham: in-migration of young working-age adults from the UK and abroad attracted by jobs in London, universities and relatively cheap rented housing; high proportion of international migrants, who are mostly young; higher fertility in some ethnic groups (e.g. Bangladeshi, Pakistani, Black African) contributes to the wide base.\n• Rother: retirement in-migration to the coast and countryside; out-migration of young adults for higher education and jobs; few young adults means low birth rates and a narrow base; longer life expectancy in less deprived areas widens the older bars.\n• Application: the contrasts reflect urban–rural differences in employment, housing type/cost and the perceptions of each place (vibrant city vs rural idyll).\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated knowledge, limited application; few links between factors and pyramid shapes.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some accurate understanding of migration and natural change, partially applied to the two pyramids.\nLevel 3 (5–6): accurate, well-developed explanation of the factors, applied clearly to both pyramids with logical connections.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 4 shows extracts from two interviews recorded in Southall, west London. Interviewee 1, a 72-year-old Sikh man who arrived in 1966: 'When I came there were very few of us. Now this is home – the gurdwara, the shops on the Broadway, everything.' Interviewee 2, a 19-year-old student who moved to the area last year: 'It's busy and the food is great, but I don't really know anyone outside college.'\n\nExplain how length of residence and age can influence people's lived experience of a place.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Long-term residents have deep attachment and a strong sense of place built through memories, family ties and social networks; they may see change (e.g. new migrant groups, gentrification) as loss, or have pride in having built the community (e.g. Southall's Punjabi institutions such as the Sri Guru Singh Sabha gurdwara).\n• Newcomers may have weaker social ties and see the place more in terms of its amenities and services; their experience is shaped by their reasons for moving (study, work).\n• Age affects how space is used: young people use leisure spaces, nightlife and online networks; older people rely on local services, places of worship and may feel more vulnerable or isolated.\n• Application to Figure 4: the older man's identity is tied to the place; the student values food and activity but feels socially disconnected.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated knowledge; limited use of the interviews.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some understanding of how age and residence affect experience, with partial use of Figure 4.\nLevel 3 (5–6): detailed understanding, applied coherently to Figure 4 with clear links between both factors and lived experience.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 4 shows the ethnic composition of Leicester at three censuses (data written for this practice question).\nCensus year: % White British / % Asian or Asian British / % Other groups\n1991: 68 / 24 / 8\n2011: 45 / 37 / 18\n2021: 33 / 43 / 24\n\nSuggest reasons for the changes in the ethnic composition of Leicester between 1991 and 2021.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Continued international migration from South Asia (India, Pakistan, Bangladesh), including family reunification and chain migration into established communities.\n• Leicester's earlier East African Asian settlement (e.g. from Uganda and Kenya in the late 1960s and 1970s) created networks and institutions that attracted later migrants.\n• Younger age structure and higher fertility in some minority ethnic groups led to natural increase.\n• Out-migration of some White British residents to suburbs and surrounding districts (counter-urbanisation).\n• Growth of 'Other groups' reflects EU migration after 2004, refugees (e.g. Somali, some via the Netherlands) and students at the city's universities.\n• Application: the data show White British falling from 68% to 33% and Asian groups rising from 24% to 43%.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated, generic reasons; little reference to Figure 4.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some relevant reasons, partially linked to the changes shown.\nLevel 3 (5–6): a range of well-developed, plausible reasons clearly linked to the changes shown.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Many UK towns and cities have communities whose origins lie in countries that were once part of the British Empire, for example the Caribbean community in Brixton and the Bangladeshi community in Tower Hamlets.\n\nExplain how past connections have shaped the demographic and cultural characteristics of one place you have studied.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks). Indicative content:\n• Colonial and Commonwealth ties gave citizens the right to settle (British Nationality Act 1948) and encouraged post-war labour migration to fill shortages (e.g. Windrush generation from 1948 working in transport and the NHS; Sylheti seamen and later families to the East End).\n• Chain migration and family reunification after the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962 increased concentrations in particular places.\n• Cultural characteristics: places of worship, shops, food, festivals and languages (e.g. Brixton Market, Notting Hill Carnival; Brick Lane's Bengali restaurants and the Jamme Masjid, a building that was previously a Huguenot church and a synagogue).\n• Demographic characteristics: younger age structures, second and third generations born in the UK, mixed ethnic identities.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated or generic knowledge; little reference to a named place.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some accurate knowledge of past connections and their effects, partly linked to a named place.\nLevel 3 (5–6): detailed, accurate knowledge with clear explanation of how past connections shaped both demographic and cultural characteristics of a named place.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. A student plans to investigate how residents feel about change in Spitalfields, east London, over the past 50 years. They intend to use oral histories alongside census data.\n\nExplain the value of oral histories in investigating how a place has changed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Oral histories give qualitative, first-hand accounts of lived experience, emotions, attachment and identity that census data cannot capture.\n• They can record changes over long periods, e.g. memories of the Jewish East End, the arrival of Bangladeshi families in the 1970s, and gentrification around the markets.\n• They give a voice to groups often missing from official sources (older residents, women, minority groups).\n• Limitations reduce value: memory is selective and may be nostalgic; small samples; interviewer bias; hard to compare or generalise; so best combined with quantitative data such as the census and IMD.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated points about oral histories; limited application to investigating change.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some understanding of the strengths (and possibly weaknesses), partly applied to Spitalfields.\nLevel 3 (5–6): well-developed explanation of value, including some recognition of limitations, applied clearly to investigating change in Spitalfields.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 5 shows the views of two residents of a village in the Cotswolds. A retired couple who moved from London in 2015: 'We love the peace and quiet and don't want new estates spoiling the views.' A 24-year-old who grew up in the village: 'There's no way I can afford to live here now. The bus only runs twice a day.'\n\nExplain why tensions can develop in rural places as a result of in-migration.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Wealthy in-migrants (retirees, commuters, second-home owners) increase house prices, excluding young locals on lower rural wages.\n• Different values and perceptions: incomers may hold a 'rural idyll' view and oppose development (NIMBYism) whereas locals may want affordable housing and jobs.\n• Change in services: commuters shop and work elsewhere, so village services decline; bus services cut; this affects older and younger non-drivers.\n• Social change: loss of traditional community, changes to village identity, and in some places (e.g. Lincolnshire, Herefordshire) tensions with seasonal international farm workers.\n• Application to Figure 5: contrasting priorities of the retired couple and the young local.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated points; limited reference to Figure 5.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some explanation of causes of tension, partially applied.\nLevel 3 (5–6): detailed explanation of a range of causes, clearly applied to Figure 5.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 5 describes internal migration flows for London in 2022 (data written for this practice question). London had a net gain of about 30,000 people aged 20–29 from the rest of the UK, but a net loss of about 60,000 people aged 30–44 and about 50,000 children aged 0–14.\n\nSuggest reasons for the pattern of internal migration shown in Figure 5.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Young adults (20–29) move to London for graduate jobs, higher wages, universities, career progression and an urban lifestyle; shared rented housing suits them.\n• People aged 30–44 move out when they form families: high house prices and small flats in London; desire for gardens, better-perceived schools and lower crime in suburbs, commuter towns and rural areas.\n• Children move with these parents, explaining the net loss of 0–14s.\n• Remote/hybrid working since 2020 has made moving out easier.\n• Application: the 'escalator region' idea – London draws in the young and loses them at family-forming ages.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated reasons with little use of the figures.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some plausible reasons partially linked to the age groups shown.\nLevel 3 (5–6): a range of well-developed reasons clearly linked to each age group in Figure 5.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 6 shows two contrasting representations of the same inner-city area. A national newspaper headline reads: 'No-go zone: crime and decay in the inner city'. A local community website describes the area as 'a vibrant, creative neighbourhood with independent shops and a proud history'.\n\nExplain why media representations of a place may differ from the lived experience of its residents.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Media representations are produced for a purpose and audience: newspapers may sensationalise crime to sell stories; estate agents, councils and community groups promote positive images to attract investment or residents.\n• Representations are selective: they focus on single events or stereotypes; residents experience the everyday place, including social networks and community facilities.\n• Lived experience varies between groups (age, gender, ethnicity, income), so no single representation fits everyone.\n• Representations can become self-fulfilling, influencing perceptions, house prices and investment (territorial stigma).\n• Application to Figure 6.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated points; limited reference to Figure 6.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some understanding of why representations differ, partially applied.\nLevel 3 (5–6): detailed and coherent explanation, clearly applied to the contrasting sources in Figure 6.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. In 2004 the UK, Ireland and Sweden were the only existing EU members to allow citizens of the eight new Central and Eastern European member states immediate access to their labour markets.\n\nExplain how national government policy has influenced demographic change in places in the UK.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (6 marks). Indicative content:\n• Opening the labour market in 2004 led to large flows of young migrants (e.g. Polish, Lithuanian) to both cities and rural areas with demand for agricultural and food-processing labour (e.g. Boston and Peterborough), increasing young working-age populations and later births.\n• The end of EU free movement in 2021 and the points-based system reduced EU arrivals and changed flows (more non-EU workers, e.g. health and care visas).\n• Earlier policies: British Nationality Act 1948 enabled Commonwealth migration; the Commonwealth Immigrants Act 1962 restricted primary migration but encouraged family reunification.\n• Asylum dispersal policy since 1999 spread refugees to cities such as Glasgow, Liverpool and Middlesbrough; humanitarian schemes for Syria, Hong Kong (BN(O) visa) and Ukraine.\n• Domestic policies such as housing (council estates, new towns), university expansion and student visas also alter age structures of places.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated or generic knowledge of policy.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some accurate knowledge of policies and their demographic effects, with some located examples.\nLevel 3 (5–6): detailed, accurate explanation of how a range of policies affected demographic change in located places.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. You have carried out fieldwork in a local place to investigate how different groups perceive recent cultural change.\n\nSuggest how a student could investigate perceptions of cultural change in a diverse local place.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks). Indicative content:\n• Primary qualitative data: semi-structured interviews and questionnaires with different groups (by age, ethnicity, length of residence), using stratified sampling to ensure each group is represented; oral histories with long-term residents.\n• Perception data: bipolar or Likert-scale surveys of feelings about the area (e.g. safe–unsafe), emotional/mental maps.\n• Photographs or rephotography to compare with historic images; annotated field sketches of shop fronts, places of worship, signage languages.\n• Secondary data: census ethnicity and country-of-birth data over time; IMD; social media, local newspaper archives.\n• Ethical considerations (consent, anonymity), and how results could be presented (e.g. divided bar charts by group, word clouds) and compared.\nLevel 1 (1–2): isolated methods with little justification.\nLevel 2 (3–4): some appropriate methods with partial justification.\nLevel 3 (5–6): a range of appropriate, well-justified methods, including sampling, clearly linked to investigating perceptions of cultural change.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Since the 1950s, international migration has transformed the cultural landscape of many UK cities, for example the Curry Mile in Manchester, the Golden Mile in Leicester and Chinatown in London.\n\nExplain how international migration has changed the cultural characteristics of urban places in the UK.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks). Indicative content:\n• Changes to the built environment and economy: shops, restaurants, places of worship (mosques, gurdwaras, temples), signage in other languages, ethnic enclaves and business districts.\n• Changes to cultural life: festivals and events (e.g. Diwali in Leicester, Notting Hill Carnival, Chinese New Year), music, food and media; hybrid identities among second and third generations.\n• Changes to social characteristics: languages spoken in schools, religious diversity, community organisations.\n• Variation over time and space: successive waves (e.g. Huguenot, Jewish and Bangladeshi in Spitalfields); recent EU migration adding Polish shops and churches; superdiversity in places such as Newham.\n• Not all change is accepted equally; some places experience tension or loss of identity felt by long-term residents.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge with little explanation; few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some accurate knowledge and explanation with some located examples, though partly descriptive.\nLevel 3 (7–8): accurate, detailed knowledge and explanation of a range of changes, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. The 2001 Cantle Report into disturbances in Bradford, Oldham and Burnley described communities living 'parallel lives' in some northern towns.\n\nExplain why some ethnic groups become residentially segregated in UK cities.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks). Indicative content:\n• Positive (internal) factors: desire to live near family and people with the same language, religion and culture; access to places of worship, specialist shops and community support; security from discrimination; chain migration.\n• Negative (external) factors: low incomes restricting newcomers to cheap inner-city terraced housing near former mill or factory jobs; past discrimination in housing and employment; 'white flight' to suburbs.\n• Institutional factors: housing allocation policies, faith schools, school catchments.\n• Segregation may reduce over time through suburbanisation of later generations (e.g. Indian communities moving to suburbs of Leicester) or persist where deprivation is high.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some explanation of reasons for segregation with some located support.\nLevel 3 (7–8): detailed explanation of a range of internal and external reasons, well supported by located examples.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Rural places in the UK are often represented in television, advertising and tourism brochures as a 'rural idyll' of picturesque villages and close-knit communities.\n\nExplain why different groups of people perceive rural places in different ways.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks). Indicative content:\n• Retirees, commuters and second-home owners may perceive the countryside as peaceful, safe and attractive (rural idyll), often shaped by media representations.\n• Young people may perceive rural areas as boring and isolating, with limited jobs, leisure, transport and affordable housing.\n• Ethnic minority residents and visitors may feel excluded or visible in largely White rural places; research (e.g. by Natural England) shows under-representation of minority groups in visiting the countryside.\n• Low-income groups and older people without cars may experience rural deprivation (hidden poverty, loss of services, fuel poverty).\n• Farmers and long-term residents may perceive change negatively (loss of community, development pressures).\n• Gender, sexual orientation and disability can also influence feelings of belonging or exclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited explanation of perceptions.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some explanation of contrasting perceptions with some examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8): detailed explanation of why a range of groups perceive rural places differently, with well-chosen examples.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. A local council wants to measure how successfully it has managed cultural and demographic change in a diverse urban ward over the past ten years.\n\nExplain how quantitative and qualitative data could be used to measure the success of managing cultural and demographic change in a place.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks). Indicative content:\n• Quantitative: changes in IMD ranks and domains (income, employment, health, crime, education); census data on ethnicity, country of birth, household composition, overcrowding; rates of hate crime; school attainment gaps; voter turnout; community survey results (e.g. % who agree people from different backgrounds get on well together).\n• Qualitative: oral histories, interviews, focus groups, local media coverage, social media sentiment, photographs of the built environment (e.g. new community centres, graffiti).\n• Quantitative data allow comparisons over time and with other places, but may not show feelings or belonging; qualitative data show lived experience but are subjective and difficult to compare.\n• Success depends on whose viewpoint is measured, so data should be disaggregated by group (age, ethnicity, length of residence).\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge of data sources; little explanation of how they measure success.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some explanation of how both types of data can be used, with some examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8): detailed explanation of a range of quantitative and qualitative data and how each measures success, including their limitations.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. In 2021 the median age of residents in Tower Hamlets was about 30, while in North Norfolk it was over 50.\n\nExplain why rates of natural change vary between places within the UK.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks). Indicative content:\n• Natural change = births minus deaths; it depends strongly on age structure: places with many young adults (inner cities, university towns) have more births and fewer deaths, giving natural increase; places with ageing populations (coastal retirement areas, remote rural areas) have natural decrease.\n• Age structure itself is driven by migration (young adults into cities; retirees to coasts; young people out of rural areas).\n• Fertility varies between ethnic and religious groups, and with education and income.\n• Mortality varies with deprivation and health: life expectancy is lower in deprived places such as parts of Blackpool or Glasgow than in affluent places such as Kensington and Chelsea.\n• Examples: Tower Hamlets has natural increase; North Norfolk has more deaths than births.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited explanation of variation.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some explanation of the factors with some located examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8): detailed explanation of the links between age structure, migration, fertility and mortality, supported by located examples.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. In the early 2020s, plans to build a shopping and office development on the site of the former Truman Brewery off Brick Lane in east London led to protests from local Bangladeshi residents and campaign groups.\n\nExplain how conflicts over the use of space can develop in diverse urban places.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `AO1 (8 marks). Indicative content:\n• Gentrification: rising rents and the arrival of wealthier, often younger and White British residents and chain/upmarket businesses displace established minority-ethnic businesses and residents (e.g. Brick Lane's curry houses, Brixton Market).\n• Contested meaning of space: one group's heritage or community space may be another's development opportunity; disputes over places of worship, street markets, festivals or public spaces.\n• Competition for scarce housing, school places and services as populations grow and change.\n• Different groups' identities and perceptions conflict (long-term residents' sense of loss vs newcomers' views of the area as 'vibrant'); media and political rhetoric can intensify tensions.\n• Conflicts can range from planning objections and protests to hate crime and, rarely, disturbances (e.g. Bradford 2001).\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited explanation.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some explanation of the causes of conflict over space with some examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8): detailed explanation of a range of causes of conflict over space, supported by located examples.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 7 shows the components of population change for two English local authorities, 2011–2021 (data written for this practice question).\nComponent: Peterborough / East Lindsey\nNatural change: +11,000 / −9,500\nNet internal migration: −6,000 / +12,000\nNet international migration: +17,000 / +2,500\n\nUsing Figure 7 and your own knowledge, assess the importance of migration compared with natural change in shaping the population structures of places.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks). Indicative content:\n• Peterborough: international migration (+17,000) is the largest component; young EU and other migrants add working-age adults and later increase births, so migration also drives natural increase.\n• East Lindsey (Lincolnshire coast): natural decrease (−9,500) because of an older population, offset by net internal in-migration (+12,000), largely of retirees, which further ages the structure.\n• Migration is selective by age, so it changes structure faster than natural change; natural change is often a consequence of past migration.\n• However, natural change matters more in some places and over longer periods (e.g. fertility differences between ethnic groups; mortality in deprived places).\n• Judgement: migration is usually the dominant driver at local scale, especially for diverse urban places and retirement areas, but the two interact.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 7; generic or unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some relevant knowledge applied to Figure 7; unbalanced assessment and partial judgement.\nLevel 3 (7–9): mostly accurate knowledge applied to produce a partially balanced assessment and a supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12): detailed, accurate knowledge applied to Figure 7 and other examples, producing a full, balanced assessment and a well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Globalisation has increased flows of people, goods, ideas and capital into UK towns and cities.\n\nAssess the extent to which globalisation has been the main cause of cultural change in UK places.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks). Indicative content:\n• Globalisation-related causes: international migration (EU enlargement, global cities such as London), global media, international students, global brands and foods; transnational connections maintained through the internet.\n• Older causes not strictly 'globalisation': colonial and Commonwealth connections (Windrush, South Asian migration), which predate recent globalisation.\n• Domestic causes: internal migration (retirees to rural places, students to university towns), gentrification, ageing, changing household structures and secularisation.\n• Government policy (immigration rules, asylum dispersal, housing) channels where change occurs.\n• Judgement: globalisation is a major cause in cities and some rural areas with migrant labour, but internal processes are often more important in rural and coastal places.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; generic statements; little assessment.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some relevant knowledge and examples; unbalanced assessment.\nLevel 3 (7–9): mostly accurate knowledge, a partially balanced assessment and a supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12): detailed knowledge with well-chosen examples, a full and balanced assessment and a well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 8 lists four sources that a student used to study Boston, Lincolnshire: (1) Census 2011 and 2021 country-of-birth tables; (2) Index of Multiple Deprivation rankings for the town's neighbourhoods; (3) a national newspaper article from 2016 about the town's 75% vote to leave the EU; (4) 30 photographs of shop fronts on West Street taken by the student.\n\nAssess the reliability of the sources in Figure 8 for representing the characteristics of a diverse place.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks). Indicative content:\n• Census: large, official, comparable over time and between places; but only every ten years, self-classification, under-counting of short-term or seasonal migrants (important in Boston's agricultural workforce).\n• IMD: robust, standardised and multi-domain; but relative not absolute, measures deprivation not diversity or lived experience; area averages hide individual variation.\n• Newspaper: shows how the place is represented; may be sensational or politically biased; a single-event snapshot.\n• Photographs: show the cultural landscape (e.g. Eastern European shops); but selective framing, one moment, depends on the photographer.\n• Judgement: official quantitative sources are more reliable but less complete; reliability improves when sources are triangulated.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated points about sources; little assessment of reliability.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some relevant points applied to some sources; unbalanced assessment.\nLevel 3 (7–9): mostly accurate understanding applied to most sources with a partially balanced assessment and supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12): detailed, accurate evaluation of all the sources, balanced and leading to a well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 9 shows the results of a survey asking residents of a seaside town 'Do you feel safe walking in the town centre after dark?' (data written for this practice question).\nAge group: % answering 'yes'\n16–24: 71\n25–44: 64\n45–64: 52\n65 and over: 33\n\nUsing Figure 9 and your own knowledge, assess the importance of age in influencing people's perceptions and lived experience of a place.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks). Indicative content:\n• Figure 9: feeling safe falls with age, from 71% (16–24) to 33% (65+), suggesting age strongly influences perception of the night-time town centre.\n• Age affects use of space (nightlife vs daytime services), mobility and dependence on public transport, attachment from length of residence, and attitudes to change.\n• Other factors also matter: gender (women often feel less safe), ethnicity (experiences of racism or belonging), sexuality, disability, income and length of residence; these intersect with age.\n• Media representations shape perceptions for all ages.\n• Judgement: age is an important influence, as the data suggest, but its importance varies with place and interacts with other identities.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited reference to Figure 9.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some relevant knowledge applied to Figure 9; unbalanced assessment.\nLevel 3 (7–9): mostly accurate knowledge, a partially balanced assessment and supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12): detailed knowledge applied to Figure 9 and other evidence, a full, balanced assessment and well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. In the summer of 2001 there were serious disturbances involving young people from Asian and White communities in Oldham, Burnley and Bradford.\n\nWith reference to one or more places you have studied, assess the reasons why demographic and cultural change can lead to tension in diverse places.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `AO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks). Indicative content:\n• Economic causes: deindustrialisation (e.g. textile mills in Oldham and Bradford), unemployment and deprivation affecting all groups, competition for jobs, housing and services.\n• Spatial segregation and 'parallel lives' (Cantle Report): little contact between communities, segregated schools and housing, reinforcing stereotypes.\n• Political causes: far-right activity, media representations, national events (e.g. reported rise in hate crime after the 2016 EU referendum).\n• Rapid change: places with fast recent migration (e.g. Boston) can experience strain on services and perceived loss of identity among long-term residents.\n• Change does not always lead to tension: many places (e.g. Leicester, often cited as a model of integration) show high cohesion; tensions there in 2022 also show it is not fixed.\n• Judgement on which reasons are most significant, e.g. deprivation and segregation combined with a trigger event.\nLevel 1 (1–3): isolated knowledge; limited located support; little assessment.\nLevel 2 (4–6): some relevant reasons with some located support; unbalanced assessment.\nLevel 3 (7–9): mostly accurate knowledge and examples, a partially balanced assessment and supported conclusion.\nLevel 4 (10–12): detailed knowledge and well-chosen located examples, a full, balanced assessment and well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Since 2001 UK governments and local councils have introduced community cohesion and integration policies, such as the Integrated Communities Strategy (2018), alongside local initiatives including inter-faith forums and English language classes.\n\nEvaluate the view that the management of cultural and demographic change has been successful for all groups in a place you have studied.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Strategies: community cohesion policies after Cantle; integration and English language funding; anti-discrimination law (Equality Act 2010); local festivals and inter-faith work; regeneration and affordable housing; rural strategies (affordable housing, community land trusts, second-home council tax premiums).\n• Measuring success: quantitative (IMD, hate crime, employment gaps, survey of cohesion) and qualitative (oral histories, media).\n• Success for some groups: improved services, cultural recognition, rising attainment of some minority groups (e.g. Bangladeshi pupils in Tower Hamlets schools).\n• Less success for others: long-term White working-class residents may feel ignored; newer migrants face language barriers; young people priced out by gentrification; older people isolated in rural areas.\n• Different stakeholders judge success differently; success varies over time and with funding cuts after 2010.\n• Conclusion: management is rarely successful for all groups; evaluation should reach a supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge; limited application; judgement unsupported.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some relevant knowledge applied in a partial evaluation; judgement partially supported.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge applied to produce a partially balanced evaluation with a reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): accurate, detailed knowledge applied to produce a full, balanced evaluation and a well-evidenced, coherent conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 10 shows two views. A rural councillor in Cornwall: 'Second homes and holiday lets are tearing our communities apart.' A community worker in an inner-city London borough: 'Rapid population change and gentrification are the biggest source of tension in the country.'\n\nUsing Figure 10 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that rural places experience greater demographic and cultural tensions than urban places.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Rural tensions: second homes and holiday lets (St Ives referendum 2016; Cornwall, Gwynedd), retiree in-migration and housing costs, loss of services, seasonal migrant workers in Lincolnshire and Herefordshire, ageing populations, minority groups' sense of exclusion.\n• Urban tensions: segregation and disturbances (Bradford 2001), gentrification and displacement (Brixton, Brick Lane), competition for housing and services, hate crime, rapid change in superdiverse places, intergenerational tensions.\n• Urban tensions are often more visible and larger in scale; rural tensions may be hidden but intense because communities are small and change is proportionally large.\n• Many places manage change with little tension in both settings.\n• Conclusion weighing type, scale and intensity of tensions.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 10; unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some knowledge applied; unbalanced evaluation; partial judgement.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge, partially balanced evaluation of rural and urban places and a reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): detailed, accurate knowledge of both, a full, balanced evaluation and a well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. People's experiences of the same street, neighbourhood or village can differ greatly.\n\nWith reference to two contrasting places you have studied, evaluate the view that ethnicity is the most important factor influencing people's lived experience of a place.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Ethnicity: belonging in established communities (Southall, Tower Hamlets); experiences of racism or exclusion, especially in predominantly White rural places; access to culturally specific services; visible minority status.\n• Other factors: age (use of space, mobility, safety); gender (perceived safety, access to space); sexual orientation (e.g. Canal Street, Manchester as a welcoming space); income and class (housing, access to services, effects of gentrification); length of residence and attachment; disability.\n• Factors intersect: e.g. a young Bangladeshi woman vs an elderly White British man in the same area.\n• The importance of ethnicity varies by place: more significant in places with recent rapid change or tension; less so in long-established diverse places.\n• Conclusion based on the contrasting places.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge; limited reference to places; unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some relevant knowledge applied to places; partial evaluation.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge of both places, partially balanced evaluation and reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): detailed knowledge of two contrasting places, a full, balanced evaluation of ethnicity against other factors and a well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Some UK cities contain neighbourhoods where one ethnic group forms a large majority of residents, for example parts of Bradford, Blackburn and east London.\n\nEvaluate the view that residential segregation in diverse places is mainly the result of choice rather than constraint.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Choice: wish to live near family, places of worship, shops and culturally specific services; mutual support; language; defensive clustering against discrimination.\n• Constraint: low incomes and housing costs restricting access to cheaper inner-city terraces; historic discrimination in housing and jobs; social housing allocation; fear of racism in other areas; 'white flight' from areas leaving others behind.\n• Change over time: second and third generations with higher incomes suburbanise (e.g. Indian communities in Leicester, Harrow), suggesting earlier clustering was partly constrained.\n• Different groups and places show different balances (e.g. Bangladeshi communities in Tower Hamlets with high deprivation vs more affluent suburban groups).\n• Conclusion on whether choice or constraint dominates, recognising they interact.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge; limited application; unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some relevant knowledge and examples; unbalanced evaluation.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge, partially balanced evaluation and reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): detailed, accurate knowledge and located examples, full, balanced evaluation and well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Figure 11 shows three strategies used to manage tensions in a diverse town (data written for this practice question): a council-funded inter-faith forum; a national government programme funding English language classes; and a resident-led community festival celebrating the town's different cultures.\n\nUsing Figure 11 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that local strategies are more effective than national strategies in reducing tensions in diverse places.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Local strategies: inter-faith forums, festivals (e.g. Leicester's Diwali celebrations, Notting Hill Carnival), youth projects, community centres, local planning decisions such as principal residence policies; tailored to local needs, involve residents, build trust and contact between groups.\n• Limitations of local strategies: dependent on council budgets (reduced since 2010), small scale, may reach only those already engaged.\n• National strategies: legislation (Equality Act 2010, hate crime laws), integration strategies, English language funding, immigration policy, housing and second-home tax powers; set frameworks and funding at scale.\n• Limitations of national strategies: one-size-fits-all; national rhetoric can increase tension; Prevent has been criticised by some Muslim communities.\n• Effectiveness measured by hate crime trends, cohesion surveys, qualitative accounts; views differ between groups.\n• Conclusion: likely that local and national strategies work best together; judgement on which is more effective.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge; limited use of Figure 11; unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some relevant knowledge applied; unbalanced evaluation.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge, partially balanced evaluation and reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): detailed knowledge and examples, a full, balanced evaluation and a well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. International migration to the UK has changed the economies, populations and cultures of many towns and cities.\n\nWith reference to places you have studied, evaluate the view that international migration has had more positive than negative impacts on local places.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Positive: fills labour shortages (agriculture and food processing in Boston and Peterborough; NHS and care); new businesses and regeneration of high streets; younger age structures offsetting ageing; cultural enrichment (food, festivals); revitalised schools and churches.\n• Negative or contested: pressure on housing, school places and GP services where funding lags behind population growth; overcrowded houses in multiple occupation; perceived wage competition; perceived loss of identity among some long-term residents; tensions and hate crime.\n• Impacts vary between groups (employers, migrants, long-term residents, young/old) and over time (initial strain vs later integration).\n• Evidence: census, IMD, local surveys, voting patterns (e.g. Boston's 2016 referendum result) and media representations.\n• Conclusion weighing evidence across places.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge; few located examples; unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some relevant knowledge and examples; unbalanced evaluation.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge and examples, partially balanced evaluation and reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): detailed, accurate knowledge and well-chosen examples, a full, balanced evaluation and well-evidenced conclusion.` },
      { q: `Section B – Diverse Places. Geographers studying diverse places use quantitative sources such as the census and the Index of Multiple Deprivation, and qualitative sources such as oral histories, photographs, art and social media.\n\nEvaluate the view that quantitative sources are more useful than qualitative sources in understanding how people experience diverse places.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `AO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks). Indicative content:\n• Quantitative: census (ethnicity, country of birth, age, language), IMD (seven domains), crime and health data, house prices; objective, comparable across places and over time, large samples, allow statistical testing; but cannot capture feelings, belonging or identity; area averages hide individuals; may be out of date.\n• Qualitative: oral histories, interviews, photographs, films, novels, music, social media; reveal lived experience, perceptions and emotions, give voice to marginalised groups; but subjective, selective, small samples, hard to generalise, potential bias.\n• The question focuses on how people experience places, which favours qualitative sources, but quantitative data give context (e.g. deprivation shaping experience).\n• Best understanding comes from triangulating both (mixed methods), e.g. using IMD with interviews in a deprived ward.\n• Conclusion with supported judgement.\nLevel 1 (1–5): isolated knowledge of sources; limited evaluation; unsupported judgement.\nLevel 2 (6–10): some relevant knowledge of both types; unbalanced evaluation.\nLevel 3 (11–15): mostly accurate knowledge, partially balanced evaluation and reasoned conclusion.\nLevel 4 (16–20): detailed knowledge with examples, a full, balanced evaluation focused on lived experience and a well-evidenced conclusion.` }
    ]
  },
  'T7': {
    name: 'Superpowers',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 1 (data written for this practice question, based on approximate published estimates for 2023).\nFigure 1: Military expenditure of the ten highest-spending countries, 2023 (US$ billion):\nUSA 916; China 296; Russia 109; India 84; Saudi Arabia 76; UK 75; Germany 67; Ukraine 65; France 61; Japan 50.\n\nCalculate the percentage of the total military expenditure of these ten countries that was spent by the USA. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Total for the ten countries = 1799 (US$ billion) and correct method shown: 916 ÷ 1799 × 100 (1)\n• Answer = 50.9% (1)\nAccept 50.9 for both marks if working is clear. Correct method with arithmetic slip = 1 mark.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Figure 1 shows China's gross domestic product (GDP) in current US$ (data written for this practice question, rounded from published estimates):\n2000: US$1.2 trillion\n2022: US$17.9 trillion\n\nCalculate the percentage increase in China's GDP between 2000 and 2022. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: (17.9 − 1.2) ÷ 1.2 × 100, or 16.7 ÷ 1.2 × 100 (1)\n• Answer = 1391.7% (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Figure 1 is a table of GDP per capita for the five original BRICS countries, 2022 (US$, data written for this practice question):\nBrazil 8,900; Russia 15,300; India 2,400; China 12,700; South Africa 6,800.\n\nCalculate the mean GDP per capita for these countries. You must show your working. Give your answer in US$.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: (8,900 + 15,300 + 2,400 + 12,700 + 6,800) ÷ 5, or 46,100 ÷ 5 (1)\n• Answer = US$9,220 (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Figure 1 is a line graph of the average September extent of Arctic sea ice, 1980–2020. The line falls unevenly, from about 7.7 million km² in 1980 to about 3.9 million km² in 2020 (values approximate, written for this practice question).\n\nCalculate the percentage change in September Arctic sea ice extent between 1980 and 2020. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: (3.9 − 7.7) ÷ 7.7 × 100 (1)\n• Answer = −49.4% (accept a decrease of 49.4%) (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 2 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 2 is a line graph of share of world GDP (%, at purchasing power parity) for the G7 and the E7 (China, India, Brazil, Russia, Indonesia, Mexico and Turkey), 1995–2025. The G7 line falls steadily from about 45% in 1995 to about 30% in 2025. The E7 line rises from about 20% to about 36%, crossing the G7 line in the mid-2010s.\n\nSuggest one reason for the increase in the E7 share of world GDP.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `One reason, developed. 1 mark for the reason, up to 2 further marks for development/exemplification.\ne.g.\n• Large, young labour forces with low wage costs attracted FDI and offshored manufacturing from TNCs (1) so output and exports grew rapidly, e.g. China's coastal Special Economic Zones from the 1980s (1) raising their share of global production as G7 manufacturing declined (1)\n• Economic liberalisation/market reforms (1), e.g. China after 1978, India after 1991 (1), opened economies to trade and investment and increased growth rates above those of the G7 (1)\n• Rapid urbanisation and a growing middle class increased domestic consumption (1) ...\n• Natural resource wealth (Russia, Brazil, Indonesia) and rising commodity prices (1) ...\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 2 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 2 is a bar chart of annual flows of Chinese foreign direct investment (FDI) into Africa, 2005–2020. Flows are below US$1 billion each year until 2007, then rise unevenly to between US$3 and US$5 billion a year after 2013. The largest recipients named are Zambia, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, Kenya and Ethiopia.\n\nSuggest one reason why China has increased its investment in African countries.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `One reason, developed (1 + 1 + 1).\ne.g.\n• To secure supplies of raw materials for its manufacturing industry (1), e.g. copper from Zambia and cobalt from the DRC (1), because China's own resources cannot meet demand from rapid industrialisation (1)\n• To gain political influence/allies (1), e.g. support for its positions in UN votes (1), extending its sphere of influence through FOCAC and the Belt and Road Initiative (1)\n• To open new markets for Chinese goods and construction firms (1), e.g. Chinese contractors building infrastructure such as the Mombasa–Nairobi railway (1) ...\n• To invest surplus foreign currency reserves for a return (1) ...\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 2, which shows approximate voting shares in the International Monetary Fund (IMF) for selected member countries (data written for this practice question):\nUSA 16.5%; Japan 6.1%; China 6.1%; Germany 5.3%; UK 4.0%; France 4.0%; India 2.6%; Brazil 2.2%.\nMajor IMF decisions require an 85% majority.\n\nSuggest one reason why emerging powers have called for reform of the IMF.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `One reason, developed (1 + 1 + 1).\ne.g.\n• Voting shares reflect the post-1945 world economy rather than today's (1); India and Brazil together have less than 5% of the vote though they are among the world's largest economies (1), so emerging powers feel under-represented in decisions that affect them (1)\n• The USA holds over 15% of the vote (1) so it can veto major decisions requiring 85% (1), allowing it to protect its own interests (1)\n• Conditions attached to IMF loans (e.g. structural adjustment) have been set by Western-dominated boards (1) ... leading BRICS to create alternatives such as the New Development Bank (1)\nMax 3.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The USA is commonly estimated to have around 750 military bases in about 80 countries, including Ramstein in Germany, Yokosuka in Japan and Diego Garcia in the Indian Ocean.\n\nExplain one reason why the USA maintains a global network of overseas military bases.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `One reason (1) with up to three further marks for explanation/development (1 + 1 + 1).\ne.g.\n• To project hard power quickly anywhere in the world (1): bases act as forward positions for aircraft, ships and troops (1) so the USA can respond to crises such as conflicts in the Middle East within hours rather than weeks (1); this deters rivals and reassures allies (1)\n• To support allies in alliances such as NATO and treaties with Japan and South Korea (1), giving a security guarantee (1) that keeps these countries within the US sphere of influence (1) and limits the influence of Russia or China (1)\n• To protect global trade routes and access to resources (1), e.g. naval bases in the Gulf protect oil shipping through the Strait of Hormuz (1) ...\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 3 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 3: Share of world GDP (%, at purchasing power parity), 1990–2020\nYear | USA | EU | China | India\n1990 | 21 | 26 | 4 | 3\n2000 | 20 | 23 | 7 | 4\n2010 | 17 | 19 | 14 | 6\n2020 | 16 | 15 | 18 | 7\n\nAnalyse the changes in share of world GDP shown in Figure 3.`, marks: 4, command: 'Analyse', markScheme: `Up to 4 marks for analysis that identifies trends and uses the data, with comparison (1 mark per valid point; max 2 if no data used).\n• The USA and EU shares both fall over the period (1); the EU falls more (from 26% to 15%, 11 percentage points) than the USA (from 21% to 16%, 5 points) (1)\n• China's share rises rapidly, 4.5 times from 4% to 18% (1), overtaking the EU and USA by 2020 (1)\n• India's share more than doubles from 3% to 7% (1) but remains much lower than China's (1)\n• Fastest change for China occurs 2000–2010 (7% to 14%, doubling in a decade) (1)\n• Overall the pattern shows a shift of economic weight from established powers towards emerging Asian economies, suggesting a more multipolar world economy (1)` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. A student investigated whether there is a relationship between economic size and military expenditure for eight countries. Figure 3 gives the ranks (data written for this practice question):\nCountry | Rank GDP | Rank military spending\nUSA | 1 | 1\nChina | 2 | 2\nJapan | 3 | 8\nGermany | 4 | 6\nIndia | 5 | 4\nUK | 6 | 5\nFrance | 7 | 7\nRussia | 8 | 3\nFormula: rs = 1 − (6Σd²) ÷ (n³ − n)\n\nCalculate the Spearman's rank correlation coefficient (rs) for these data. You must show your working. Give your answer to two decimal places.`, marks: 4, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct differences in rank (d): 0, 0, 5, 2, 1, 1, 0, 5 (signs ignored) (1)\n• Σd² = 0 + 0 + 25 + 4 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 25 = 56 (1)\n• Substitution: rs = 1 − (6 × 56) ÷ (512 − 8) = 1 − 336 ÷ 504 (1)\n• rs = 0.33 (1)\nCarry forward an incorrect Σd² for the final two marks if the method is correct.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. China's Belt and Road Initiative (BRI), launched in 2013, has financed ports, railways and power stations in more than 100 countries, including the Port of Gwadar in Pakistan and the Mombasa–Nairobi Standard Gauge Railway in Kenya.\n\nExplain one way in which the Belt and Road Initiative increases China's global influence.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `One way (1) with up to three further marks for development (1 + 1 + 1).\ne.g.\n• Loans for infrastructure create economic dependency (1): borrower countries owe large debts to Chinese state banks (1), which may give China leverage over their policies, e.g. Sri Lanka leasing Hambantota port to a Chinese firm for 99 years in 2017 (1), extending China's sphere of influence (1)\n• New transport links tie trade flows to China (1), securing routes for imports of oil and raw materials that avoid the Strait of Malacca (1) and opening markets for Chinese exports (1), strengthening China's economic power (1)\n• BRI projects build goodwill and political alliances (1) ...\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Summer sea ice in the Arctic Ocean has declined markedly since the 1980s. Eight countries are members of the Arctic Council, including Russia, the USA, Canada and Denmark (through Greenland).\n\nExplain one reason why the loss of Arctic sea ice may increase tension between powerful countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `One reason (1) with up to three further marks for development (1 + 1 + 1).\ne.g.\n• Melting ice makes undersea oil and gas reserves more accessible (1); the Arctic is estimated to hold a large share of the world's undiscovered gas and oil (1); Russia, Denmark and Canada have overlapping claims to the Lomonosov Ridge beyond their 200 nautical mile Exclusive Economic Zones under UNCLOS (1), so competition for these resources could lead to disputes (1)\n• New shipping routes such as the Northern Sea Route open up (1), shortening journeys between Asia and Europe (1); Russia claims control of this route while the USA argues for freedom of navigation (1), and China describes itself as a "near-Arctic state" seeking access (1)\n• Militarisation: Russia has reopened Soviet-era Arctic bases (1) ...\nMax 4.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. At its peak in the early twentieth century, the British Empire covered around a quarter of the world's land area and population, linked by the Royal Navy and a network of coaling stations and ports such as Gibraltar, Cape Town, Aden and Singapore.\n\nExplain how the British Empire was able to exercise global power during the imperial era.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Direct colonial control through military force and administration: territory was governed from London, e.g. the British Raj in India\n• Naval supremacy: the Royal Navy controlled key sea lanes and chokepoints (Suez Canal after 1882, Gibraltar, Singapore) — links to Mackinder's ideas on geostrategic location\n• Economic power: industrialisation produced manufactured goods for export; colonies supplied raw materials (cotton, rubber, tea) and captive markets — a core–periphery relationship\n• Financial dominance: London as the centre of global finance and investment\n• Cultural power: spread of the English language, education systems, legal systems and sport, which persists as soft power today\n• Political power: treaties and the ability to set rules of trade\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. After 1945 the world was divided between the USA and its allies (NATO, formed 1949) and the USSR and its allies (Warsaw Pact, formed 1955). Germany was divided, and the Berlin Wall stood from 1961 to 1989.\n\nExplain how the Cold War created a bipolar world.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Two superpowers with competing ideologies — capitalism/democracy (USA) and communism (USSR) — each sought to spread its system\n• Military alliances (NATO, Warsaw Pact) divided Europe into two blocs; the 'Iron Curtain'\n• Nuclear arms race and mutually assured destruction meant neither could defeat the other, producing a balance of power\n• Indirect control through spheres of influence: aid (Marshall Plan; Soviet aid to Cuba), proxy wars (Korea, Vietnam, Afghanistan)\n• Economic blocs: Western trade and IGOs (IMF, World Bank) versus COMECON\n• Space race and cultural competition as soft power\n• Bipolar structure ended with the collapse of the USSR in 1991, leading to a unipolar period\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 4 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 4 is a bar chart showing the share of global film box-office revenue earned by films from different countries in a recent year: USA about 65%; China about 15%; India about 3%; UK about 3%; all others about 14%. A caption notes that the most-watched streaming platforms worldwide are US-owned.\n\nExplain how media and culture help a superpower to maintain its global influence.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nAO1:\n• Soft power (Nye) — the ability to influence others through attraction rather than coercion\n• Cultural exports spread values, lifestyles and the English language (Americanisation/westernisation)\n• Brands and TNCs (Disney, Netflix, McDonald's) make the superpower's culture familiar and desirable\nAO2 (use of Figure 4):\n• The USA earns about 65% of box-office revenue — over four times China's share — so its narratives dominate global audiences\n• Positive images of the superpower may make foreign publics and governments more willing to support its policies\n• China's rising share suggests it is trying to build its own soft power, though its films are watched mainly at home\n• Cultural influence can be resisted (e.g. censorship, quotas) so it does not guarantee political influence\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout, applied to the resource to give a thorough, logical explanation that makes clear use of the data.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge, applied to the resource to give a partial explanation with some reference to the data.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge with some inaccuracies; little or no reference to the resource.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 4 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 4 compares the share of world population and share of world GDP (US$, current prices) in 2022:\nGroup | % world population | % world GDP\nUSA | 4 | 25\nEU | 6 | 17\nChina | 18 | 18\nIndia | 18 | 3\nRest of world | 54 | 37\n\nSuggest reasons for the differences between share of world population and share of world GDP shown in Figure 4.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nAO2 (use of Figure 4):\n• USA has 4% of population but 25% of GDP — over six times its population share; India has 18% of population but only 3% of GDP; China's shares are about equal\nAO1/AO2 reasons:\n• The USA and EU have high productivity, a large tertiary and quaternary sector, high-value TNCs, capital and technology accumulated over a long period of industrialisation\n• Established powers dominate global finance and IGOs, attracting investment and setting trade rules in their favour (world systems theory: core vs periphery)\n• India remains at an earlier stage of development: much employment in low-productivity agriculture and the informal sector, infrastructure gaps, lower levels of education for some groups\n• China's rapid growth since 1978 from manufacturing and FDI has raised its GDP share to match its population share\n• Use of market exchange rates under-states GDP of emerging economies compared with PPP\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (3 marks)\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout, applied to the resource to give a thorough, logical explanation that makes clear use of the data.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge, applied to the resource to give a partial explanation with some reference to the data.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated knowledge with some inaccuracies; little or no reference to the resource.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. In 2023 the world's largest companies by market value included Apple, Microsoft and Alphabet (USA), Saudi Aramco (Saudi Arabia) and Tencent (China).\n\nExplain how transnational corporations (TNCs) help superpowers to maintain economic influence.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• TNCs are headquartered in superpowers, so profits, tax revenues and research and development are concentrated there\n• They control global supply chains, shaping where production and jobs are located (e.g. Apple's assembly in China)\n• Ownership of intellectual property and patents — high-value activities stay in the core\n• TNCs spread brands and consumer culture (soft power) and set technological standards (e.g. US operating systems and platforms)\n• Lobbying of governments and IGOs to shape trade rules in their favour (WTO intellectual property rules)\n• Emerging-power TNCs (Huawei, Tata, Petrobras) are now challenging this dominance; states may restrict rivals' TNCs (US sanctions on Huawei)\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. China became the world's largest importer of crude oil in 2017 and consumes around half of the world's coal and steel. India's energy demand is forecast to grow faster than that of any other major economy over the next two decades.\n\nExplain why emerging powers have increasing demands for resources.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Rapid industrialisation: manufacturing for export (China as 'workshop of the world') needs energy, metals and water\n• Urbanisation: construction of cities, housing and infrastructure requires cement, steel and energy\n• Growing middle class: rising incomes increase consumption of meat, cars, electricity and consumer goods (nutrition transition)\n• Large and still-growing populations (India) add to demand\n• Domestic resources are insufficient, so emerging powers seek resources overseas (Chinese investment in African and Latin American mining)\n• Consequences may be mentioned briefly: rising CO₂ emissions, deforestation (soya and beef from Brazil), competition for resources\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The World Bank and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) were created at Bretton Woods in 1944. By convention, the President of the World Bank has always been a US citizen and the Managing Director of the IMF has been a European.\n\nExplain how intergovernmental organisations (IGOs) have helped to maintain the influence of established superpowers.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Voting power is linked to financial contributions, so the USA, EU and Japan dominate decisions; the USA has an effective veto at the IMF\n• Leadership conventions keep control with the USA and Europe\n• Loan conditions (structural adjustment programmes in the 1980s–90s) spread free-market policies favoured by the West — 'Washington Consensus'\n• UN Security Council: the five permanent members (USA, Russia, China, UK, France) hold a veto, reflecting the 1945 balance of power\n• WTO trade rules on intellectual property and services benefit developed economies\n• NATO extends US military influence\n• Emerging powers respond with alternatives: New Development Bank (BRICS), Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (China-led)\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Development theorists have offered different explanations of why some countries hold more power than others. Rostow's modernisation theory, Frank's dependency theory and Wallerstein's world systems theory are three examples.\n\nExplain how dependency theory and world systems theory can be used to explain global patterns of power.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nDependency theory:\n• Developed 'metropole' countries have kept developing 'satellite' countries poor by extracting raw materials and cheap labour\n• Colonial and neo-colonial trade patterns (primary exports, manufactured imports) cause declining terms of trade and debt\n• Explains continued dominance of former imperial powers and why some countries remain dependent on aid/loans\nWorld systems theory:\n• World divided into core, semi-periphery and periphery; core exploits periphery through unequal exchange\n• Countries can move between categories — semi-periphery includes emerging powers (China, India, Brazil) that have moved up\n• Explains a dynamic pattern of power and the rise of emerging powers\nComparison: dependency theory is more static; world systems allows change. Rostow's model, by contrast, sees all countries able to develop through stages.\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The South China Sea is bordered by China, Taiwan, Vietnam, the Philippines, Malaysia and Brunei. China claims most of the sea within its 'nine-dash line', and has built artificial islands with airstrips in the Spratly Islands. In 2016 an international tribunal ruled against China's claims in a case brought by the Philippines.\n\nExplain why the South China Sea is a contested region.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Overlapping territorial claims to islands (Spratly, Paracel) and to Exclusive Economic Zones under UNCLOS\n• Resources: fishing grounds and potential offshore oil and gas reserves\n• Strategic location: a major route for global shipping (often estimated at several trillion US$ of trade a year), including oil imports to China, Japan and South Korea\n• China's island-building and militarisation; China rejected the 2016 Permanent Court of Arbitration ruling\n• Superpower rivalry: the USA conducts 'freedom of navigation' operations and has alliances with the Philippines and Japan; China sees the area as its sphere of influence\n• Risk of incidents between coastguards and navies, e.g. confrontations between Chinese and Philippine vessels\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. China's population peaked in 2021 and began to fall in 2022. The proportion of its population aged 65 and over is projected to rise sharply by 2050, partly a legacy of the one-child policy (1979–2015).\n\nExplain why demographic change may limit the future growth of China as a superpower.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Shrinking working-age population reduces the labour supply and may raise wage costs, reducing China's advantage as a low-cost manufacturer\n• Rising dependency ratio increases spending on pensions and healthcare, diverting money from investment and the military\n• Gender imbalance from the one-child policy reduces family formation and future births\n• Slower growth could make it 'grow old before it grows rich', limiting GDP per capita\n• A smaller consumer base may slow domestic demand\n• Counter-points: automation, rising productivity, higher education levels and raising the retirement age may offset the effects\n\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Demonstrates accurate knowledge and understanding of concepts and processes throughout. Gives a thorough, well-developed explanation that links ideas logically and uses relevant, located examples.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Demonstrates mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a partial explanation with some linked ideas and some use of examples.\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, with some inaccuracies. Gives a basic, generalised explanation with few links.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. NATO grew from 12 founding members in 1949 to 32 members by 2024, when Sweden joined following Finland in 2023. Russia, China and other countries cooperate through the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation.\n\nExplain the importance of geopolitical alliances in maintaining the power of superpowers.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Alliances multiply power: NATO's collective defence (Article 5) gives the USA influence across Europe and access to bases\n• They create spheres of influence, e.g. NATO expansion eastwards after 1991 extended Western influence into former Warsaw Pact states\n• Alliances share the costs of defence, though the USA has pressed allies to spend at least 2% of GDP\n• Economic and diplomatic alliances (G7, EU, BRICS) coordinate positions on trade, sanctions and climate\n• Alliances legitimise action — coalitions are seen as more acceptable than unilateral action\n• Rival alliances (SCO, Russia–China partnership, BRICS expansion in 2024) show emerging powers using the same strategy\n• Alliances can be a source of tension and conflict, e.g. Russia's opposition to NATO enlargement and Ukraine\n\nAO1 (8 marks)\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Gives a detailed, coherent explanation of the processes and interrelationships, supported by precise located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates mostly relevant and accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a clear but partial explanation, with some supporting examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is basic and generalised.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Commentators describe the period after the collapse of the USSR in 1991 as 'unipolar', with the USA the only superpower. Many now argue the world is becoming multipolar.\n\nExplain why the stability of the world may differ between unipolar, bipolar and multipolar patterns of power.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Bipolar (Cold War): two blocs balanced by nuclear deterrence — relatively stable at the core but with proxy wars in peripheral regions\n• Unipolar (1990s–2000s): a hegemon can act as 'global policeman' and enforce rules (Gulf War 1991), giving stability; but unchecked power may lead to unilateral intervention (Iraq 2003) and resentment\n• Multipolar: several powers (USA, China, EU, Russia, India) competing — more complex and potentially unstable, as in pre-1914 Europe, with shifting alliances and miscalculation\n• Multipolar systems may encourage cooperation through IGOs and trade interdependence, reducing the chance of war\n• Examples: Russia's invasion of Ukraine 2022 and tension in the South China Sea suggest instability as power shifts\n• Stability depends on alliances, economic interdependence, nuclear deterrence and the strength of global governance\n\nAO1 (8 marks)\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Gives a detailed, coherent explanation of the processes and interrelationships, supported by precise located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates mostly relevant and accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a clear but partial explanation, with some supporting examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is basic and generalised.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Chinese firms and state banks have financed major projects in Africa, such as the Mombasa–Nairobi railway in Kenya and the African Union headquarters in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. China opened its first overseas military base in Djibouti in 2017.\n\nExplain the reasons for growing Chinese influence in Africa and the consequences for African countries.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nReasons:\n• Securing raw materials (oil from Angola, copper from Zambia, cobalt from the DRC) for China's industry\n• Markets for Chinese goods and contracts for construction firms\n• Political allies: votes in the UN; diplomatic isolation of Taiwan\n• Strategic presence: Djibouti base near the Bab-el-Mandeb strait; ports under the Belt and Road Initiative\n• Forum on China–Africa Cooperation (FOCAC) and 'no strings attached' loans contrast with Western conditionality\nConsequences:\n• Positive: new infrastructure (railways, roads, ports, hospitals), faster economic growth, alternatives to Western aid\n• Negative: debt concerns (Zambia defaulted in 2020), use of Chinese labour, environmental damage from mining, competition with local businesses, accusations of neo-colonialism\n• Varies by country and over time\n\nAO1 (8 marks)\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Gives a detailed, coherent explanation of the processes and interrelationships, supported by precise located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates mostly relevant and accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a clear but partial explanation, with some supporting examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is basic and generalised.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. US federal government debt was above US$30 trillion in 2022, and the USA spends more on defence than the next nine countries combined.\n\nExplain why the economic costs of maintaining superpower status may become unsustainable.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Military costs: overseas bases, aircraft carriers and long wars (Iraq, Afghanistan) cost trillions of dollars\n• Debt: government borrowing and interest payments limit future spending; risk to the US dollar's role as reserve currency\n• Opportunity cost: spending on defence reduces investment in infrastructure, education and health at home\n• Economic restructuring: deindustrialisation, loss of manufacturing jobs to emerging economies, and a shift to services\n• Ageing populations increase pensions and healthcare costs (USA, EU, Japan, China)\n• Competition from China (and others) increases the cost of keeping a technological and military lead (AI, space, cyber)\n• Historical comparison: 'imperial overstretch' contributed to the decline of the British Empire and the USSR\n\nAO1 (8 marks)\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Gives a detailed, coherent explanation of the processes and interrelationships, supported by precise located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates mostly relevant and accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a clear but partial explanation, with some supporting examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is basic and generalised.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Under the 2015 Paris Agreement almost every country set targets to reduce greenhouse gas emissions. The USA has twice moved to withdraw from the agreement, while China is the world's largest emitter and also the largest producer of solar panels and wind turbines.\n\nExplain the role of superpowers and emerging powers in global environmental governance.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Superpowers and emerging powers are the largest emitters (China, USA, EU, India), so agreements cannot succeed without them\n• Their leadership can drive agreements (US–China joint announcement before Paris 2015) or weaken them (US withdrawals; earlier refusal to ratify Kyoto)\n• Tension between historical responsibility of developed countries and current emissions of emerging economies — 'common but differentiated responsibilities'\n• Funding: developed countries pledged climate finance to developing countries (US$100 billion a year target)\n• Technology: China's dominance of renewable manufacturing lowers global costs\n• National interests (fossil fuel industries, economic growth, energy security) often override global environmental goals\n• Other environmental issues: resource consumption, deforestation linked to emerging-power demand\n\nAO1 (8 marks)\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Gives a detailed, coherent explanation of the processes and interrelationships, supported by precise located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates mostly relevant and accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a clear but partial explanation, with some supporting examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is basic and generalised.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Korean pop music (K-pop), Japanese anime, Indian 'Bollywood' films and Chinese-owned apps such as TikTok now attract large global audiences alongside long-established US and European media.\n\nExplain how the spread of cultural influence from emerging powers is challenging the dominance of Western culture.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content:\n• Soft power: emerging powers increasingly invest in cultural exports to raise their global attractiveness (China's Confucius Institutes, CGTN; India's Bollywood and yoga diplomacy)\n• Digital platforms spread content quickly across borders; Chinese-owned apps reach Western audiences\n• Diaspora communities spread music, food and festivals (Indian diaspora in the UK and USA)\n• Education and tourism: growing numbers of international students and tourists to Asia\n• Limits: English-language media and US platforms still dominate; censorship and political concerns (bans or restrictions on Chinese apps) limit Chinese soft power\n• Cultural hybridity rather than replacement — glocalisation of Western brands\n• Shows a more multipolar cultural world, linking to wider shifts in economic power\n\nAO1 (8 marks)\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Demonstrates accurate and relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Gives a detailed, coherent explanation of the processes and interrelationships, supported by precise located examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Demonstrates mostly relevant and accurate knowledge and understanding. Gives a clear but partial explanation, with some supporting examples.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Demonstrates isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is basic and generalised.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The European Union has 27 member states, a single market of about 450 million people and a common currency (the euro) used by most of its members. It has no single army and member states keep control of most foreign and defence policy.\n\nAssess the extent to which the European Union can be considered a superpower.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nArguments for:\n• Economic power: one of the three largest economies; largest trading bloc; major source of FDI and aid\n• Regulatory power ('Brussels effect'): EU standards on data (GDPR), chemicals and products adopted worldwide\n• Soft power: culture, education, tourism, human rights and environmental leadership\n• Political influence in IGOs (G7, G20, WTO negotiations as a bloc); France holds a permanent UN Security Council seat (as the UK did as a member)\nArguments against:\n• Not a single state; foreign policy requires unanimity, often divided (e.g. response to migration, relations with China)\n• Limited hard power: depends on NATO and the USA for defence; France is its only nuclear power after Brexit\n• Brexit (2020) reduced population, economy and military capacity\n• Ageing population, slow growth and energy dependence (on Russian gas before 2022)\nConclusion: an economic and regulatory superpower but not a full superpower — perhaps an 'emerging' or partial superpower.\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to deconstruct the question and find full and logical connections, producing a full, coherent and balanced argument that reaches a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge. Applies knowledge to find some logical connections, producing a partially balanced argument with a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, with some inaccuracy. Applies knowledge to find some connections, producing an unbalanced or partial argument with a generic or unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Few connections; an unsupported or generic argument and judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. In 2022 Russia invaded Ukraine using military force, while many countries responded with economic sanctions. At the same time, China has expanded its influence through aid, loans and trade.\n\nAssess the importance of hard power compared with soft power in maintaining global influence.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nHard power:\n• Military strength (USA's global bases, nuclear arsenals) deters rivals and protects allies; can secure territory (Russia in Crimea 2014)\n• Economic coercion: sanctions, tariffs (US–China trade war from 2018)\n• Limits: very costly; interventions may fail (Afghanistan 2001–2021) and damage reputation\nSoft power:\n• Culture, values, education and diplomacy build long-term attraction (US universities, Hollywood, BBC)\n• Cheaper and more sustainable; builds alliances voluntarily\n• Limits: slow to act; cannot stop military aggression on its own\n'Smart power': combination, e.g. the USA uses both; China's BRI combines economic influence with growing military reach\nConclusion: judgement on relative importance, recognising that they depend on context and are interlinked.\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to deconstruct the question and find full and logical connections, producing a full, coherent and balanced argument that reaches a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge. Applies knowledge to find some logical connections, producing a partially balanced argument with a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, with some inaccuracy. Applies knowledge to find some connections, producing an unbalanced or partial argument with a generic or unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Few connections; an unsupported or generic argument and judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Zambia became the first African country to default on its debt during the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020; China was one of its largest creditors. Ethiopia's Addis Ababa–Djibouti railway, opened in 2016–2018, was largely built and financed by Chinese companies and banks.\n\nAssess the extent to which Chinese investment has benefited African countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nBenefits:\n• Infrastructure gap reduced: railways, roads, ports, power and telecoms (Kenya, Ethiopia)\n• Faster economic growth, trade and new export markets; cheaper consumer goods\n• Loans without political conditions are attractive and quicker than Western finance\n• Some jobs and skills; Special Economic Zones (e.g. in Ethiopia)\nCosts:\n• Debt burdens and risks of default (Zambia)\n• Projects may use Chinese labour and materials, limiting local benefit\n• Environmental damage from mining and logging; poor labour standards reported in some mines\n• Competition from imports can harm local manufacturers\n• Concerns about political influence and support for authoritarian governments\nVariation: benefits depend on governance and negotiation by individual countries\nConclusion: a balanced judgement on the extent of benefit.\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to deconstruct the question and find full and logical connections, producing a full, coherent and balanced argument that reaches a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge. Applies knowledge to find some logical connections, producing a partially balanced argument with a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, with some inaccuracy. Applies knowledge to find some connections, producing an unbalanced or partial argument with a generic or unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Few connections; an unsupported or generic argument and judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Russia has reopened military bases along its Arctic coast and operates the world's largest fleet of icebreakers. Russia, Denmark (Greenland) and Canada have submitted overlapping claims to the Lomonosov Ridge to the UN Commission on the Limits of the Continental Shelf.\n\nAssess the extent to which the Arctic is likely to become a region of conflict between powerful countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nLikely conflict:\n• Melting sea ice increases access to oil, gas, minerals and fishing\n• Overlapping seabed claims (Lomonosov Ridge) and disputes over the Northern Sea Route and Northwest Passage\n• Militarisation by Russia and NATO exercises; heightened tension after 2022; Finland and Sweden joining NATO means seven of the eight Arctic states are now NATO members\n• China's interest as a 'near-Arctic state' and its 'Polar Silk Road'\n• Strategic value for missiles and submarines\nLess likely conflict:\n• UNCLOS provides a legal framework for claims; most resources lie within agreed EEZs\n• Arctic Council has promoted cooperation (search and rescue, environment), although its work was disrupted after 2022\n• Harsh environment and high extraction costs; falling demand for fossil fuels under climate commitments\n• Indigenous peoples' rights and environmental opposition\nConclusion: tension likely to rise but open conflict less likely; a judgement weighing these.\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to deconstruct the question and find full and logical connections, producing a full, coherent and balanced argument that reaches a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge. Applies knowledge to find some logical connections, producing a partially balanced argument with a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, with some inaccuracy. Applies knowledge to find some connections, producing an unbalanced or partial argument with a generic or unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Few connections; an unsupported or generic argument and judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. In 2024 the BRICS group expanded beyond Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa to include Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran and the United Arab Emirates. The New Development Bank, set up by BRICS in 2014, is headquartered in Shanghai.\n\nAssess the importance of BRICS as a challenge to the existing global balance of power.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nImportance:\n• Large share of world population and a growing share of world GDP (PPP) — economic weight comparable to the G7\n• Expansion adds oil producers, increasing influence over energy markets\n• New Development Bank and Contingent Reserve Arrangement provide alternatives to World Bank and IMF\n• Calls for reform of IGOs and for trade in local currencies rather than US dollars\n• A political voice for the Global South\nLimitations:\n• Very diverse members with differing political systems and interests; tensions between China and India\n• China dominates the group economically\n• NDB lending is small compared with the World Bank\n• Russia's isolation after 2022; Brazil and South Africa have slower growth\n• No military alliance; little shared soft power\nConclusion: significant symbolic and growing economic challenge, but less coherent than established powers.\n\nAO1 (3 marks) / AO2 (9 marks)\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to deconstruct the question and find full and logical connections, producing a full, coherent and balanced argument that reaches a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Mostly accurate knowledge. Applies knowledge to find some logical connections, producing a partially balanced argument with a conclusion that is partly supported.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant knowledge, with some inaccuracy. Applies knowledge to find some connections, producing an unbalanced or partial argument with a generic or unsupported conclusion.\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Few connections; an unsupported or generic argument and judgement.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Study Figure 5 (data written for this practice question).\nFigure 5: Selected indicators of power, 2022\nIndicator | USA | China | EU | Russia | India\nGDP (US$ trillion) | 25.4 | 17.9 | 16.7 | 2.2 | 3.4\nMilitary spending (US$ billion) | 877 | 292 | 240 | 86 | 81\nNuclear warheads (approx.) | 5,200 | 400 | 290 | 5,900 | 160\nPopulation (million) | 333 | 1,412 | 448 | 144 | 1,417\n\nUsing Figure 5 and your own knowledge, evaluate the view that the world is becoming increasingly multipolar.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nFor the view (multipolar):\n• Rise of China as the second-largest economy (Figure 5: GDP about 70% of the USA's), largest trading nation, BRI and growing navy\n• EU's economic weight is comparable with China; India's large population and growth; Russia's nuclear arsenal and willingness to use military force (Ukraine 2022)\n• BRICS expansion and new IGOs (NDB, AIIB) create alternative centres of power\n• Decline in relative US share of world GDP; regional powers more assertive (Turkey, Saudi Arabia, Iran)\nAgainst the view (unipolar or bipolar):\n• The USA still leads in military spending (Figure 5: about three times China's), global bases, alliances, the dollar as reserve currency, technology and soft power\n• Russia's economy is small (GDP about US$2.2 trillion), so its power is mainly military\n• EU lacks a single military and foreign policy; India's GDP per capita is low\n• Some argue an emerging US–China bipolar rivalry rather than true multipolarity\nEvaluation: depends on the type of power measured — economic power is becoming multipolar, military power remains US-dominated; trends over time; reliability of data\nConclusion: a substantiated judgement on the extent.\nAO2 credit for drawing on the data in Figure 5 with comparisons (e.g. USA military spending is about three times China's).\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The USA and China together produce over 40% of global carbon dioxide emissions. In recent years China has installed more renewable energy capacity each year than the rest of the world combined, while the USA is the world's largest producer of oil and natural gas.\n\nEvaluate the view that superpowers and emerging powers have more negative than positive impacts on the global environment.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nNegative impacts:\n• Largest emitters of CO₂, historically (USA, EU) and currently (China, India); global warming impacts on vulnerable states\n• Resource demand drives deforestation (soya and beef from Brazil), mining and water stress\n• Military activity, weapons testing and conflict damage environments\n• Withdrawal from or weakening of agreements (US withdrawals from Paris, failure to ratify Kyoto)\n• Consumption-based emissions outsourced to emerging economies\nPositive impacts:\n• Investment in renewable technology lowers global costs (China's solar and battery industries; US innovation)\n• Leadership of agreements (Paris 2015, Montreal Protocol 1987), climate finance pledges\n• Scientific research and satellite monitoring (NASA, ESA)\n• Domestic policies (EU Emissions Trading System, US Inflation Reduction Act 2022)\nEvaluation: impacts differ by country and over time; negative impacts are current and certain, positive ones often promised; responsibility debate; scale and permanence of impacts\nConclusion: substantiated judgement.\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The five permanent members of the UN Security Council — the USA, Russia, China, the UK and France — each hold a veto. Since 2022 Russia has vetoed resolutions on Ukraine, while the USA has vetoed resolutions on the Middle East.\n\nEvaluate the extent to which intergovernmental organisations (IGOs) are dominated by superpowers.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nDominated:\n• UN Security Council veto reflects the 1945 balance of power, allowing P5 members to block action (Syria, Ukraine)\n• IMF and World Bank voting shares and leadership conventions favour the USA and Europe\n• NATO led by the USA; WTO rules shaped by developed economies\n• Funding: the USA is the largest contributor to many UN bodies, giving leverage\nNot dominated / changing:\n• UN General Assembly: one country, one vote — developing countries are the majority\n• WTO disputes have been won by smaller countries against the USA and EU\n• Emerging powers gaining voice: G20 since 2008, IMF quota reforms (2010, implemented 2016), BRICS and the NDB, AIIB\n• IGOs constrain superpowers too (WTO rulings, Paris Agreement pressure)\n• Regional IGOs (African Union, ASEAN) act independently\nEvaluation: dominance varies between IGOs, issues and over time; emerging powers both reform and create new IGOs\nConclusion: substantiated judgement.\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. In 2022 the USA accounted for about a quarter of world GDP, the US dollar was used in most international transactions, and US firms led in artificial intelligence and semiconductors. China's economy grew much faster than the USA's between 2000 and 2020 but has since slowed.\n\nEvaluate the view that the USA will remain the dominant superpower for the rest of the twenty-first century.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nUSA will remain dominant:\n• Unmatched military reach, alliances and bases; nuclear arsenal\n• Dollar as reserve currency; deep financial markets; leading universities and innovation (AI, tech TNCs)\n• Soft power: culture, language, attracting migrants and talent\n• Favourable demography compared with China, Japan, EU (immigration, younger population); energy self-sufficiency\n• China faces challenges: ageing and falling population, debt, property crisis, slowing growth, tensions with neighbours\nUSA may not remain dominant:\n• China's economic size, manufacturing dominance, BRI and military modernisation; possible overtaking in GDP\n• US debt, political polarisation, costs of maintaining power, deindustrialisation\n• Multipolar trend: India's growth, BRICS, EU's economic weight\n• Reduced soft power through unilateral actions\nEvaluation: uncertainty of long-term forecasts; different dimensions of power; the possibility of shared or bipolar dominance\nConclusion: substantiated judgement.\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Contested regions include the South China Sea, the Arctic, Ukraine and the Middle East. Around a third of global shipping is often estimated to pass through the South China Sea.\n\nEvaluate the view that the South China Sea is the greatest current threat to global stability.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nFor the view:\n• Overlapping claims of China, Vietnam, Philippines, Malaysia, Brunei and Taiwan; China rejects the 2016 tribunal ruling\n• Militarised artificial islands; frequent confrontations between Chinese and Philippine vessels\n• Directly involves two superpowers: US alliances and freedom of navigation operations versus China's sphere of influence; link to Taiwan\n• Huge economic importance for world trade; any conflict would disrupt global supply chains (semiconductors from Taiwan)\nAgainst the view:\n• Russia's war in Ukraine already involves major conflict, energy and food price shocks, and a risk of NATO–Russia escalation\n• Middle East conflicts threaten oil supplies (Strait of Hormuz) and regional stability\n• Arctic tensions are rising but cooperation still exists\n• Economic interdependence between China and the USA makes war costly and less likely; diplomacy (ASEAN code of conduct talks)\n• Other threats: climate change, cyber conflict, nuclear proliferation\nEvaluation: compare likelihood versus scale of impact; timescales\nConclusion: substantiated judgement.\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. Japan has the world's fourth-largest economy but a limited military, while Russia has a relatively small economy but the world's largest nuclear arsenal. Saudi Arabia controls a large share of global oil reserves.\n\nEvaluate the view that economic power is the most important characteristic of a superpower.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nEconomic power most important:\n• Funds military spending, technology, aid and cultural influence — the base of all other forms of power\n• TNCs, trade and investment create global influence (China's rise based on economic growth)\n• Control of the reserve currency (US dollar) and financial institutions\n• Economic decline (USSR in the 1980s) led to loss of superpower status\nOther characteristics:\n• Military power: Russia's influence relies on nuclear weapons and force; Japan has economic power but limited global influence\n• Political power: UN Security Council membership, alliances, diplomatic reach\n• Cultural/soft power: language, media, values (USA, UK)\n• Natural resources (Saudi Arabia, Russia's gas), demographic size and geostrategic location (Mackinder)\nEvaluation: characteristics are interdependent; importance varies with context — economic power may be necessary but not sufficient\nConclusion: substantiated judgement.\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` },
      { q: `Section A — Superpowers. The USA spent an estimated US$2 trillion on the war in Afghanistan (2001–2021). It also benefits from global use of the US dollar, its alliances and the profits of its TNCs.\n\nEvaluate the view that, for the USA, the costs of maintaining superpower status now outweigh the benefits.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content:\nCosts:\n• Military spending (over US$800 billion a year) and costly interventions (Iraq, Afghanistan) with uncertain outcomes\n• Government debt above US$30 trillion; interest payments rising\n• Opportunity costs: underinvestment in infrastructure, health, education\n• Reputational costs, anti-Americanism, terrorist threats; loss of life\n• Deindustrialisation linked to globalisation that the USA promoted\nBenefits:\n• 'Exorbitant privilege' of the dollar lowers borrowing costs\n• Security for trade routes and allies; access to resources and markets\n• Global TNCs bring profits, jobs and technology leadership\n• Ability to shape IGOs and rules in its interest\n• Soft power attracts talent and investment\nEvaluation: costs are visible and immediate, benefits often indirect; benefits accrue unevenly (TNCs and investors vs workers in deindustrialised regions); comparison with British imperial overstretch; rise of China changes the balance\nConclusion: substantiated judgement.\n\nAO1 (5 marks) / AO2 (15 marks)\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge and understanding of geographical information/ideas to find fully logical and relevant connections and relationships. Produces a full, coherent and balanced argument supported by evidence, reaching a rational, substantiated conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate and relevant knowledge. Finds some logical connections and relationships. Produces a partially balanced and coherent argument with evidence; the conclusion is partially supported.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant knowledge with some inaccuracy. Finds partial connections. Produces an unbalanced or partially coherent argument; the conclusion is generic or weakly supported.\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge. Finds few, if any, connections. Produces an unsupported or generic argument, with a judgement that is not supported by evidence.\n0 marks: No acceptable response.` }
    ]
  },
  'T8': {
    name: 'Health, Human Rights & Intervention',
    questions: [
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 1 is a table showing life expectancy at birth (years) for six selected countries, 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nJapan 84.5; United Kingdom 80.7; Brazil 72.8; India 67.2; Nigeria 53.9; Chad 52.5.\n\nCalculate the range in life expectancy at birth for the countries shown. You must show your working. Give your answer in years.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: highest − lowest = 84.5 − 52.5 (1)\n• Answer: 32.0 years (accept 32 years) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 2 shows the Official Development Assistance (ODA) spent by one donor country (data written for this practice question):\n2019: £15.2 billion (0.70% of Gross National Income)\n2021: £11.4 billion (0.50% of Gross National Income)\n\nCalculate the percentage change in ODA spending between 2019 and 2021. Give your answer to one decimal place. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: (11.4 − 15.2) ÷ 15.2 × 100 (1)\n• Answer: −25.0% (accept a decrease of 25.0%) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 3 is a table showing the Human Development Index (HDI) for five countries (data written for this practice question):\nNorway 0.962; United Kingdom 0.929; Brazil 0.754; India 0.633; Niger 0.394.\n\nCalculate the mean HDI value for these five countries. Give your answer to three decimal places. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: (0.962 + 0.929 + 0.754 + 0.633 + 0.394) ÷ 5 = 3.672 ÷ 5 (1)\n• Answer: 0.734 (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 4 lists the Gender Inequality Index (GII) values for eight countries, in rank order (data written for this practice question). The GII ranges from 0 (women and men fare equally) to 1 (women fare as poorly as possible in all measured dimensions).\n0.039; 0.098; 0.269; 0.390; 0.437; 0.490; 0.611; 0.652\n\nCalculate the median GII value for these countries. You must show your working.`, marks: 2, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `• Correct method: identifies the 4th and 5th values and averages them: (0.390 + 0.437) ÷ 2 (1)\n• Answer: 0.4135 (accept 0.414 or 0.41) (1)\nCorrect answer with no working = 1 mark only.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 5 is a scatter graph of GDP per capita (US$ PPP, x-axis, 0–80,000) against life expectancy at birth (years, y-axis, 50–85) for 60 countries. Life expectancy rises steeply as GDP per capita increases up to about US$15,000, then levels off. A small number of countries, labelled Cuba, Sri Lanka and Costa Rica, lie well above the general trend, with life expectancies of 76–80 years despite GDP per capita below US$22,000.\n\nStudy Figure 5.\nSuggest one reason why some countries have a higher life expectancy than their GDP per capita would suggest.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for extension/development of that reason.\nPossible reasons:\n• Government prioritises universal, state-funded primary healthcare (1), e.g. Cuba trains large numbers of doctors and places family doctors in every community (1), so preventable diseases are treated early and infant mortality is low (1).\n• High levels of public spending on education, especially female education (1), raising health literacy and use of vaccination/clinics (1), lowering child mortality and raising average life expectancy (1).\n• Healthy traditional diets and lower rates of obesity/smoking than in some richer countries (1), reducing non-communicable diseases (1).\n• Strong public health measures such as clean water, sanitation and vaccination campaigns (1) that are low-cost but highly effective (1).\nCredit other valid suggestions.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 6 is a table showing female literacy rates for two states in India, 2011 Census:\nKerala 92.1%; Bihar 51.5%.\nKerala has a long history of state investment in schools and public health; Bihar is one of India's poorest and most rural states.\n\nStudy Figure 6.\nSuggest one reason for the difference in female literacy between these two states.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for extension/development.\nPossible reasons:\n• Higher state government spending on education in Kerala (1), giving near-universal access to schools including in rural areas (1), so girls stay in school for longer (1).\n• Higher rural poverty in Bihar (1) means girls may be kept at home to work or care for siblings (1), or married early, ending their schooling (1).\n• Cultural / social attitudes: Kerala has a long tradition of valuing female education (some matrilineal communities) (1), whereas in parts of Bihar patriarchal norms prioritise boys' education (1).\n• Better infrastructure (roads, transport) in Kerala makes schools more accessible (1).\nCredit other valid suggestions.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 7 is a bar chart comparing, for eight recipient countries, ODA received per person (US$) and the percentage of the population living in extreme poverty (data written for this practice question). Some of the countries with the highest aid per person (for example, a small country hosting a foreign military base and a strategic ally of the donor) have relatively low levels of extreme poverty, while two landlocked countries in the Sahel with over 40% in extreme poverty receive some of the lowest amounts of aid per person.\n\nStudy Figure 7.\nSuggest one reason why the distribution of aid does not always match levels of need.`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 2 further marks for extension/development.\nPossible reasons:\n• Aid is often allocated for geopolitical/strategic reasons (1), e.g. to reward allies or secure military bases or influence in a region (1), rather than according to poverty levels (1).\n• Donors may give tied aid or prefer countries with trade links/former colonial ties (1), benefiting donor businesses (1).\n• Donors may avoid countries with conflict, poor governance or high corruption (1) because they fear aid will be misused or cannot be delivered safely (1).\n• Small-population countries receive more aid per person because some aid is a fixed amount per country (1).\nCredit other valid suggestions.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nQatar and Cuba have broadly similar Human Development Index scores, but Qatar's GDP per capita (PPP) is over five times that of Cuba (data written for this practice question).\n\nExplain one reason why GDP per capita may be a poor measure of human development.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for identifying a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation/development.\nPossible reasons:\n• GDP per capita is a mean and hides inequality in the distribution of income (1); a wealthy elite can raise the average (1) while many people, such as migrant workers, have low incomes and limited rights (1), so it says little about the wellbeing of the majority (1).\n• GDP does not measure health, education or freedom (1); a country can have high income but poor access to healthcare or schooling for some groups (1), while lower-income countries such as Cuba invest in universal services (1), giving better outcomes than GDP suggests (1).\n• GDP ignores the informal economy and unpaid work (1) and counts environmentally damaging activity as growth (1), e.g. oil extraction (1), so it may overstate sustainable wellbeing (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn the 1980s and 1990s many developing countries borrowing from the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and World Bank were required to adopt Structural Adjustment Programmes based on the 'Washington Consensus'.\n\nExplain one way in which the Washington Consensus influenced human development in these countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid way and up to 3 further marks for explanation/development.\nPossible ways:\n• Conditions required cuts in government spending (1), including on health and education (1), leading to the introduction of user fees for clinics and schools (1), which reduced access for the poorest and in some countries slowed improvements in life expectancy and literacy (1).\n• Trade liberalisation and privatisation opened economies to foreign investment (1), stimulating export-led economic growth in some countries (1), which increased incomes and tax revenue (1) that could later be spent on development (1).\n• Removal of food and fuel subsidies (1) raised the cost of living for the poor (1), causing protests and increased poverty in some places (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn Australia, the life expectancy of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people is around eight years lower than that of non-Indigenous Australians.\n\nExplain one reason why Indigenous groups may experience lower levels of health and human rights than other groups within the same country.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation/development.\nPossible reasons:\n• Historic discrimination and dispossession of land (1), e.g. colonial policies and the 'Stolen Generations' in Australia (1), led to loss of culture and intergenerational trauma (1), which is linked to higher rates of poor mental health, alcohol misuse and chronic illness today (1).\n• Many Indigenous communities live in remote areas (1) with poor access to hospitals, GPs and specialist services (1), so diseases are diagnosed later (1), raising mortality (1).\n• Lower incomes and educational attainment (1) are associated with poorer diets, overcrowded housing and higher smoking rates (1), raising rates of diabetes and heart disease (1).\n• Limited political representation (1) means their needs are given low priority in government spending (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nThe Gender Inequality Index (GII), published by the United Nations Development Programme, combines three dimensions: reproductive health (maternal mortality ratio and adolescent birth rate), empowerment (share of parliamentary seats held by women and secondary education attainment) and the labour market (female and male labour force participation).\n\nExplain one limitation of using the Gender Inequality Index to compare gender equality between countries.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid limitation and up to 3 further marks for explanation/development.\nPossible limitations:\n• It is a national average (1) which hides differences within a country, e.g. between urban and rural areas or ethnic groups (1), so women in some regions may experience much greater inequality (1) than the index suggests (1).\n• It omits important dimensions (1) such as gender-based violence, pay gaps, unpaid care work or legal rights such as property inheritance (1), so two countries with similar scores may differ greatly in women's lived experience (1).\n• Data quality varies (1); maternal deaths and labour participation are under-recorded in countries with weak registration systems or large informal economies (1), reducing the reliability of comparisons (1).\n• Parliamentary seats can be boosted by quotas (1) without real changes in women's power (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn 1999 NATO carried out a bombing campaign against Serbian forces in Kosovo to halt the persecution of ethnic Albanians. The campaign did not have the authorisation of the United Nations Security Council.\n\nExplain one reason why military interventions to protect human rights can be difficult to justify in international law.`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Award 1 mark for a valid reason and up to 3 further marks for explanation/development.\nPossible reasons:\n• The UN Charter protects state sovereignty and non-interference (1); force is only lawful in self-defence or with Security Council authorisation (1); permanent members such as Russia or China may veto intervention (1), so interventions like Kosovo 1999 are described as 'illegal but legitimate' (1).\n• The Responsibility to Protect (2005) is a political commitment rather than binding law (1), and requires Security Council approval for military action (1), so states disagree over when it applies (1), e.g. its use in Libya 2011 was criticised as a cover for regime change (1).\n• Human rights norms vary between states (1), so what one state sees as a violation another sees as an internal matter (1).` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 8 is a scatter graph of the mean years of schooling of women aged 25 and over (x-axis, 0–14 years) against under-5 mortality rate (deaths per 1,000 live births, y-axis, 0–150) for 40 countries, 2020 (data written for this practice question). There is a strong negative relationship: countries where women average fewer than 3 years of schooling mostly have under-5 mortality above 80 per 1,000, while those where women average over 10 years have rates below 10 per 1,000. Two oil-exporting countries have moderate schooling but lower child mortality than the trend.\n\nStudy Figure 8.\nSuggest reasons for the relationship shown between women's education and under-5 mortality.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1 (knowledge and understanding):\n• Educated mothers have greater health literacy: they understand hygiene, nutrition, oral rehydration and the importance of vaccination.\n• Education delays marriage and first births and lowers fertility, so more resources and care per child and fewer high-risk pregnancies.\n• Education raises women's incomes and decision-making power within households, so more can be spent on food, clean water and clinic visits.\n• Both variables are linked to national wealth and government investment in services (education and healthcare tend to improve together).\nAO2 (application to Figure 8):\n• Strong negative relationship: under-5 mortality above 80 per 1,000 where women have under 3 years' schooling, below 10 per 1,000 above 10 years.\n• Relationship is correlation, not necessarily direct causation; other factors such as GDP or health spending may explain both.\n• Anomalies (oil exporters) suggest wealth can fund healthcare that lowers child mortality even where female education is lower.\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 3 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Limited application to the resource; explanation of the relationship or pattern is superficial.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant knowledge and understanding, mostly accurate. Some use of the resource to support a partial explanation; connections are made but not always developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Resource used selectively and accurately to support a fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nA 2008 World Health Organization report found that life expectancy for men in Calton, a deprived district in the east end of Glasgow, was 54 years, while in Lenzie, a suburb about 12 km away, it was 82 years.\n\nExplain why there are variations in life expectancy within developed countries.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Income and deprivation: lower incomes are associated with poorer diets, cold or overcrowded housing and higher stress, raising rates of heart disease and respiratory illness.\n• Lifestyle factors: smoking, alcohol and drug use and obesity are more common in deprived areas, e.g. east Glasgow.\n• Employment: higher unemployment or manual/hazardous work in deindustrialised areas, with associated physical and mental health problems.\n• Access to and use of healthcare: some groups make less use of preventive services or face longer waiting times; 'inverse care law'.\n• Education and health literacy vary between areas and groups.\n• Environment: air pollution, lack of green space and crime in some urban districts.\n• Ethnicity and gender: differences between groups (e.g. women generally live longer than men; Indigenous or minority groups may have lower life expectancy).\n• Use of located examples (Calton vs Lenzie; London boroughs such as Kensington and Chelsea vs Barking and Dagenham; differences between US states).\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn 1980 the World Health Organization (WHO) declared smallpox eradicated after a global vaccination and surveillance campaign. Since 1988 the Global Polio Eradication Initiative, led by the WHO and partners, has reduced wild polio cases by over 99%.\n\nExplain the role of Intergovernmental Organisations such as the WHO in improving health in developing countries.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Co-ordinating global vaccination programmes (smallpox, polio, measles) and working with partners such as Gavi and UNICEF to supply cheap vaccines.\n• Surveillance and response to outbreaks, e.g. Ebola in West Africa (2014–16), COVID-19; setting International Health Regulations.\n• Setting standards and goals (Millennium and Sustainable Development Goals, e.g. SDG 3) and publishing data that guide national policy.\n• Funding and technical assistance for health systems and training of health workers; essential medicines list.\n• Campaigns on non-communicable diseases, e.g. the Framework Convention on Tobacco Control.\n• Limitations: reliant on member-state funding and co-operation; slower progress in conflict zones (polio persisting in Afghanistan and Pakistan); criticism of its early response to Ebola.\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nThe Universal Declaration of Human Rights (UDHR) was adopted by the United Nations General Assembly in 1948. It sets out 30 articles, including the right to life, freedom from torture, freedom of expression and the right to education.\n\nExplain why the Universal Declaration of Human Rights has not led to the same level of human rights protection in all countries.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The UDHR is a declaration, not a legally binding treaty; it relies on states incorporating rights into national law (unlike the European Convention on Human Rights, enforced by the European Court of Human Rights).\n• State sovereignty: governments decide how rights are interpreted and enforced; some authoritarian states restrict freedom of speech and assembly.\n• Different cultural, religious and political norms, e.g. differing views on the death penalty, women's rights or LGBTQ+ rights; some states argue rights reflect 'Western' values.\n• Weak rule of law and corruption mean rights that exist on paper are not enforced (link to Corruption Perceptions Index).\n• Conflict and state failure prevent protection of rights.\n• Limited enforcement: the UN Human Rights Council and treaty bodies can monitor and criticise but have few sanctions; Security Council vetoes.\n• Use of located examples (e.g. contrasts between Norway and North Korea; use of the death penalty in the USA, China, Iran).\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 9 is a table showing the Gender Inequality Index (GII) by world region, 2021 (data written for this practice question). (0 = equality; higher values = greater inequality.)\nEurope and Central Asia 0.23; East Asia and Pacific 0.34; Latin America and Caribbean 0.38; South Asia 0.51; Arab States 0.54; Sub-Saharan Africa 0.57.\n\nStudy Figure 9.\nSuggest reasons for the variations in gender inequality shown.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Levels of economic development: higher incomes are linked to better maternal healthcare, lower adolescent birth rates and greater female education.\n• Cultural and religious norms affect women's roles, e.g. expectations of early marriage or restrictions on employment in some societies.\n• Legal frameworks: equal-rights legislation, quotas for women in parliament and anti-discrimination laws in many European states.\n• Access to reproductive healthcare and contraception.\n• Conflict and weak governance increase gender-based violence and disrupt girls' schooling.\nAO2 (use of Figure 9):\n• Lowest inequality in Europe and Central Asia (0.23), more than half the value of Sub-Saharan Africa (0.57).\n• Arab States have relatively high incomes in some countries but high GII (0.54), suggesting cultural/legal factors as well as wealth.\n• Regional averages hide wide variation between and within countries.\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 3 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Limited application to the resource; explanation of the relationship or pattern is superficial.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant knowledge and understanding, mostly accurate. Some use of the resource to support a partial explanation; connections are made but not always developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Resource used selectively and accurately to support a fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nDevelopment aid can be given bilaterally by governments, multilaterally through IGOs such as the World Bank, or by NGOs. Some bilateral aid is 'tied' or has political conditions attached.\n\nExplain how development aid can be used by countries to achieve geopolitical aims.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Aid can build alliances and influence, e.g. long-standing US military and economic aid to Egypt and Israel; Soviet and US aid during the Cold War.\n• Conditional aid can require political or economic reforms (good governance, democracy, human rights, market liberalisation).\n• Tied aid benefits donor businesses and secures trade links and access to resources.\n• Emerging donors such as China use loans and infrastructure investment (Belt and Road Initiative) in Africa and Asia to gain resources, markets and diplomatic support; claims of 'debt-trap' diplomacy.\n• Aid can be withdrawn or suspended to put pressure on governments over human rights abuses (e.g. suspension of aid after coups).\n• Aid can serve security aims: stabilising fragile states to reduce terrorism or migration flows.\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nAfter the US-led invasion of Afghanistan in 2001, girls' enrolment in school rose sharply and life expectancy increased. However, the Taliban returned to power in 2021 and the country remained one of the poorest in the world.\n\nExplain why it is difficult to measure the success of a geopolitical intervention.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Different players have different aims (removing a regime, counter-terrorism, democracy, human rights, development), so 'success' depends on whose criteria are used.\n• Choice of indicators: GDP growth, life expectancy, school enrolment, democracy indices, freedom from conflict deaths, refugee numbers; these may move in different directions.\n• Timescale: short-term gains (e.g. Afghan girls' education after 2001) may be reversed (Taliban 2021); long-term effects may take decades.\n• Counterfactual problem: it is impossible to know what would have happened without intervention (e.g. Syria non-intervention vs Libya intervention).\n• Data reliability in conflict zones is poor; governments may manipulate statistics.\n• Unintended consequences such as civilian casualties, insurgency or displacement must be weighed against gains.\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn Brazil, the 1988 Constitution recognises the rights of Indigenous peoples to their traditional lands. However, many Indigenous territories in the Amazon have faced illegal mining and logging, and land demarcation has been slow.\n\nExplain why human rights can vary between different groups within one country.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Unequal enforcement of rights: rights exist in law but are not enforced in remote areas (e.g. the Amazon) or for marginalised groups.\n• Economic interests: powerful players (agribusiness, mining companies) may conflict with Indigenous land rights; corruption weakens protection.\n• Discrimination based on ethnicity, gender, religion, caste or sexuality, e.g. Dalits in India, Indigenous groups in Brazil or Australia.\n• Gender: women may have fewer rights in practice over property, education, employment, or protection from violence.\n• Political representation: groups with little voice in government receive less protection.\n• Regional variations in governance, e.g. differences between US states (death penalty, abortion law), or federal systems.\n• Use of a located example to support the explanation.\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn 2008 Bhutan adopted Gross National Happiness (GNH) as a goal of government, measuring psychological wellbeing, health, education, culture, good governance, community vitality, ecological diversity and living standards rather than economic output alone.\n\nExplain why there are different views on the relationship between economic growth and human wellbeing.`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The neo-liberal / Washington Consensus view: economic growth (free markets, trade) raises incomes and tax revenue, funding health and education; 'trickle-down'.\n• Evidence of growth improving wellbeing: China lifting hundreds of millions out of extreme poverty since 1980.\n• Counter-views: growth can increase inequality, damage the environment and erode traditional cultures; wellbeing may stall above a certain income (Easterlin paradox).\n• Alternative models: Bhutan's GNH; Cuba's emphasis on state healthcare and education; sustainable development and the 'circular economy'.\n• Some argue that development should be measured by freedoms and capabilities (Amartya Sen), not GDP.\n• Views depend on players' values: governments, TNCs, NGOs and indigenous communities may prioritise different aims.\n\nLevels (AO1 6 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited and generic.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed reasoning, perhaps supported by limited exemplification.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. A fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning, supported by well-chosen located examples.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 10 shows under-5 mortality (deaths per 1,000 live births) in Sierra Leone (data written for this practice question):\n1995: 270; 2000: 235; 2005: 190; 2010: 160; 2015: 125; 2020: 105.\nSierra Leone's civil war (1991–2002) ended after British military intervention in 2000 and a large UN peacekeeping mission. A free healthcare initiative for pregnant women and children under five was introduced in 2010. The Ebola epidemic affected the country in 2014–16.\n\nStudy Figure 10.\nSuggest reasons for the changes in under-5 mortality in Sierra Leone shown.`, marks: 6, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• End of conflict allowed rebuilding of health infrastructure and return of health workers; reduced displacement and malnutrition.\n• Aid from donors (e.g. UK DFID) and IGOs funded vaccination, bed nets and clinics.\n• Free healthcare for under-fives and pregnant women (2010) removed user fees, increasing clinic use.\n• Improving female education and access to clean water/sanitation.\n• Ebola (2014–16) overwhelmed health services and deterred clinic visits, possibly slowing progress.\nAO2 (use of Figure 10):\n• Overall fall from 270 to 105 per 1,000, a reduction of 165 (about 61%) between 1995 and 2020.\n• Steady decline after 2000, coinciding with the end of the war; continued fall after 2010.\n• Even in 2020 the rate remained very high by global standards, showing progress was incomplete.\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 3 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–2 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Limited application to the resource; explanation of the relationship or pattern is superficial.\nLevel 2 (3–4 marks): Elements of relevant knowledge and understanding, mostly accurate. Some use of the resource to support a partial explanation; connections are made but not always developed.\nLevel 3 (5–6 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Resource used selectively and accurately to support a fully developed explanation with logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nThe World Bank, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the World Trade Organization (WTO) were established or developed after the Second World War to promote global economic stability and trade.\n\nExplain the role of these Intergovernmental Organisations in promoting a particular model of development.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• The Bretton Woods institutions (World Bank, IMF) and the WTO promote a free-market, neo-liberal model of development, the 'Washington Consensus'.\n• IMF loans to countries in financial crisis with conditions: fiscal discipline, privatisation, deregulation, reducing subsidies (Structural Adjustment Programmes in the 1980s–90s; later Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers).\n• World Bank funds infrastructure and development projects (dams, roads, education), often linked to economic reforms.\n• WTO promotes trade liberalisation by reducing tariffs and enforcing trade rules through its dispute settlement process.\n• Debt relief, e.g. the Heavily Indebted Poor Countries (HIPC) initiative, linked to spending on poverty reduction.\n• Voting power is weighted by contributions, giving the USA and Europe dominance; critics argue this promotes Western interests.\n• Located examples: SAPs in Ghana or Zambia; IMF conditions in Greece (2010s) or Argentina.\n\nLevels (AO1 8 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited, with few or no examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed lines of reasoning and some use of examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples. A full explanation with clear, developed and logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn the Indian state of Kerala, high levels of literacy, especially among women, have been linked to an infant mortality rate far below the Indian national average and life expectancy well above it.\n\nExplain how education contributes to improvements in health and human development.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Education is itself a component of human development (HDI education dimension) and a human right (UDHR Article 26; SDG 4).\n• Health literacy: understanding of hygiene, nutrition, disease prevention, vaccination and family planning.\n• Female education reduces fertility and child mortality; healthier, smaller families; delayed marriage.\n• Education increases employability and incomes, giving access to better diets, housing and healthcare; generates tax revenue for services.\n• Educated populations demand better governance and are more aware of their rights, supporting political participation.\n• Training of doctors, nurses and community health workers depends on education systems.\n• Located examples: Kerala compared with Bihar or Uttar Pradesh; Cuba; contrasts in sub-Saharan Africa.\n• Limits: benefits depend on quality of education and on employment opportunities and health services being available.\n\nLevels (AO1 8 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited, with few or no examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed lines of reasoning and some use of examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples. A full explanation with clear, developed and logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn 2005 all UN member states endorsed the 'Responsibility to Protect' (R2P), which states that if a government fails to protect its population from genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing and crimes against humanity, the international community has a responsibility to act. R2P was cited in UN Security Council Resolution 1973 on Libya in 2011.\n\nExplain the differing views on using human rights as a justification for military intervention.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\nArguments in support:\n• Moral duty to prevent mass atrocities, informed by failures to act in Rwanda (1994) and Srebrenica (1995).\n• Sovereignty seen as conditional on protecting citizens.\n• Examples seen as successful: British intervention in Sierra Leone (2000); NATO in Kosovo (1999).\nArguments against:\n• Violates state sovereignty and the UN Charter's principle of non-interference.\n• Human rights arguments may disguise strategic or economic interests (oil, regime change), e.g. criticism of Iraq 2003 and Libya 2011.\n• Selective application: intervention in some places (Libya) but not others (Syria, Yemen, Myanmar), shaped by Security Council vetoes and alliances.\n• Risks of civilian casualties, power vacuums and long-term instability (Libya after 2011).\n• Different views among players: Western states, Russia and China, the African Union, NGOs such as Amnesty International.\n\nLevels (AO1 8 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited, with few or no examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed lines of reasoning and some use of examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples. A full explanation with clear, developed and logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nSince 2017, millions of Rohingya Muslims have fled Myanmar to Bangladesh following military violence. The European Union and the USA responded with targeted sanctions on Myanmar military leaders, and a case was brought at the International Court of Justice.\n\nExplain how non-military forms of intervention are used to try to improve human rights in other countries.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Economic sanctions and trade embargoes, either broad (e.g. sanctions on apartheid South Africa) or targeted (asset freezes and travel bans on individuals, e.g. Myanmar generals, Russian officials).\n• Conditional development aid and trade agreements: EU trade preferences linked to human rights; aid suspended after coups.\n• Diplomatic pressure: UN Human Rights Council reviews, General Assembly resolutions, expelling diplomats, sporting/cultural boycotts.\n• International courts: International Criminal Court, International Court of Justice, European Court of Human Rights.\n• NGO campaigning and monitoring (Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch) raising awareness and influencing consumers and governments.\n• Humanitarian aid to refugees and affected populations.\n• Limitations: sanctions can harm ordinary citizens more than elites; can be evaded through trade with other powers (e.g. China, Russia).\n\nLevels (AO1 8 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited, with few or no examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed lines of reasoning and some use of examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples. A full explanation with clear, developed and logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn Canada, First Nations, Métis and Inuit people have higher rates of diabetes, tuberculosis and infant mortality than the non-Indigenous population, and life expectancy for some groups is several years lower.\n\nExplain why levels of health vary between Indigenous and non-Indigenous populations in one country you have studied.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Historic factors: colonisation, loss of land, residential schools (Canada) or the Stolen Generations (Australia), causing intergenerational trauma and loss of culture.\n• Socio-economic disadvantage: lower incomes, higher unemployment, overcrowded and poor-quality housing, food insecurity in remote communities (e.g. Inuit in northern Canada).\n• Lifestyle and diet changes: shift from traditional diets to processed food, higher smoking rates, alcohol and substance misuse.\n• Remoteness: limited access to hospitals, specialists, clean water (boil-water advisories on some reserves).\n• Discrimination and cultural barriers within health services, lower trust and use of services.\n• Lower educational attainment and political representation.\n• Government responses: e.g. Australia's 'Closing the Gap' strategy, with mixed results.\n• Answers must refer to a named country and specific evidence.\n\nLevels (AO1 8 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited, with few or no examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed lines of reasoning and some use of examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples. A full explanation with clear, developed and logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nMany interventions in developing and emerging countries are evaluated by NGOs, IGOs and governments using a range of indicators, including life expectancy, school enrolment, democracy and freedom indices, and numbers of refugees and internally displaced people.\n\nExplain how different indicators can be used to measure the success of geopolitical interventions.`, marks: 8, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `Indicative content\n• Development indicators: GDP per capita, HDI, life expectancy, infant and maternal mortality, literacy and school enrolment (including gender gaps).\n• Human rights indicators: freedom of speech and press, democracy indices, Freedom House scores, numbers of political prisoners, use of torture.\n• Security indicators: conflict deaths, terrorist attacks, refugee and IDP numbers, return of displaced people.\n• Governance: Corruption Perceptions Index, rule of law, free and fair elections.\n• Contrasting players' criteria: donors may focus on economic growth; NGOs on human rights; local people on safety and jobs.\n• Problems: data reliability in conflict zones; short-term vs long-term change; correlation vs causation; indicators may show contradictory trends.\n• Located examples: Afghanistan after 2001 (rise in girls' enrolment but continued conflict); Iraq after 2003; Sierra Leone after 2000.\n\nLevels (AO1 8 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of geographical knowledge and understanding, some of which may be inaccurate or irrelevant. Explanation is limited, with few or no examples.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Elements of relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. A partial explanation with some developed lines of reasoning and some use of examples.\nLevel 3 (7–8 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples. A full explanation with clear, developed and logical chains of reasoning.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 11 is a table comparing GDP per capita (US$ PPP) rank and HDI rank for five countries out of 191, 2021 (data written for this practice question):\nQatar: GDP per capita rank 4, HDI rank 42\nEquatorial Guinea: GDP per capita rank 105, HDI rank 145\nCuba: GDP per capita rank 98, HDI rank 83\nSri Lanka: GDP per capita rank 88, HDI rank 73\nNorway: GDP per capita rank 6, HDI rank 2\n\nUsing Figure 11 and your own knowledge, assess the extent to which the Human Development Index is a better measure of development than GDP per capita.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• GDP per capita measures economic output per person; HDI (UNDP, since 1990) combines life expectancy, education (mean and expected years of schooling) and GNI per capita.\n• Other measures: Gender Inequality Index, inequality-adjusted HDI, Happy Planet Index, Gross National Happiness.\nAO2 (assessment and use of Figure 11):\n• Figure 11 shows GDP can overstate development where wealth is unequally shared or recently gained: Qatar ranks 4th for GDP but 42nd for HDI; Equatorial Guinea's oil wealth does not translate into HDI (rank 145).\n• Cuba and Sri Lanka rank higher on HDI than GDP, showing that investment in health and education can raise human development at modest incomes.\n• HDI is broader and closer to the idea of development as wellbeing and capability.\n• But HDI still includes income, uses national averages, ignores inequality, human rights, gender and environment; weightings are arbitrary; data quality varies.\n• GDP is simpler, frequently updated and useful for comparing economic capacity.\n• Judgement: HDI is a better but still incomplete measure; best used alongside other indicators.\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 9 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated application of knowledge to the question; an assessment with limited and unsupported judgements. Little or no use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Some application of knowledge, producing a partial interpretation of the evidence; a judgement that is unbalanced or only partly supported. Some use of the resource.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation of the evidence, with logical connections; a balanced judgement, supported by evidence, though not always fully developed.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation of the evidence, making logical connections between concepts; a balanced, well-supported judgement that reaches a clear conclusion about the extent or importance asked about.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 12 is a table showing healthcare spending per capita and life expectancy at birth for five developed countries, 2020 (data written for this practice question):\nUnited States: US$11,900; 77.0 years\nSwitzerland: US$7,100; 83.1 years\nUnited Kingdom: US$5,000; 80.4 years\nJapan: US$4,600; 84.6 years\nSingapore: US$2,600; 83.5 years\n\nUsing Figure 12 and your own knowledge, assess the importance of government policy in explaining variations in health and life expectancy between countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Government policy shapes healthcare systems (universal state provision such as the UK NHS vs largely insurance-based systems such as the USA), public health campaigns, regulation (tobacco, sugar taxes), vaccination, water and sanitation, and welfare spending.\n• Other factors: income and inequality, diet and lifestyle, culture, education, environment, genetics, conflict.\nAO2 (assessment and use of Figure 12):\n• USA spends more than twice the UK per capita but has the lowest life expectancy shown, suggesting how money is spent (access, coverage, inequality) matters more than how much.\n• Japan and Singapore achieve the highest life expectancy at lower spending, linked to universal coverage and preventive policies, but also diet and lifestyle.\n• Government policy is important but interacts with culture and lifestyle (obesity in the USA; traditional diets in Japan).\n• In developing countries policy (e.g. Cuba, Kerala) can offset low incomes, but limited budgets, debt and IMF conditions constrain governments.\n• Judgement on the relative importance of government policy compared with other factors.\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 9 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated application of knowledge to the question; an assessment with limited and unsupported judgements. Little or no use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Some application of knowledge, producing a partial interpretation of the evidence; a judgement that is unbalanced or only partly supported. Some use of the resource.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation of the evidence, with logical connections; a balanced judgement, supported by evidence, though not always fully developed.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation of the evidence, making logical connections between concepts; a balanced, well-supported judgement that reaches a clear conclusion about the extent or importance asked about.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 13 is a line graph showing ODA received by Ethiopia (US$ billion, y-axis 0–5) and Ethiopia's HDI (y-axis 0.2–0.5), 2000–2020 (data written for this practice question). ODA rises from about US$0.7 billion in 2000 to about US$4 billion by 2016 and then levels off. HDI rises steadily from 0.29 in 2000 to 0.49 in 2020. Over the same period Ethiopia's government restricted press freedom and conflict broke out in the Tigray region in 2020.\n\nUsing Figure 13 and your own knowledge, assess the success of development aid in improving human development and human rights in one or more countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Types of aid: bilateral, multilateral, NGO, emergency vs long-term; conditional and tied aid.\n• Aid can fund health (vaccination, malaria nets, HIV treatment), education, water and infrastructure; can be linked to good governance conditions.\nAO2 (assessment and use of Figure 13):\n• Figure 13 shows ODA and HDI both rose, suggesting aid contributed to improvements in health and education (e.g. Ethiopia's community health extension programme), but correlation does not prove causation; economic growth and government policy also played a part.\n• Human rights did not improve in line with development: press restrictions and the Tigray conflict show aid may support development while authoritarian governments restrict freedoms.\n• Critics (e.g. Dambisa Moyo) argue aid creates dependency, supports corrupt elites and distorts markets; some aid is spent on donor priorities.\n• Successes in other countries: e.g. global reductions in under-5 mortality; Sierra Leone after 2002.\n• Judgement: aid has been more successful for human development indicators than for human rights, and success depends on governance and how aid is delivered.\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 9 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated application of knowledge to the question; an assessment with limited and unsupported judgements. Little or no use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Some application of knowledge, producing a partial interpretation of the evidence; a judgement that is unbalanced or only partly supported. Some use of the resource.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation of the evidence, with logical connections; a balanced judgement, supported by evidence, though not always fully developed.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation of the evidence, making logical connections between concepts; a balanced, well-supported judgement that reaches a clear conclusion about the extent or importance asked about.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 14 is a world map showing the status of the death penalty in each country, 2023. Most of Europe, Latin America and Australia have abolished the death penalty for all crimes; much of Russia and parts of Central Africa and Asia are shaded as 'abolitionist in practice'; China, Iran, Saudi Arabia, the USA (some states) and several other countries in Asia, the Middle East and Africa are shaded as 'retains and uses the death penalty'.\n\nUsing Figure 14 and your own knowledge, assess the extent to which human rights vary geopolitically between countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Universal frameworks (UDHR 1948, international covenants) vs regional frameworks (European Convention on Human Rights, African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights).\n• Rights vary in law and in practice: death penalty, freedom of speech, women's rights, LGBTQ+ rights, religious freedom.\n• Factors: political system (democracy vs authoritarian), culture and religion, legal systems, corruption, conflict.\nAO2 (assessment and use of Figure 14):\n• Figure 14 shows clear regional patterns: abolition is concentrated in Europe (a condition of Council of Europe membership), Latin America and Australasia; retention in parts of Asia and the Middle East.\n• The USA, a democracy that promotes human rights abroad, retains the death penalty in some states, showing variation does not simply follow political systems and also varies within countries.\n• Map shows only one right; other rights (freedom of expression, women's rights) show different patterns.\n• Variation in practice may be greater than in law, e.g. countries that have signed treaties but fail to enforce them.\n• Judgement on the extent of geopolitical variation and whether it is decreasing over time (growing number of abolitionist states).\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 9 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated application of knowledge to the question; an assessment with limited and unsupported judgements. Little or no use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Some application of knowledge, producing a partial interpretation of the evidence; a judgement that is unbalanced or only partly supported. Some use of the resource.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation of the evidence, with logical connections; a balanced judgement, supported by evidence, though not always fully developed.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation of the evidence, making logical connections between concepts; a balanced, well-supported judgement that reaches a clear conclusion about the extent or importance asked about.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nFigure 15 is a table showing selected gender indicators for three countries (data written for this practice question):\nSweden: women in national parliament 46%; female labour force participation 62%; maternal deaths per 100,000 births 5\nIndia: women in national parliament 15%; female labour force participation 24%; maternal deaths per 100,000 births 103\nRwanda: women in national parliament 61%; female labour force participation 55%; maternal deaths per 100,000 births 259\n\nUsing Figure 15 and your own knowledge, assess the reasons why gender equality varies between countries.`, marks: 12, command: 'Assess', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Dimensions of gender equality: health (maternal mortality, adolescent births), empowerment (education, political representation), economic participation; measured by GII and Global Gender Gap Index.\n• Factors: economic development, cultural and religious norms, legal frameworks, government policy (quotas, parental leave), education, conflict.\nAO2 (assessment and use of Figure 15):\n• Sweden performs well across all indicators, linked to high income, welfare state and long-standing equality legislation and parental leave.\n• Rwanda has the highest share of women in parliament because of constitutional quotas after the 1994 genocide, yet maternal mortality remains high, showing political representation does not guarantee health outcomes when income and healthcare are limited.\n• India's low female labour force participation reflects cultural norms, limited job opportunities and safety concerns, despite economic growth.\n• Assessment of relative importance: economic development strongly influences health indicators, while government policy and culture are more important for political and economic participation.\n• National figures hide variations within countries (e.g. Kerala vs other Indian states).\n\nLevels (AO1 3 marks, AO2 9 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–3 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated application of knowledge to the question; an assessment with limited and unsupported judgements. Little or no use of the resource.\nLevel 2 (4–6 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Some application of knowledge, producing a partial interpretation of the evidence; a judgement that is unbalanced or only partly supported. Some use of the resource.\nLevel 3 (7–9 marks): Relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial but coherent interpretation of the evidence, with logical connections; a balanced judgement, supported by evidence, though not always fully developed.\nLevel 4 (10–12 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout. Applies knowledge to produce a full and coherent interpretation of the evidence, making logical connections between concepts; a balanced, well-supported judgement that reaches a clear conclusion about the extent or importance asked about.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nSince 1999 there have been a number of military interventions justified at least partly on human rights grounds, including Kosovo (1999), Sierra Leone (2000), Afghanistan (2001), Iraq (2003) and Libya (2011).\n\nEvaluate the view that military interventions have done more harm than good to human rights and human development.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Types of military intervention: direct (invasion, air strikes, peacekeeping) and indirect (arms supplies, training, drone strikes).\n• Justifications: R2P, humanitarian intervention, counter-terrorism.\nAO2 – evidence that interventions did more harm:\n• Iraq 2003: large numbers of civilian deaths, sectarian violence, rise of ISIS, displacement of millions; no weapons of mass destruction found.\n• Libya 2011: removal of Gaddafi followed by civil war, collapse of state services, migrant abuses and slave markets.\n• Afghanistan 2001–21: gains in education and health largely reversed after the Taliban return in 2021; civilian casualties.\n• Interventions can be seen as illegitimate, fuelling anti-Western sentiment and terrorism.\nAO2 – evidence that interventions did good:\n• Sierra Leone 2000: British intervention helped end the civil war; elections and gains in life expectancy and child mortality followed.\n• Kosovo 1999: halted ethnic cleansing; Kosovo declared independence in 2008.\n• Afghanistan: millions of girls attended school; life expectancy and maternal health improved during 2001–21.\n• Non-intervention can also cause harm (Rwanda 1994, Syria).\nEvaluation:\n• Outcomes depend on clear goals, UN legitimacy, post-conflict planning, local support and long-term commitment.\n• Timescale and measurement problems; counterfactuals.\n• Conclusion: a substantiated judgement on whether, overall, harm outweighs benefits.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nThe 'Washington Consensus' was a set of free-market policies (fiscal discipline, trade liberalisation, privatisation and deregulation) promoted by the IMF, World Bank and US Treasury from the 1980s onwards.\n\nEvaluate the view that the Washington Consensus has hindered rather than helped human development.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Origins and policies of the Washington Consensus; role of IMF, World Bank, WTO; Structural Adjustment Programmes (SAPs) and later Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers.\nAO2 – arguments that it hindered human development:\n• SAPs cut spending on health and education and introduced user fees, reducing access for the poor in many sub-Saharan African countries during the 1980s–90s ('lost decade').\n• Privatisation of utilities raised prices (e.g. water privatisation protests in Cochabamba, Bolivia, 2000).\n• Trade liberalisation exposed farmers and infant industries to competition; rising inequality.\n• Conditions undermined national sovereignty; IGOs dominated by Western states.\nAO2 – arguments that it helped:\n• Market reforms contributed to growth and poverty reduction in some countries (e.g. Chile; liberalisation in India from 1991), raising incomes and tax revenue for services.\n• Macroeconomic stability (lower inflation) and foreign investment.\n• Debt relief initiatives (HIPC, 2005 Multilateral Debt Relief Initiative) later linked to poverty spending.\n• China's rapid development followed a state-led rather than Washington Consensus model, complicating judgements.\nEvaluation:\n• Outcomes varied by country, governance and timing; correlation vs causation.\n• Evolution of IGO policy towards a 'post-Washington Consensus' recognising health, education and governance.\n• Substantiated conclusion.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nInterventions to improve human rights include development aid, trade agreements and embargoes, diplomatic pressure, legal action through international courts, and military action.\n\nEvaluate the view that development aid is the most effective form of intervention for improving human rights.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Range of intervention types: development aid (bilateral, multilateral, NGO; conditional and unconditional), trade embargoes and sanctions, diplomatic pressure, international courts (ICC, ECHR), military intervention.\nAO2 – aid as most effective:\n• Aid can fund education, healthcare and legal systems, strengthening rights to health, education and justice (e.g. reductions in child mortality, girls' schooling programmes).\n• Conditional aid can encourage good governance and democracy; aid supporting civil society and NGOs.\n• Less destructive and more legitimate than military action.\nAO2 – limitations of aid / other forms more effective:\n• Aid may be captured by corrupt elites or authoritarian governments; human rights may not improve (e.g. Ethiopia, Rwanda: development gains but limited political freedoms).\n• Sanctions and boycotts helped end apartheid in South Africa; EU accession conditions drove human rights reforms in Central and Eastern Europe.\n• International courts have prosecuted war crimes (e.g. Charles Taylor, Sierra Leone; ICTY for former Yugoslavia).\n• Military intervention may be the only way to stop mass atrocities (Kosovo, Sierra Leone).\nEvaluation:\n• Effectiveness depends on the context (conflict vs stable state), the type of right, and whether interventions are combined.\n• Substantiated conclusion about whether aid is the most effective.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nBetween 1990 and 2019, global average life expectancy rose from about 64 years to about 73 years, while global GDP per capita more than doubled. However, life expectancy in some high-income countries, such as the USA, stalled in the 2010s.\n\nEvaluate the view that economic growth is the most important factor in improving health and life expectancy.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Measures of health: life expectancy, infant and maternal mortality, disease prevalence; relationship between income and health (Preston curve).\n• Factors affecting health: income, government policy and healthcare provision, education, water and sanitation, culture and lifestyle, conflict, environment.\nAO2 – economic growth as most important:\n• Rising incomes improve nutrition, housing, water and sanitation; growth generates tax revenue for healthcare (e.g. China and South Korea).\n• Strong correlation between GDP per capita and life expectancy at low incomes.\nAO2 – other factors as more important:\n• Countries such as Cuba, Costa Rica, Sri Lanka and Kerala achieve high life expectancy at modest incomes through state healthcare and education.\n• The USA has very high GDP and healthcare spending but lower life expectancy than many peers due to inequality, insurance-based access, obesity and the opioid crisis.\n• Female education, vaccination programmes (WHO), and public health campaigns often deliver large gains at low cost.\n• Growth can harm health: pollution in rapidly industrialising cities, lifestyle diseases.\n• Within-country inequality (e.g. Glasgow) shows distribution matters more than average growth.\nEvaluation:\n• Growth is a necessary enabler at low incomes, but how wealth is distributed and spent matters more at higher incomes.\n• Substantiated conclusion.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nHuman rights are protected through national constitutions and courts, regional bodies such as the European Court of Human Rights, and global organisations such as the UN Human Rights Council and the International Criminal Court.\n\nEvaluate the view that human rights are better protected by national governments than by global organisations.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• National protection: constitutions, bills of rights, independent courts, elections, free press; e.g. UK Human Rights Act 1998.\n• Global and regional protection: UDHR, UN Human Rights Council, Universal Periodic Review, ICC (2002), ECHR (Council of Europe), NGOs.\nAO2 – national governments better:\n• Governments have direct legal authority and enforcement power within their territory; democratic states with strong rule of law (e.g. Norway, Canada) protect rights effectively.\n• Global organisations rely on state consent, have limited enforcement, and are affected by Security Council vetoes; some major states (USA, China, Russia) are not ICC members.\nAO2 – global organisations better or necessary:\n• National governments may be the abusers of rights (e.g. Myanmar and the Rohingya; North Korea), so external bodies are needed.\n• The ECHR has binding judgments that have changed national laws; international tribunals prosecuted genocide and war crimes (Rwanda, former Yugoslavia).\n• Global organisations set norms and monitor, giving NGOs and citizens leverage over governments.\n• Variations within countries show national protection can be uneven for minorities and Indigenous groups.\nEvaluation:\n• The two operate together: global norms are most effective when incorporated into national law.\n• Effectiveness depends on type of political system, corruption and geopolitical power.\n• Substantiated conclusion.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nWestern governments have often judged the success of interventions by the establishment of elections and democratic institutions, for example in Afghanistan after 2001 and Iraq after 2003. Others argue that improvements in life expectancy, education and freedom from violence are better measures.\n\nEvaluate the view that the success of geopolitical interventions should be judged by improvements in human development rather than by the spread of democracy.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Indicators of human development (HDI, life expectancy, literacy, under-5 mortality) and of democracy (free elections, democracy indices, press freedom).\n• Types of intervention: aid, trade, military, diplomatic.\nAO2 – human development as the better measure:\n• Directly reflects improvements in people's lives and wellbeing; aligns with the SDGs and capability approaches.\n• Elections can be held without real democratic change (corruption, fraud, ethnic division in Iraq and Afghanistan).\n• Some states have achieved major development gains without Western-style democracy (e.g. China, Rwanda), suggesting democracy is not a precondition.\nAO2 – democracy as an important measure:\n• Democratic institutions protect civil and political rights (free speech, rule of law) that are themselves human rights and help sustain development.\n• Development gains under authoritarian rule may be reversed or come at the cost of repression.\n• Democracy may reduce the risk of famine and conflict (Amartya Sen's argument).\nEvaluation:\n• Different players (donor governments, NGOs, local populations) value different outcomes.\n• Both are needed: a combination of indicators over the long term gives a more complete judgement; issues of data reliability and timescale.\n• Substantiated conclusion.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` },
      { q: `Section C — Global Development and Connections: Health, Human Rights and Intervention.\nIn the USA in 2021, life expectancy for Native Americans was about 65 years compared with about 76 years for the population as a whole. In many countries women, ethnic minorities, Indigenous peoples and LGBTQ+ people experience different levels of rights from the majority population.\n\nEvaluate the view that variations in human rights within countries are now more significant than variations between countries.`, marks: 20, command: 'Evaluate', markScheme: `Indicative content\nAO1:\n• Measures of human rights and their variations: UDHR, democracy and freedom indices, GII, Corruption Perceptions Index.\n• Geopolitical variations between countries (democratic vs authoritarian states; regional norms such as the death penalty) and variations within countries (gender, ethnicity, Indigenous status, region, sexuality, caste).\nAO2 – within-country variations more significant:\n• Indigenous groups in Australia, Canada, Brazil and the USA experience lower life expectancy and weaker land and political rights despite national protections.\n• Gender inequalities persist in high-income countries (pay gaps, violence against women).\n• Regional variation in federal states: US states differ on the death penalty and abortion law; India's states differ in female literacy (Kerala vs Bihar).\n• Globalisation and growth have narrowed some between-country gaps in health and education, while inequality within countries has often widened.\nAO2 – between-country variations more significant:\n• Huge contrasts remain between, for example, Norway and Afghanistan in women's rights, or between democracies and authoritarian states such as North Korea in civil and political rights.\n• National political systems, laws and conflict shape the rights of everyone in a country.\n• Between-country gaps in life expectancy (about 30 years) are larger than most within-country gaps.\nEvaluation:\n• The significance depends on which rights are considered and how they are measured; national averages can hide inequalities.\n• Substantiated conclusion.\n\nLevels (AO1 5 marks, AO2 15 marks):\nLevel 1 (1–5 marks): Isolated elements of knowledge and understanding, some inaccurate or irrelevant. Isolated, unsupported judgements; limited or no conclusion. Few or no located examples.\nLevel 2 (6–10 marks): Some relevant, mostly accurate knowledge and understanding. Applies knowledge to produce a partial interpretation of the evidence; an unbalanced argument with a generic or partly supported conclusion.\nLevel 3 (11–15 marks): Mostly accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding, supported by located examples. Applies knowledge to produce a coherent but partial argument that considers more than one view; a conclusion that is supported by some evidence, although not all aspects are equally developed.\nLevel 4 (16–20 marks): Accurate, relevant knowledge and understanding throughout, supported by detailed located examples / case studies. Applies knowledge to produce a full, coherent and balanced argument that weighs contrasting views; a well-evidenced and substantiated conclusion that directly answers the view in the question.` }
    ]
  }
};
